window.QUIZ_DATA = {"version":"f15756e34109","papers":[{"id":"quine1951-two-dogmas-empiricism","title":"Two Dogmas of Empiricism","authors":["W. V. O. Quine"],"year":"1951","area":"language","topic":"analyticity & holism","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"our statements about the external world face the tribunal of sense experience not individually but only as a corporate body.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Any statement can be held true come what may, if we make drastic enough adjustments elsewhere in the system. … no statement is immune to revision.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Analyticity cannot be non-circularly explained: appeals to meanings, definition, synonymy, interchangeability salva veritate, and semantical rules for artificial languages each presuppose analyticity or an equally obscure notion, so the analytic–synthetic boundary has never been drawn.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Analyticity and statement-by-statement verification rest on one mistake: splitting each statement's truth into linguistic and factual components. Science as a whole faces experience, so no statement, even of logic, is immune from pragmatic revision.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"logical truth","aliases":["logically true","logical"],"definition":"If we suppose a prior inventory of logical particles, comprising 'no,' 'un-' 'if,' 'then,' 'and,' etc., then in general a logical truth is a statement which is true and remains true under all reinterpretations of its components other than the logical particles.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"cognitive synonymy","aliases":["cognitively synonymous","synonymous","synonymy"],"definition":"to say that 'bachelor' and 'unmarried man' are cognitively synonymous is to say no more nor less than that the statement: (3) All and only bachelors are unmarried men is analytic.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"reductionism","aliases":["reductionist"],"definition":"the belief that each meaningful statement is equivalent to some logical construct upon terms which refer to immediate experience.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"semantical rule","aliases":["semantical rules","Semantical Rules","Semantical rules"],"definition":"Semantical rules are distinguishable, apparently, only by the fact of appearing on a page under the heading 'Semantical Rules'; and this heading is itself then meaningless.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Consider 'creature with a heart' and 'creature with a kidney': as it happens, perhaps every creature with a heart is a creature with a kidney, and vice versa.","phenomenon":"coextensive but non-synonymous terms","near":["cognitively synonymous predicates","lexical ambiguity","empty general terms"],"exact":false},{"id":"e2","text":"Truths which become false under substitution of 'unmarried man' for 'bachelor' are easily constructed … 'Bachelor' has less than ten letters.","phenomenon":"substitution failure in quotation contexts","aliases":["substitution"],"near":["substitution failure in modal contexts","substitution failure in belief contexts","lexical ambiguity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Revision even of the logical law of the excluded middle has been proposed as a means of simplifying quantum mechanics; and what difference is there in principle between such a shift and the shift whereby Kepler superseded Ptolemy, or Einstein Newton, or Darwin Aristotle?","phenomenon":"revisability of logic","aliases":["Revision","logical"],"near":["logical pluralism","conventionalism about logic","dialetheism"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Physical objects are conceptually imported into the situation as convenient intermediaries -- not by definition in terms of experience, but simply as irreducible posits comparable, epistemologically, to the gods of Homer. Let me interject that for my part I do, qua lay physicist, believe in physical objects and not in Homer's gods; and I consider it a scientific error to believe otherwise. But in point of epistemological footing the physical objects and the gods differ only in degree and not in kind.","phenomenon":"cultural posits","aliases":["posits"],"near":["fictionalism about physical objects","phenomenalist reduction of physical objects","Moorean common-sense certainty"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2181906"},{"id":"grice1957-meaning","title":"Meaning","authors":["H. P. Grice"],"year":"1957","area":"language","topic":"speaker meaning & intention","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"\"A meantNN something by x\" is (roughly) equivalent to \"A intended the utterance of x to produce some effect in an audience by means of the recognition of this intention\"","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"for x to have meaningNN, … the recognition of the intention behind x is for the audience a reason and not merely a cause.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"U meant something by uttering x ↔ U intended (1) x to produce a response r in an audience A, (2) A to recognize intention (1), and (3) that recognition to be (part of) A's reason for r.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Separates factive natural meaning (spots meaning measles) from non-natural meaning, and rejects causal theories that analyze the latter as a sign's conditioned tendency to produce attitudes, analyzing it instead through speakers' intentions on particular occasions.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"natural meaning","aliases":[],"definition":"x meant that p and x means that p entail p. … I cannot argue from \"Those spots meant measles\" to any conclusion to the effect that somebody or other meant by those spots so-and-so.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"nonnatural meaning","aliases":["meantNN"],"definition":"\"A meantNN something by x\" is roughly equivalent to \"A uttered x with the intention of inducing a belief by means of the recognition of this intention.\"","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"primary intention","aliases":[],"definition":"only what I may call the primary intention of an utterer is relevant to the meaningNN of an utterance. … if (say) I intend to get a man to do something by giving him some information, it cannot be regarded as relevant to the meaningNN of my utterance to describe what I intend him to do.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"timeless meaning","aliases":["timeless"],"definition":"\"x meansNN (timeless) that so-and-so\" might as a first shot be equated with some statement or disjunction of statements about what \"people\" (vague) intend (with qualifications about \"recognition\") to effect by x.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"\"Those spots mean (meant) measles.\" \"Those spots didn't mean anything to me, but to the doctor they meant measles.\" \"The recent budget means that we shall have a hard year.\" … I cannot say, \"Those spots meant measles, but he hadn't got measles,\"","phenomenon":"natural meaning","near":["non-natural meaning","speaker meaning","conventional meaning"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"\"Those three rings on the bell (of the bus) mean that the 'bus is full.'\" … I can use the first of these and go on to say, \"But it isn't in fact full—the conductor has made a mistake\"","phenomenon":"non-natural meaning","near":["natural meaning","conversational implicature","conventional implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Herod presents Salome with the head of St. John the Baptist on a charger. … Feeling faint, a child lets its mother see how pale it is (hoping that she may draw her own conclusions and help). … I leave the china my daughter has broken lying around for my wife to see. … Herod intended to make Salome believe that St. John the Baptist was dead and no doubt also intended Salome to recognize that he intended her to believe that St. John the Baptist was dead.","phenomenon":"deliberately and openly letting someone know","near":["non-natural meaning","covert intention to induce belief","conversational implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"I might leave B's handkerchief near the scene of a murder in order to induce the detective to believe that B was the murderer","phenomenon":"covert intention to induce belief","aliases":["induce"],"near":["deliberately and openly letting someone know","non-natural meaning","conversational implicature"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2182440"},{"id":"grice1975-logic-conversation","title":"Logic and Conversation","authors":["H. P. Grice"],"year":"1975","area":"language","topic":"implicature","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Our talk exchanges do not normally consist of a succession of disconnected remarks, and would not be rational if they did. They are characteristically … cooperative efforts","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"initially at least, conversational implicata are not part of the meaning of the expressions to the employment of which they attach.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"The supposed divergences in meaning between the formal connectives and their natural-language counterparts ('and', 'or', 'if') are illusory: the extra content is implicated, not part of the words' meaning, arising from a presumption that speakers observe the Cooperative Principle.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Speakers convey more than they say: a hearer calculates an implicature as whatever the speaker must be supposed to think to reconcile her utterance, even a blatant breach of a maxim, with the presumption that she observes the Cooperative Principle.","formal":false}],"pick":3,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"conversational implicature","aliases":[],"definition":"to calculate a conversational implicature is to calculate what has to be supposed in order to preserve the supposition that the Cooperative Principle is being observed","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"Cooperative Principle","aliases":["principle"],"definition":"a rough general principle which participants will be expected (ceteris paribus) to observe, namely: Make your conversational contribution such as is required, at the stage at which it occurs, by the accepted purpose or direction of the talk exchange in which you are engaged.","exact":true},{"id":"t7","term":"maxim of Quantity","aliases":[],"definition":"1. Make your contribution as informative as is required (for the current purposes of the exchange). 2. Do not make your contribution more informative than is required.","exact":true},{"id":"t8","term":"maxim of Quality","aliases":[],"definition":"Try to make your contribution one that is true … 1. Do not say what you believe to be false. 2. Do not say that for which you lack adequate evidence.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"what is said","aliases":["say","said"],"definition":"In the sense in which I am using the word say, I intend what someone has said to be closely related to the conventional meaning of the words (the sentence) he has uttered.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"flouting","aliases":["flout"],"definition":"He may flout a maxim; that is, he may blatantly fail to fulfill it. … the hearer is faced with a minor problem: How can his saying what he did say be reconciled with the supposition that he is observing the overall CP?","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"generalized conversational implicature","aliases":["implicature"],"definition":"Sometimes one can say that the use of a certain form of words in an utterance would normally (in the absence of special circumstances) carry such-and-such an implicature or type of implicature.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"conventional implicature","aliases":["conventional","implicated"],"definition":"In some cases the conventional meaning of the words used will determine what is implicated, besides helping to determine what is said.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"A is writing a testimonial about a pupil who is a candidate for a philosophy job, and his letter reads as follows: 'Dear Sir, Mr. X's command of English is excellent, and his attendance at tutorials has been regular. Yours, etc.'","phenomenon":"flouting the maxim of Quantity","near":["clash between maxims","opting out of the Cooperative Principle","conventional implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"X, with whom A has been on close terms until now, has betrayed a secret of A's to a business rival. A and his audience both know this. A says 'X is a fine friend'.","phenomenon":"verbal irony","near":["metaphor (categorial falsity)","meiosis (understatement)","hyperbole (overstatement)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"A is standing by an obviously immobilized car and is approached by B; the following exchange takes place: … A: I am out of petrol. B: There is a garage round the corner.","phenomenon":"implicature from observing the maxim of Relation","near":["flouting the maxim of Relation","scalar implicature","conventional implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"A is planning with B an itinerary for a holiday in France. Both know that A wants to see his friend C, if to do so would not involve too great a prolongation of his journey: … A: Where does C live? B: Somewhere in the South of France.","phenomenon":"clash between maxims (Quantity versus Quality)","near":["flouting the maxim of Quantity","opting out of the Cooperative Principle","conventional implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"At a genteel tea party, A says Mrs. X is an old bag. There is a moment of appalled silence, and then B says The weather has been quite delightful this summer, hasn't it?","phenomenon":"flouting the maxim of Relation","near":["implicature from observing the maxim of Relation","flouting the maxim of Manner","clash between maxims"],"exact":true},{"id":"e6","text":"If I say (smugly), He is an Englishman; he is, therefore, brave, I have certainly committed myself, by virtue of the meaning of my words, to its being the case that his being brave is a consequence of (follows from) his being an Englishman. But … I do not want to say that I have SAID (in the favored sense) that it follows from his being an Englishman that he is brave.","phenomenon":"conventional implicature","near":["generalized conversational implicature","particularized conversational implicature","entailment (part of what is said)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e7","text":"Anyone who uses a sentence of the form X is meeting a woman this evening would … implicate that the person to be met was someone other than X's wife, mother, sister, or perhaps even close platonic friend.","phenomenon":"generalized conversational implicature","aliases":["implicate"],"near":["particularized conversational implicature","conventional implicature","presupposition"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1163/9789004368811_003"},{"id":"davidson1967-truth-meaning","title":"Truth and Meaning","authors":["Donald Davidson"],"year":"1967","area":"language","topic":"theory of meaning & truth conditions","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"a theory of meaning for a language L shows \"how the meanings of sentences depend upon the meanings of words\" if it contains a (recursive) definition of truth-in-L.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"My objection to meanings in the theory of meaning is not that they are abstract or that their identity conditions are obscure, but that they have no demonstrated use.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A theory of meaning for L must, using no further semantic notions, entail every instance of 's is T ↔ p' (s a structural description of an L-sentence, p that sentence or its translation); T then coincides with truth-in-L.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Against Tarski's pessimism, a truth theory can serve as an empirical theory of a natural language: tested by pairing sentences speakers hold true (charitably), with demonstratives handled by relativizing truth to speaker and time.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"theory of meaning","aliases":["theories of meaning"],"definition":"What we require of a theory of meaning for a language L is that without appeal to any (further) semantical notions it place enough restrictions on the predicate 'is T' to entail all sentences got from schema T when 's' is replaced by a structural description of a sentence of L and 'p' by that sentence.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"structural description","aliases":["structural descriptions"],"definition":"A 'structural description' of an expression describes the expression as a concatenation of elements drawn from a fixed finite list (for example of words or letters).","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"meaning holism","aliases":["holism","holistic"],"definition":"If sentences depend for their meaning on their structure, and we understand the meaning of each item in the structure only as an abstraction from the totality of sentences in which it features, then we can give the meaning of any sentence (or word) only by giving the meaning of every sentence (and word) in the language.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"charity","aliases":["Charity","principle of charity"],"definition":"just as we must maximize agreement, or risk not making sense of what the alien is talking about, so we must maximize the self-consistency we attribute to him, on pain of not understanding him.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"suppose that 'R' and 'S' abbreviate any two sentences alike in truth value. Then the following four sentences have the same reference: (1) R (2) x̂(x=x.R)=x̂(x=x) (3) x̂(x=x.S) = x̂(x=x) (4) S For (1) and (2) are logically equivalent, as are (3) and (4), while (3) differs from (2) only in containing the singular term 'x̂(x=x.S)' where (2) contains 'x̂(x=x.R)' and these refer to the same thing if S and R are alike in truth value. Hence any two sentences have the same reference if they have the same truth value.","phenomenon":"slingshot argument","near":["Frege's puzzle about identity statements","Mates's puzzle about synonym substitution","the paradox of analysis"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Still, this fact ought not to con us into thinking a theory any more correct that entails '\"Snow is white\" is true if and only if snow is white' than one that entails instead: (S) 'Snow is white' is true if and only if grass is green, provided, of course, we are as sure of the truth of (S) as we are of that of its more celebrated predecessor.","phenomenon":"Foster's extensionality problem for T-theories","near":["slingshot argument","Frege's puzzle about identity statements","the liar paradox"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"'good' as it features in 'Bardot is a good actress' is another matter. … The problem is to frame a truth definition such that '\"Bardot is a good actress\" is true if and only if Bardot is a good actress' - and all other sentences like it - are consequences. Obviously 'good actress' does not mean 'good and an actress'.","phenomenon":"non-intersective (attributive) adjective modification","near":["intersective adjective modification","privative adjectives","emotive meaning of evaluative terms"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"the same sentence may at one time or in one mouth be true and at another time or in another mouth be false. … 'I am tired' is true as (potentially) spoken by p at t if and only if p is tired at t. 'That book was stolen' is true as (potentially) spoken by p at t if and only if the book demonstrated by p at t is stolen prior to t.","phenomenon":"indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)","aliases":["demonstrated"],"near":["lexical ambiguity","anaphoric dependence","the essential indexical"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00485035"},{"id":"putnam1975-meaning-meaning","title":"The Meaning of ‘Meaning’","authors":["Hilary Putnam"],"year":"1975","area":"language","topic":"externalism & mental content","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Traditional semantic theory leaves out only two contributions to the determination of extension - the contribution of society and the contribution of the real world!","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Cut the pie any way you like, \"meanings\" just ain't in the head!","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Speakers who are molecule-for-molecule duplicates, in the same narrow psychological state, can use 'water' with different extensions; so no notion of meaning can both be fixed by what is in the head and determine extension.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Extension is fixed socially, through a division of linguistic labor with experts, and indexically, by the hidden nature of local paradigms; meaning is reconstructed as a vector of syntactic markers, semantic markers, stereotype and extension.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"division of linguistic labor","aliases":["division of labor","linguistic labor"],"definition":"everyone to whom gold is important for any reason has to acquire the word \"gold\"; but he does not have to acquire the method of recognizing if something is or is not gold. He can rely on a special subclass of speakers.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"stereotype","aliases":["stereotypical","stereotypes"],"definition":"a standardized description of features of the kind that are typical, or \"normal,\" or at any rate stereotypical. The central features of the stereotype generally are criteria - features which in normal situations constitute ways of recognizing if a thing belongs to the kind","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"methodological solipsism","aliases":[],"definition":"the assumption that no psychological state, properly so called, presupposes the existence of any individual other than the subject to whom that state is ascribed.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"sameL","aliases":["same liquid as"],"definition":"x bears the relation sameL to y just in case (1) x and y are both liquids, and (2) x and y agree in important physical properties.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"indexical component","aliases":["indexical"],"definition":"words like \"water\" have an unnoticed indexical component: \"water\" is stuff that bears a certain similarity relation to the water around here.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"meaning vector","aliases":["vector","normal form description"],"definition":"the normal form description of the meaning of a word should be a finite sequence, or \"vector,\" whose components should certainly include … (1) the syntactic markers that apply to the word, e.g., \"noun\"; (2) the semantic markers that apply to the word, e.g., \"animal,\" \"period of time\"; (3) a description of the additional features of the stereotype, if any; (4) a description of the extension.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"One of the peculiarities of Twin Earth is that the liquid called \"water\" is not H2O but a different liquid … XYZ. I shall suppose that XYZ is indistinguishable from water at normal temperatures and pressures. … Let Oscar1 be [a] typical Earthian English speaker, and let Oscar2 be his counterpart on Twin Earth. … you may even suppose that Oscar1 and Oscar2 were exact duplicates in appearance, feelings, thoughts, interior monologue, etc. … Oscar1 and Oscar2 understood the term \"water\" differently in 1750 although they were in the same psychological state","phenomenon":"semantic externalism about natural-kind terms","near":["division of linguistic labor","social externalism about mental content","indeterminacy of translation"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Consider our community as a \"factory\": in this \"factory\" some people have the \"job\" of wearing gold wedding rings, other people have the \"job\" of selling gold wedding rings, still other people have the job of telling whether or not something is really gold.","phenomenon":"division of linguistic labor","near":["vagueness (borderline cases)","stereotype as minimal linguistic competence","rigid designation of natural kinds"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Suppose, now, that I discover the microstructure of water - that water is H2O. At this point I will be able to say that the stuff on Twin Earth that I earlier mistook for water isn't really water. In the same way, if you describe not another planet in the actual universe, but another possible universe in which there is stuff with the chemical formula XYZ which passes the \"operational test\" for water, we shall have to say that that stuff isn't water but merely XYZ.","phenomenon":"necessary a posteriori truth","near":["contingent a priori truth","analytic truth","world-relative (non-rigid) meaning"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"There were or are pieces of metal which could not have been determined not to be gold in Archimedes' day, but which we can distinguish from gold quite easily with modern techniques. Let X be such a piece of metal. Clearly X does not lie in the extension of \"gold\" in standard English; my view is that it did not lie in the extension of [χρυσός] in Attic Greek, either, although an ancient Greek would have mistaken X for gold","phenomenon":"stability of reference across theory change","near":["incommensurability of scientific theories","operational definition of meaning","vagueness (borderline cases)"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"donnellan1966-reference-definite-descriptions","title":"Reference and Definite Descriptions","authors":["Keith Donnellan"],"year":"1966","area":"language","topic":"descriptions & definiteness","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"In the attributive use, the attribute of being the so-and-so is all important, while it is not in the referential use.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Using a definite description referentially, a speaker may say something true even though the description correctly applies to nothing.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Definite descriptions have two uses: attributive, saying something about whatever uniquely fits the description, and referential, a mere device for getting the audience to pick out a particular thing, which can succeed even if nothing fits.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Whether a description is used referentially or attributively depends on the speaker's intentions on an occasion, not on the sentence; referring is not denoting, and neither Russell's existence entailment nor Strawson's truth-value gap holds for referential uses.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"attributive use","aliases":["attributively","attributive"],"definition":"A speaker who uses a definite description attributively in an assertion states something about whoever or whatever is the so-and-so. … the definite description might be said to occur essentially, for the speaker wishes to assert something about whatever or whoever fits that description","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"referential use","aliases":["referentially","referential"],"definition":"A speaker who uses a definite description referentially in an assertion … uses the description to enable his audience to pick out whom or what he is talking about and states something about that person or thing.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"pragmatic ambiguity","aliases":["pragmatically ambiguous","pragmatically","ambiguous"],"definition":"Perhaps we could say that the sentence is pragmatically ambiguous: the distinction between roles that the description plays is a function of the speaker's intentions.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"denoting","aliases":["denotes","denotation","denote"],"definition":"a definite description denotes an entity if that entity fits the description uniquely … Thus whether or not a definite description is used referentially or attributively, it may have a denotation. Hence, denoting and referring, as I have explicated the latter notion, are distinct","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose first that we come upon poor Smith foully murdered. From the brutal manner of the killing and the fact that Smith was the most lovable person in the world, we might exclaim, \"Smith's murderer is insane.\" I will assume, to make it a simpler case, that in a quite ordinary sense we do not know who murdered Smith","phenomenon":"attributive use of a definite description","near":["referential use of a definite description","presupposition failure","generic use of a definite description"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Suppose one is at a party and, seeing an interesting-looking person holding a martini glass, one asks, \"Who is the man drinking a martini?\" If it should turn out that there is only water in the glass, one has nevertheless asked a question about a particular person, a question that it is possible for someone to answer.","phenomenon":"referential use of a definite description","near":["attributive use of a definite description","presupposition accommodation","narrow-scope (de dicto) reading of a description"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"If someone said, for example, in 1960 before he had any idea that Mr. Goldwater would be the Republican nominee in 1964, \"The Republican candidate for president in 1964 will be a conservative,\" … the definite description here would denote Mr. Goldwater. But would we wish to say that the speaker had referred to, mentioned, or talked about Mr. Goldwater?","phenomenon":"denotation without reference","aliases":["denote","referred"],"near":["reference without denotation","referential use of a definite description","empty definite description"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Suppose that I think I see at some distance a man walking and ask, \"Is the man carrying a walking stick the professor of history?\" … There is finally the case in which there is nothing at all where I thought there was a man with a walking stick … it was, perhaps, a trick of light that made me think there was a man there.","phenomenon":"reference failure","aliases":["refer"],"near":["reference via misdescription","attributive use of a definite description","empty proper name"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2183143"},{"id":"kaplan1989-demonstratives","title":"Demonstratives","authors":["David Kaplan"],"year":"1989","area":"language","topic":"indexicals & demonstratives","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Indexicals, pure and demonstrative alike, are directly referential.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Character: Contexts → Contents; Content: Circumstances → Extensions; or, in more familiar language, Meaning + Context = Intension; Intension + Possible World → Extension","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Indexicals have two kinds of meaning: a character, fixed by convention and known to competent speakers, maps each context of use to a content, and that content is the referent itself rather than any descriptive condition.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"φ is valid iff, in every context c, its content at c is true at c's own world and time; hence 'I am here now' is valid while □(I am here now) is not.","formal":true}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"character","aliases":["characters"],"definition":"That aspect of the meaning of an expression which determines what its Content will be in each context, we call the Character of the expression. … the Character of each well-formed expression is determined by rules of the language … which are presumably known to all competent speakers.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"content","aliases":["contents"],"definition":"Let us call this first kind of meaning—what is said—content. … It is contents that are evaluated in circumstances of evaluation. If the content is a proposition …, the result of the evaluation will be a truth-value.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"directly referential","aliases":["direct reference"],"definition":"in the case of a singular term which is directly referential, the constituent of the proposition is just the object itself. Thus it is that it does not just turn out that the constituent determines the same object in every circumstance, the constituent (corresponding to a rigid designator) just is the object.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"circumstance of evaluation","aliases":["circumstances of evaluation","circumstances","circumstance"],"definition":"we must distinguish possible occasions of use—which I call contexts—from possible circumstances of evaluation of what was said on a given occasion of use. Possible circumstances of evaluation I call circumstances or, sometimes, just counterfactual situations.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"monster","aliases":["monsters"],"definition":"Operators like 'In some contexts it is true that', which attempt to meddle with character, I call monsters. I claim that none can be expressed in English (without sneaking in a quotation device).","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"dthat","aliases":[],"definition":"[A] special demonstrative which requires completion by a description and which is treated as a directly referential term whose referent is the denotation of the associated description","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"(6) I am here now. … Intuitively, (6) is deeply, and in some sense, which we will shortly make precise, universally, true. One need only understand the meaning of (6) to know that it cannot be uttered falsely. … It is certainly not necessary that I be here now. But for several contingencies, I would be working in my garden now, or even delivering this paper in a location outside of Portland.","phenomenon":"contingent a priori truth","aliases":["contingencies"],"near":["necessary a posteriori truth","Moore's paradox","answering-machine paradox"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"We raise two identical twins, Castor and Pollux, under qualitatively identical conditions, qualitatively identical stimuli, etc. If necessary, we may monitor their brain states and make small corrections in their brain structures if they begin drifting apart. They respond to all cognitive stimuli in identical fashion. … Each sincerely says, My brother was born before I was and the beliefs they thereby express conflict. In this, Castor speaks the truth, while Pollux speaks falsely.","phenomenon":"same character, different content","near":["same content, different character","Frege's puzzle about informative identity","monster (context-shifting operator)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"on March 27 of this year, having lost track of the date, I may continue to hope to be finished by this March 26, without hoping to be finished by yesterday.","phenomenon":"same content, different character","near":["same character, different content","contingent a priori truth","monster (context-shifting operator)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Suppose I point at Paul and say, He now lives in Princeton, New Jersey. Call what I said … 'Pat'. … Suppose that Paul and Charles had each disguised themselves as the other and had switched places. If that had happened, and I had uttered as I did, then the proposition I would have expressed would have been false. But in that possible context the proposition I would have expressed is not Pat. … Pat … would still be true in the circumstances of the envisaged possible context … provided that Paul—in whatever costume he appeared—were still residing in Princeton.","phenomenon":"direct reference of demonstratives","near":["non-rigid descriptive designation","deferred reference","referential use of a definite description"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"A kidnapped heiress, locked in the trunk of a car, knowing neither the time nor where she is, may think 'It is quiet here now'. Could she plead that she could not have believed a proposition about that very place, since when thinking 'here' she did not know where she was?","phenomenon":"singular thought without acquaintance","near":["descriptive (de dicto) thought","reference failure","knowledge by acquaintance"],"exact":false}],"doi":null},{"id":"stalnaker1978-assertion","title":"Assertion","authors":["Robert Stalnaker"],"year":"1978","area":"language","topic":"speech acts & assertion","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"To make an assertion is to reduce the context set in a particular way, provided that there are no objections from the other participants in the conversation.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the essential effect of an assertion is to change the presuppositions of the participants in the conversation by adding the content of what is asserted to what is presupposed.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Asserting p, unless rejected, shrinks the context set C to C ∩ p. Rational assertion requires C ∩ p ≠ ∅ and C ⊈ p, and that the utterance express one proposition throughout C.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Where an utterance would express different propositions across the context set, it is reinterpreted as asserting its diagonal proposition, true at w iff what it expresses at w is true at w, explaining informative identities and negative existentials.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"context set","aliases":["context sets"],"definition":"the possible worlds compatible with what is presupposed. This set … is the set of possible worlds recognized by the speaker to be the “live options” relevant to the conversation.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"propositional concept","aliases":["propositional concepts"],"definition":"A propositional concept is … a function from an ordered pair of possible worlds into a truth value.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"diagonal proposition","aliases":["diagonal","diagonal propositions"],"definition":"the function from possible worlds into truth values whose values are read along the diagonal of the matrix from upper left to lower right … true at i for any i if and only if what is expressed in the utterance at i is true at i.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"speaker presupposition","aliases":["presupposed","presupposes","presuppose","presuppositions","presupposition"],"definition":"A proposition is presupposed if the speaker is disposed to act as if he assumes or believes that the proposition is true, and as if he assumes or believes that his audience assumes or believes that it is true as well.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"nondefective context","aliases":["nondefective","nondefective contexts"],"definition":"We may define a NONDEFECTIVE CONTEXT as one in which the presuppositions of the various participants in the conversation are all the same.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"dagger","aliases":[],"definition":"an operator which takes the diagonal proposition and projects it onto the horizontal. … [It] always yields a constant propositional concept as its value.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"I said You are a fool to O'Leary. O'Leary is a fool, so what I said was true, although O'Leary does not think so. Now Daniels, who is no fool and who knows it, was standing nearby, and he thought I was talking to him. So both O'Leary and Daniels thought I said something false: O'Leary understood what I said, but disagrees with me about the facts; Daniels, on the other hand, agrees with me about the fact (he knows that O'Leary is a fool), but misunderstood what I said.","phenomenon":"propositional concept (two-dimensional intension)","near":["rigid designation","essential indexical","contingent a priori truth"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Suppose that in worlds i, j, and k, a certain object, a metal bar, is one, two, and three meters long, respectively, at a certain time t. Now suppose an appropriate authority fixes the reference of the expression one meter by making the following statement in each of the worlds i, j, and k: This bar is one meter long. … The proposition expressed by the authority is one that might have been false, although he couldn't have expressed a false proposition in that utterance.","phenomenon":"contingent a priori truth","near":["necessary a posteriori truth","necessary a priori truth","analytic truth"],"exact":false},{"id":"e3","text":"hearing a woman talking in the next room, I tell you, That is either Zsa Zsa Gabor or Elizabeth Anscombe. Assuming that both demonstrative pronouns and proper names are rigid designators—terms that refer to the same individual in all possible worlds—this sentence comes out expressing either a necessary truth or a necessary falsehood, depending on whether it is one of the two mentioned women or someone else who is in the next room.","phenomenon":"diagonalization","near":["presupposition accommodation","contingent a priori truth","referential opacity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Suppose for example that you know that Jones won the election, believe mistakenly that I know it as well, and are prepared to take the truth of this proposition for granted if the occasion should arise, say by using it as a suppressed premiss in an argument, or by using the description the man who won the election to refer to Jones.","phenomenon":"defective context","near":["presupposition accommodation","presupposition failure","common knowledge"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1163/9789004368873_013"},{"id":"montague1973-proper-treatment-quantification","title":"The Proper Treatment of Quantification in Ordinary English","authors":["Richard Montague"],"year":"1973","area":"language","topic":"quantification & formal semantics","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I regard it as one of the principal virtues of the present treatment … that it enables us to deal directly with intensional locutions.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"We thus see the virtue of having intransitive verbs and common nouns denote sets of individual concepts rather than sets of individuals","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"A fragment of English, including intensional verbs like 'seek', gets a rigorous truth-conditional semantics via systematic translation of each syntactic derivation into tensed intensional logic; alternative derivations yield genuine scope and de re/de dicto ambiguities.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Every term, proper names included, denotes a set of properties: ‘John’ ↦ λP.P{^j}, ‘every man’ ↦ λP.∀x[man′(x) → P{x}]; quantifying a term α in for the pronoun heₙ in φ yields α′(^λxₙ.φ′), generating scope readings.","formal":true}],"pick":2,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"analysis tree","aliases":["analysis trees","tree","trees"],"definition":"To each node we attach a meaningful expression … with the index of that structural operation … which we understand as having been applied in obtaining the expression in question; the nodes dominated by any node are to be occupied by the expressions to which the structural operation is understood as having been applied …","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"logically determinate","aliases":["logically determinate"],"definition":"The truth of (1) guarantees that proper nouns will be “logically determinate” according to the interpretations under consideration, that is, will have extensions invariant with respect to possible worlds and moments of time.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"individual concept","aliases":["individual concepts","individual-concept"],"definition":"An object of type ⟨s,e⟩: a function from world–time pairs to entities. Common nouns and intransitive verbs denote sets of these; for ‘horse’ they are constant functions, but ‘the temperature’ picks out one whose value varies with time.","exact":false},{"id":"t4","term":"pronoun of laziness","aliases":["pronouns of laziness","pronoun of laziness","laziness"],"definition":"… pronouns of laziness, that is, as “standing for” longer terms bearing a somewhat indefinite relation to other expressions in the sentence in question (or preceding sentences within the discourse in question).","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"The source of the ambiguity of John seeks a unicorn will perhaps be clarified if we compare that sentence with the intuitively synonymous John tries to find a unicorn … Here … the ambiguity is clearly a matter of scope, and indeed depends on the possibility of regarding either the component find a unicorn or the whole sentence as the scope of the existential quantification indicated by a unicorn.","phenomenon":"de dicto vs de re reading","near":["referential vs attributive use of a description","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","collective vs distributive reading"],"avoid":["quantifier scope ambiguity","specific vs non-specific indefinite","intensional transitive verb"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"‘a woman loves every man’ translates into two non-equivalent formulas, ∃u[woman′*(u) ∧ ∀v[man′*(v) → love′*(u, v)]] and ∀v[man′*(v) → ∃u[woman′*(u) ∧ love′*(u, v)]], although the sentence contains no intensional expression.","phenomenon":"quantifier scope ambiguity","near":["de dicto vs de re reading","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","collective vs distributive reading"],"exact":false},{"id":"e3","text":"From the premises the temperature is ninety and the temperature rises, the conclusion ninety rises would appear to follow by normal principles of logic; yet there are occasions on which both premises are true, but none on which the conclusion is.","phenomenon":"Partee's temperature paradox","aliases":["temperature"],"near":["Frege's puzzle about informative identity","substitution failure in attitude contexts","problem of temporary intrinsics"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"On the other hand, in each of the following examples only one reading is possible, and that the referential: (1) John seeks a unicorn and Mary seeks it, (2) John tries to find a unicorn and wishes to eat it … This is, according to my intuitions … as it should be; but David Kaplan would differ, at least as to (2).","phenomenon":"intentional identity","near":["donkey anaphora","cataphora (backward anaphora)","sloppy identity under ellipsis"],"avoid":["pronoun of laziness","bound-variable anaphora","modal subordination","de dicto vs de re reading"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/978-94-010-2506-5_10"},{"id":"lewis1979-scorekeeping-language-game","title":"Scorekeeping in a Language Game","authors":["David Lewis"],"year":"1979","area":"language","topic":"context & common ground","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I suggest that many components of conversational score obey rules of accommodation, and that these rules figure prominently among the rules governing the kinematics of conversational score.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Say something that requires a missing presupposition, and straightway that presupposition springs into existence, making what you said acceptable after all.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Contextual parameters (what is presupposed, which individual is most salient, how precise we are being, which possibilities are live) tend, within limits, to shift so that whatever is said counts as acceptable.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Rule schema: if at t an utterance requires conversational-score component sᵢ to lie in range r for acceptability, sᵢ ∉ r just before t, and further conditions hold, then (ceteris paribus) sᵢ ∈ r at t.","formal":true}],"pick":2,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"conversational score","aliases":["score"],"definition":"Like the components of a baseball score, the components of a conversational score at a given stage are abstract entities. They may not be numbers, but they are other set-theoretic constructs: sets of presupposed propositions, boundaries between permissible and impermissible courses of action, or the like.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"rule of accommodation","aliases":[],"definition":"If at time t something is said that requires component sᵢ of conversational score to have a value in the range r if what is said is to be true, or otherwise acceptable; and if sᵢ does not have a value in the range r just before t; and if such-and-such further conditions hold; then at t the score-component sᵢ takes some value in the range r.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"true enough","aliases":["enough"],"definition":"we treat a sentence more or less as if it is simply true, if it is true over a large enough part of the range of delineations of its vagueness. (For short: if it is true enough.)","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"definite description","aliases":[],"definition":"“the F” denotes x if and only if x is the most salient F in the domain of discourse, according to some contextually determined salience ranking.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"In the room is a cat, Bruce, who has been making himself very salient by dashing madly about. He is the only cat in the room, or in sight, or in earshot. I start to speak to you: The cat is in the carton. The cat will never meet our other cat, because our other cat lives in New Zealand. Our New Zealand cat lives with the Cresswells. And there he’ll stay, because Miriam would be sad if the cat went away.","phenomenon":"incomplete definite description resolved by salience","aliases":["salient"],"near":["attributive use of a definite description","donkey anaphora","bridging (associative) anaphora"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Peter Unger has argued that hardly anything is flat. Take something you claim is flat; he will find something else and get you to agree that it is even flatter. You think the pavement is flat - but how can you deny that your desk is flatter? But “flat” is an absolute term: it is inconsistent to say that something is flatter than something that is flat. Having agreed that your desk is flatter than the pavement, you must concede that the pavement is not flat after all.","phenomenon":"accommodating rise in standards of precision","near":["sorites paradox","scalar implicature","faultless disagreement"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"The commonsensical epistemologist says: “I know the cat is in the carton - there he is before my eyes - I just can’t be wrong about that!” The sceptic replies: “You might be the victim of a deceiving demon”. Thereby he brings into consideration possibilities hitherto ignored, else what he says would be false.","phenomenon":"accommodation widening the range of relevant possibilities","aliases":["possibilities"],"near":["Gettier case","lottery paradox","Moore's paradox"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"That is why it is peculiar to say, out of the blue, “All Fred’s children are asleep, and Fred has children.” The first part requires and thereby creates a presupposition that Fred has children; so the second part adds nothing to what is already presupposed when it is said; so the second part has no conversational point. It would not have been peculiar to say instead “Fred has children, and all Fred’s children are asleep.”","phenomenon":"presupposition accommodation","aliases":["presupposition","presupposed"],"near":["presupposition cancellation","scalar implicature","Moore's paradox"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00258436"},{"id":"perry1979-problem-essential-indexical","title":"The Problem of the Essential Indexical","authors":["John Perry"],"year":"1979","area":"language","topic":"attitude reports & de se","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"belief states must be distinguished from objects of belief, cannot be individuated in terms of them, and are what is crucial for the explanation of action","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[T]here is not an identity, or even an isomorphic correspondence, but only a systematic relationship between the belief states one is in and what one thereby believes.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Indexical beliefs such as 'I am making a mess' explain action in a way no non-indexical substitute can, and no account of belief as a relation to propositions (Fregean, de re, or relativized) captures them.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Belief has two tiers: belief states, classified by the indexical sentences one would accept and doing the explanatory work, and the de re propositions believed, fixed by state plus context; one state can carry different beliefs.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"essential indexical","aliases":["indexicals","indexical","essential","essentially"],"definition":"In these three cases the subjects in explaining their actions, would use indexicals to characterize certain beliefs they came to have. These indexicals are essential, in that replacement of them by other terms destroys the force of the explanation, or at least requires certain assumptions to be made to preserve it.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"locating beliefs","aliases":["locating belief","locating"],"definition":"I shall use the term \"locating beliefs\" to refer to one's beliefs about where one is, when it is, and who one is. Such beliefs seem essentially indexical.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"missing conceptual ingredient","aliases":["conceptual ingredient"],"definition":"There is a missing conceptual ingredient: a sense for which I am the reference, or a complex of properties I alone have, or a singular term that refers to no one but me. To identify the proposition I came to believe, the advocate of the doctrine of propositions must identify this missing conceptual ingredient.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"belief state","aliases":["belief states"],"definition":"We use sentences with indexicals or relativized propositions to individuate belief states, for the purposes of classifying believers in ways useful for explanation and prediction. That is, belief states individuated in this way enter into our common sense theory about human behavior","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"limited accessibility","aliases":["inaccessible","accessibility","limited"],"definition":"there is a class of propositions which can only be expressed in special circumstances. In particular, only I could express the proposition I expressed when I said \"I am making a mess.\" Others can see, perhaps by analogy with their own case, that there is a proposition that I express, but it is in a sense inaccessible to them.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"I once followed a trail of sugar on a supermarket floor, pushing my cart down the aisle on one side of a tall counter and back the aisle on the other, seeking the shopper with the torn sack to tell him he was making a mess. With each trip around the counter, the trail became thicker. But I seemed unable to catch up. Finally it dawned on me. I was the shopper I was trying to catch.","phenomenon":"essential indexical","near":["immunity to error through misidentification","self-deception","Moore's paradox"],"avoid":["de se belief","self-locating belief","Frege's puzzle","de re belief"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Imagine two lost campers who trust the same guidebook but disagree about where they are. If we were to try to characterize the beliefs of these campers without the use of indexicals, it would seem impossible to bring out this disagreement. If, for example, we characterized their beliefs by the set of \"eternal sentences,\" drawn from the guidebook they would mark \"true\", there is no reason to suppose that the sets would differ. They could mark all of the same sentences \"true\", and still disagree","phenomenon":"self-locating belief","near":["faultless disagreement","verbal dispute","Gettier case"],"avoid":["essential indexical","de se belief","locating belief"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Suppose there were mirrors at either end of the counter so that as I pushed my cart down the aisle in pursuit I saw myself in the mirror. I take what I see to be the reflection of the messy shopper going up the aisle on the other side, not realizing that what I am really seeing is a reflection of a reflection of myself. I point and say, truly, \"I believe that he is making a mess.\"","phenomenon":"de re (not de se) belief about oneself","near":["de se belief","de dicto belief","reference failure"],"avoid":["essential indexical","Frege's puzzle"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Of course if the change of context is not noted, the adjustment of belief states will not occur, and a wholesale change from believing truly to believing falsely may occur. This is what happened to Rip Van Winkle. He awakes in the same belief states he fell asleep in twenty years earlier, unadjusted to the dramatic change in context, and so with a whole new set of beliefs, such as that he is a young man, mostly false.","phenomenon":"cognitive dynamics: losing track of time","near":["Sleeping Beauty problem","Gettier case","Moore's paradox"],"avoid":["self-locating belief","essential indexical","slow switching"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2214792"},{"id":"burge1979-individualism-mental","title":"Individualism and the Mental","authors":["Tyler Burge"],"year":"1979","area":"language","topic":"externalism & mental content","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Social context infects even the distinctively mental features of mentalistic attributions. No man's intentional mental phenomena are insular.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"to a fair degree, mentalistic attribution rests not on the subject's having mastered the contents of the attribution … but on his having a certain responsibility to communal conventions","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Holding fixed everything about an individual described physically, behaviorally and phenomenally, varying only how his linguistic community uses a word he partly misunderstands changes which beliefs he has; so mental content depends partly on social environment.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Ordinary practice correctly ascribes thoughts whose contents the thinker only partly understands, since speakers answer to communal standards; this tells against Cartesian transparency of content and against functionalist accounts confined to the individual's inputs, outputs and inner states.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"individualistic","aliases":["individualism","individualistically"],"definition":"philosophical treatments that seek to see a person's intentional mental phenomena ultimately and purely in terms of what happens to the person, what occurs within him, and how he responds to his physical environment, without any essential reference to the social context in which he or the interpreter of his mental phenomena are situated.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"oblique occurrence","aliases":["oblique","obliquely","oblique occurrences"],"definition":"When an expression like 'water' functions in a content clause so that it is not freely exchangeable with all extensionally equivalent expressions, we shall say that it has oblique occurrence.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"partial understanding","aliases":["partially understands","partially","partial"],"definition":"One may pick a case in which the subject only partially understands an expression. He may apply it firmly and correctly in a range of cases, but be unclear or agnostic about certain of its applications or implications which, in fact, are fully established in common practice.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"metalinguistic reinterpretation","aliases":["metalinguistic","reinterpreted","reinterpretation"],"definition":"to count the error of the subject as purely metalinguistic. Thus the patient's apparent belief that he had arthritis in the thigh would be reinterpreted as a belief that 'arthritis' applied to something (or some disease) in his thigh.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"The patient reports to his doctor his fear that his arthritis has now lodged in his thigh. The doctor replies by telling him that this cannot be so, since arthritis is specifically an inflammation of joints. … We are to conceive of a situation in which the patient proceeds from birth through the same course of physical events that he actually does … The counterfactuality in the supposition touches only the patient's social environment. … in our imagined case, physicians, lexicographers, and informed laymen apply 'arthritis' not only to arthritis but to various other rheumatoid ailments.","phenomenon":"social externalism (anti-individualism)","aliases":["social"],"near":["natural-kind externalism","indexical reference","inverted qualia"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"When Alfred refers to an apple, saying to himself \"That is wholesome,\" what he refers to depends not just on the content of what he says or thinks, but on what apple is before him. Without altering the meaning of Alfred's utterance, the nature of his perceptual experiences, or his physical acts or dispositions, we could conceive an exchange of the actual apple for another one that is indistinguishable to Alfred. We would thereby conceive him as referring to something different and even as saying something with a different truth value.","phenomenon":"indexical (demonstrative) reference","aliases":["indexicality","indexical","indexically"],"near":["social externalism (anti-individualism)","referential opacity","de dicto belief"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"from the facts that water is H2O and that Bertrand thought that water is not fit to drink, it does not follow that Bertrand thought that H2O is not fit to drink.","phenomenon":"referential opacity (substitution failure)","aliases":["oblique","obliquely","substitutable","intersubstitutable"],"near":["de re belief","indexical reference","necessary a posteriori identity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"If Archie says, 'Lead the way and we will precede', we routinely reinterpret the words in describing his expectations.","phenomenon":"malapropism","aliases":["malapropisms"],"near":["spoonerism","verbal irony","regional dialect usage"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1111/j.1475-4975.1979.tb00374.x"},{"id":"davidson1967-logical-form-action","title":"The Logical Form of Action Sentences","authors":["Donald Davidson"],"year":"1967","area":"language","topic":"tense, events & logical form","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The basic idea is that verbs of action—verbs that say 'what someone did'—should be construed as containing a place, for singular terms or variables, that they do not appear to.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"there is, of course, no variable polyadicity. The problem is solved in the natural way, by introducing events as entities about which an indefinite number of things can be said.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Action verbs carry a hidden event place: 'Shem kicked Shaun' is ∃e Kicked(Shem, Shaun, e), and modifiers are conjoined predicates of e, so ∃e(Buttered(Jones, toast, e) ∧ In(bathroom, e)) entails ∃e Buttered(Jones, toast, e).","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Unlike analyses via 'x brings it about that p' or an operator on whole sentences, quantifying over events keeps action sentences extensional: co-referring terms substitute, modifiers drop, and a Tarski-style truth theory can handle them.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"variable polyadicity","aliases":[],"definition":"The apparent feature of action verbs, so named by Kenny, that they take a varying, indefinitely extendable number of argument places (two in 'Jones buttered the toast', three with 'in the bathroom', and so on), an analysis that obliterates the entailments among such sentences.","exact":false},{"id":"t2","term":"event-place","aliases":["event-places"],"definition":"Part of what we must learn when we learn the meaning of any predicate is how many places it has, and what sorts of entities the variables that hold these places range over. Some predicates have an event-place, some do not.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"logical form","aliases":[],"definition":"[Limits on logical form] are set if our interest is in giving a coherent and constructive account of meaning: we must uncover enough structure to make it possible to state, for an arbitrary sentence, how its meaning depends on that structure, and we must not attribute more structure than such a theory of meaning can accommodate.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"intentionally","aliases":["intentional"],"definition":"intentional actions are not a class of actions, or, to put the point a little differently, doing something intentionally is not a manner of doing it. To say someone did something intentionally is to describe the action in a way that bears a special relation to the beliefs and attitudes of the agent","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Jones buttered the toast in the bathroom with a knife at midnight. … If we go on to analyse 'Jones buttered the toast' as containing a two-place predicate, 'Jones buttered the toast in the bathroom' as containing a three-place predicate, and so forth, we obliterate the logical relations between these sentences, namely that (2) entails the others.","phenomenon":"adverb-dropping entailment (modifier drop)","near":["non-intersective (attributive) adjective modification","quantifier scope ambiguity","causative/inchoative alternation"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Susan says, 'I crossed the Channel in fifteen hours.' 'Good grief, that was slow.' … Now Susan adds, 'But I swam.' 'Good grief, that was fast.' We do not withdraw the claim that it was a slow crossing; this is consistent with its being a fast swimming.","phenomenon":"non-intersective (attributive) adjective modification","near":["intersective adjective modification","privative adjective modification","lexical ambiguity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"'I didn't know it was loaded' belongs to one standard pattern of excuse. I do not deny that I pointed the gun and pulled the trigger, nor that I shot the victim. My ignorance explains how it happened that I pointed the gun and pulled the trigger intentionally, but did not shoot the victim intentionally.","phenomenon":"one action under different descriptions","near":["deviant (wayward) causal chain","weakness of will (akrasia)","doctrine of double effect"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Treat 'x consists in the fact that S' as a predicate of events that permits substituting co-referring singular terms and logically equivalent sentences. S is logically equivalent to 'ιy(y=y ∧ S) = ιy(y=y)', and for any R materially equivalent to S, 'ιy(y=y ∧ S)' and 'ιy(y=y ∧ R)' refer to the same thing; so whatever consists in the fact that S consists in the fact that R, and all events that occur are identical.","phenomenon":"slingshot argument","near":["Frege's puzzle about identity statements","Mates's puzzle about synonym substitution","Russell's paradox"],"exact":false}],"doi":"10.2307/jj.13027259.6"},{"id":"davidson1973-radical-interpretation","title":"Radical Interpretation","authors":["Donald Davidson"],"year":"1973","area":"language","topic":"interpretation & convention","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"what Tarski assumed outright for each T-sentence can be indirectly elicited by a holistic constraint. If that constraint is adequate, each T-sentence will in fact yield interpretations.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"interpreting an agent's intentions, his beliefs and his words are parts of a single project, no part of which can be assumed to be complete before the rest is.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Because belief and meaning are interdependent, the interpreter holds belief constant by charity—treating speakers' held-true sentences as mostly true by his own lights—and solves for meaning, fitting first-order logic first, then indexical sentences, then the rest.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A truth theory serves for interpretation if it entails, for each sentence s, 's is true ↔ p'—p merely true iff s is, not a translation—and its T-sentences jointly best fit evidence about which sentences speakers hold true.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"radical interpretation","aliases":[],"definition":"The task, implicit in all understanding of another's speech, of saying what one could know that would suffice to understand a speaker's utterances, and what evidence—available without prior knowledge of the language and statable without notions like meaning or synonymy—could support it.","exact":false},{"id":"t2","term":"T-sentence","aliases":["T-sentences"],"definition":"s is true (in the object language) if and only if p. Instances of the form (which we shall call T-sentences) are obtained by replacing \"s\" by a canonical description of s, and \"p\" by a translation of s.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"holding a sentence true","aliases":["holding true","holds true","held true","hold true"],"definition":"A single attitude, applicable to all sentences, of accepting a sentence as true—one an interpreter can plausibly identify before interpreting, since he may know that a speaker intends to express a truth without knowing what truth; the evidential base of the theory.","exact":false},{"id":"t4","term":"principle of charity","aliases":["charity"],"definition":"Solving the interdependence of belief and meaning by holding belief constant as far as possible while solving for meaning: assigning truth conditions that make speakers right when plausibly possible, by our own lights—applied across the board, not only to the sentential connectives as in Quine.","exact":false}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Kurt belongs to the German speech community and Kurt holds true \"Es regnet\" on Saturday at noon and it is raining near Kurt on Saturday at noon. This is taken as evidence that (x)(t)(if x belongs to the German speech community then (x holds true \"Es regnet\" at t if and only if it is raining near x at t)), even though Kurt, or anyone else, may be wrong about whether it is raining near him.","phenomenon":"principle of charity","near":["indeterminacy of translation","Gricean non-natural meaning","semantic deference"],"exact":false},{"id":"e2","text":"the T-sentence does fix the truth value relative to certain conditions, but it does not say the object language sentence is true because the conditions hold. Yet if truth value were all that mattered, the T-sentence for \"Snow is white\" could as well say that it is true if and only if grass is green or 2 + 2 = 4 as say that it is true if and only if snow is white.","phenomenon":"Foster's extensionality problem for T-theories","near":["slingshot argument","Frege's puzzle about identity statements","Mates's puzzle about synonym substitution"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Suppose, absurdly, that the constraints narrowed the possible theories down to one, implying '\"Es regnet\" is true-in-German when spoken by x at time t if and only if it is raining near x at t'; we could then take Kurt's utterance of \"Es regnet\" as his saying that it is raining. Realistically, acceptable theories will not all be identical: when all the evidence is in, there will remain trade-offs between the beliefs we attribute to a speaker and the meanings we assign his words, yet any theory that passes the tests will serve.","phenomenon":"indeterminacy of interpretation","near":["Foster's extensionality problem for T-theories","conceptual-scheme relativism","slingshot argument"],"exact":false}],"doi":"10.1111/j.1746-8361.1973.tb00623.x"},{"id":"davidson1974-very-idea-conceptual","title":"On the Very Idea of a Conceptual Scheme","authors":["Donald Davidson"],"year":"1974","area":"language","topic":"interpretation & convention","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[The] dualism of scheme and content … cannot be made intelligible and defensible. It is itself a dogma of empiricism, the third dogma.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Given the underlying methodology of interpretation, we could not be in a position to judge that others had concepts or beliefs radically different from our own.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"There is no coherent notion of an alternative conceptual scheme: the dualism of organizing scheme and unorganized content is an untenable third dogma of empiricism, so we can say neither that schemes differ nor that all share one.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A different scheme would have to be largely true but untranslatable, yet truth cannot be understood apart from translation (Convention T); and because interpretation must be charitable, even partial untranslatability cannot reveal radically different concepts.","formal":false}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"conceptual scheme","aliases":["conceptual schemes","scheme"],"definition":"We may identify conceptual schemes with languages, then, or better, allowing for the possibility that more than one language may express the same scheme, sets of intertranslatable languages.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"scheme–content dualism","aliases":["dualism of conceptual scheme and empirical content","dualism"],"definition":"in place of the dualism of the analytic-synthetic we get the dualism of conceptual scheme and empirical content. The new dualism is the foundation of an empiricism shorn of the untenable dogmas of the analytic-synthetic distinction and reductionism","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"charity","aliases":["Charity","principle of charity"],"definition":"charity is not an option, but a condition of having a workable theory … if we want to understand others, we must count them right in most matters.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"holding a sentence true","aliases":["holds a certain sentence to be true","holding it true","holding the sentence true"],"definition":"if we merely know that someone holds a certain sentence to be true, we know neither what he means by the sentence nor what belief his holding it true represents. His holding the sentence true is thus the vector of two forces","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"incommensurable","aliases":["Incommensurable","incommensurability"],"definition":"\"Incommensurable\" is, of course, Kuhn and Feyerabend's word for \"not intertranslatable.\"","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"If you see a ketch sailing by and your companion says, \"Look at that handsome yawl,\" you may be faced with a problem of interpretation. One natural possibility is that your friend has mistaken a ketch for a yawl, and has formed a false belief. But if his vision is good and his line of sight favorable it is even more plausible that he does not use the word \"yawl\" quite as you do, and has made no mistake at all about the position of the jigger on the passing yacht.","phenomenon":"principle of charity","near":["social externalism about content","linguistic division of labor","conversational implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"some language, say Saturnian, may be translatable into English, and some further language, like Plutonian, may be translatable into Saturnian, while Plutonian is not translatable into English. Enough translatable differences may add up to an untranslatable one.","phenomenon":"intransitivity of translatability","near":["indeterminacy of translation","inscrutability of reference","radical interpretation"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Someone who sets out to organize a closet arranges the things in it. If you are told not to organize the shoes and shirts, but the closet itself, you would be bewildered. How would you organize the Pacific Ocean? Straighten out its shores, perhaps, or relocate its islands, or destroy its fish.","phenomenon":"conceptual scheme organizing reality","aliases":["organize"],"near":["conceptual scheme fitting experience","incommensurability of theories","intransitivity of translatability"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"The sentence \"My skin is warm\" is true if and only if my skin is warm. Here there is no reference to a fact, a world, an experience, or a piece of evidence.","phenomenon":"Tarskian T-sentence","near":["verification condition","assertibility condition","liar sentence"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/3129898"},{"id":"lewis1970-general-semantics","title":"General Semantics","authors":["David Lewis"],"year":"1970","area":"language","topic":"quantification & formal semantics","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"It is natural … to identify meanings with semantically interpreted phrase markers minus their terminal nodes: finite ordered trees having at each node a category and an appropriate intension.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Semantics with no treatment of truth conditions is not semantics.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Basic-category intensions are functions from indices (world, time, place, speaker, audience, indicated objects, prior discourse, variable assignment) to extensions; a c/c₁…cₙ intension maps c₁-,…,cₙ-intensions to a c-intension, so every projection rule is φ₀(φ₁…φₙ).","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"If every language of interest has a categorially based transformational grammar whose base structure alone fixes meaning, one uniform format serves all possible languages: categorial syntax paired with function-application semantics, transformations being semantically idle.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"Markerese","aliases":["Semantic Markerese","markers"],"definition":"Semantic markers are symbols: items in the vocabulary of an artificial language we may call Semantic Markerese. … we can know the Markerese translation of an English sentence without knowing the first thing about the meaning of the English sentence: namely, the conditions under which it would be true.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"index","aliases":["indices"],"definition":"We may take indices as n-tuples (finite sequences) of the various items other than meaning that may enter into determining extensions. We call these various items coordinates of the index","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"compositional intension","aliases":["compositional intensions","intension","intensions"],"definition":"an appropriate intension for a (c/c1...cn) … is any n-place function from c1-intensions, ..., and cn-intensions to c-intensions. … We will call these intensions for derived categories compositional intensions.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"binder","aliases":["binding"],"definition":"a separate constituent called a binder: a certain sort of (S/N)/S that takes a sentence and makes an extensional verb phrase by binding a variable at all its free occurrences (if any) in the sentence.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"method of paraphrased performatives","aliases":["paraphrases","performatives"],"definition":"I propose that [non-declaratives like 'Be late!'] ought to be treated as paraphrases of the corresponding performatives, having the same base structure, meaning, intension, and truth-value at an index or on an occasion.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"delineation coordinate","aliases":[],"definition":"Let the indices contain a delineation coordinate: a positive real number, regarded as the boundary temperature between cool and warm things. … More generally, the delineation coordinate must be a sequence of boundary-specifying numbers.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"What is the set of things to which 'alleged' applies? An alleged Communist is not something which is, on the one hand, an alleged thing and, on the other hand, a Communist. … at certain indices 'Communist' and 'Maoist' have the same extension but 'alleged Communist' and 'alleged Maoist' do not","phenomenon":"intensional (non-extensional) adjective","near":["intersective adjective modification","subsective adjective","privative adjective modification"],"avoid":["non-intersective (attributive) adjective modification"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"[Doubling object-takers] would give us one meaning for 'Every boy loves some girl': the weaker meaning, on which the sentence can be true even if each boy loves a different girl. But the sentence is ambiguous; where shall we get a stronger meaning, on which the sentence is true only if a certain girl - Zuleika, perhaps - is loved by all boys?","phenomenon":"quantifier scope ambiguity","aliases":["ambiguous"],"near":["de dicto vs de re reading","lexical ambiguity (homonymy)","collective vs distributive reading"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"'Lothario seeks a girl', in the sense in which it can be paraphrased as 'Lothario seeks a certain particular girl', can have the base structure … but what about the sense in which any old girl would do?","phenomenon":"de dicto vs de re reading","near":["referential vs attributive use of a description","free-choice indefinite","collective vs distributive reading"],"avoid":["quantifier scope ambiguity","specific vs non-specific indefinite","intensional transitive verb","referential opacity (substitution failure)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Consider the sentence 'This is older than this'. I might say it pointing at a 1962 Volkswagen when I say the first 'this' and at a 1963 Volkswagen when I say the second 'this'. The sentence should be true on such an occasion; but how can it be?","phenomenon":"repeated demonstrative with distinct demonstrata","near":["anaphoric (coreferential) pronoun","deferred ostension","shifted indexical (monster)"],"avoid":["indexical (demonstrative) reference","indexicality (context-sensitive expressions)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"Consider the sentence 'The door is open'. This does not mean that the one and only door that now exists is open; nor does it mean that the one and only door near the place of utterance, or pointed at, or mentioned in previous discourse, is open. Rather it means that the one and only door among the objects that are somehow prominent on the occasion is open.","phenomenon":"incomplete definite description resolved by salience","near":["anaphoric definite description","deictic use of a definite description","generic definite description"],"avoid":["attributive use of a definite description","referential use of a definite description","quantifier domain restriction"],"exact":true},{"id":"e6","text":"Pretend first that the only vagueness is the vagueness of 'cool' and 'warm' … sentences with vague constituents are not necessarily vague: 'This is cool or warm, but not both' is true at all delineations, on an occasion on which there is a unique indicated object, even if the indicated object is lukewarm.","phenomenon":"penumbral connection","near":["sorites paradox","higher-order vagueness","context-sensitivity of gradable adjectives"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00413598"},{"id":"stalnaker1974-pragmatic-presuppositions","title":"Pragmatic Presuppositions","authors":["Robert Stalnaker"],"year":"1974","area":"language","topic":"presupposition & projection","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"constraints on presuppositions are constraints on the contexts in which statements can be made, and not constraints on the truth-conditions of propositions expressed in making the statements","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Presupposing is thus not a mental attitude like believing, but is rather a linguistic disposition—a disposition to behave in one's use of language as if one had certain beliefs","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A speaker presupposes P when she takes P for granted as common background: she believes P, believes her addressee believes P, and takes the addressee to recognize this. Presuppositions belong to persons and contexts, not sentences.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Facts linguists build into the lexicon or semantics, such as the factive/semi-factive contrast and Karttunen's projection rule for conjunctions and conditionals, follow from general conversational rationality: what is asserted or supposed joins the background, and supposing or asking signals non-presupposition.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"pragmatic presupposition","aliases":["speaker presupposition"],"definition":"A proposition P is a pragmatic presupposition of a speaker in a given context just in case the speaker assumes or believes that P, assumes or believes that his addressee assumes or believes that P, and assumes or believes that his addressee recognizes that he is making these assumptions, or has these beliefs.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"semantic presupposition","aliases":["presupposes"],"definition":"a proposition that P presupposes that Q if and only if Q must be true in order that P have a truth-value at all","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"common background","aliases":["background"],"definition":"a background of beliefs or assumptions which are shared by the speaker and his audience, and which are recognized by them to be so shared","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"conversational acceptability","aliases":["conversationally acceptable"],"definition":"a speech act is conversationally acceptable in the relevant sense just in case it can reasonably be expected to accomplish its purpose in the normal way in which the normal purposes of such speech acts are accomplished","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"If I say that Sam regrets that he voted for Nixon, I presuppose that Sam voted for Nixon, and assert that he feels bad about it.","phenomenon":"factive presupposition","near":["existential presupposition","presupposition accommodation","presupposition filtering"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"I am asked by someone who I have just met, \"Are you going to lunch?\" I reply, \"No, I've got to pick up my sister.\" Here I seem to presuppose that I have a sister, even though I do not assume that the addressee knows this. Yet the statement is clearly acceptable","phenomenon":"presupposition accommodation","near":["presupposition failure","presupposition projection","presupposition filtering"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Compare \"If I regret later that I have not told the truth, I will confess it to everyone\" with the same sentence using \"realize\" or \"discover\" in place of \"regret\". With \"regret\" the speaker clearly presupposes that he has not told the truth; with \"realize\" or \"discover\" he clearly does not.","phenomenon":"presupposition cancellation with semi-factives","near":["presupposition failure","existential presupposition","presupposition filtering"],"exact":false},{"id":"e4","text":"So if I say \"Harry is married and Harry's wife is a great cook,\" I assert, and do not presuppose, that Harry is married. But the second conjunct, stated alone (Harry's wife is a great cook), would require the presupposition that Harry is married. The sentence with conjuncts in reverse order would be unacceptable in any normal context (Harry's wife is a great cook, and Harry is married).","phenomenon":"presupposition filtering","aliases":["presupposition"],"near":["presupposition accommodation","presupposition failure","temporal implicature of 'and'"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"stalnaker1968-theory-conditionals","title":"A Theory of Conditionals","authors":["Robert Stalnaker"],"year":"1968","area":"language","topic":"conditionals","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"First, add the antecedent (hypothetically) to your stock of beliefs; second, make whatever adjustments are required to maintain consistency …; finally, consider whether or not the consequent is then true.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"A > B is true in α if B is true in f(A, α); A > B is false in α if B is false in f(A, α).","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"'If A, then B' is true just in case B is true in the one A-world that a selection function picks out as differing minimally from the actual world (the actual world itself, if A is true).","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"The conditional connective lies between material and strict implication but, unlike both, invalidates transitivity, strengthening of the antecedent and contraposition, while ¬(A > B) is equivalent to A > ¬B whenever A is possible.","formal":true}],"pick":2,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"selection function","aliases":["s-function"],"definition":"a selection function, f, which takes a proposition and a possible world as arguments and a possible world as its value. [It picks], for each antecedent A, a particular possible world in which A is true.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"absurd world","aliases":[],"definition":"[the one] in which contradictions and all their consequences are true. It is an isolated element under R … The purpose of λ is to allow for an interpretation of “If A, then B” in the case where A is impossible","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"pragmatic ambiguity","aliases":["pragmatic","pragmatically","ambiguous","ambiguity"],"definition":"A sentence is ambiguous if there is more than one proposition which it may properly be interpreted to express. … pragmatic (if the interpretation depends directly on the context of use). … Any sentence involving pronouns, tensed verbs, articles or quantifiers is pragmatically ambiguous.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"pragmatic problem of counterfactuals","aliases":["problem"],"definition":"The questions, “On what basis do we select a selection function from among the acceptable ones?” and “What are the criteria for ordering possible worlds?” are reformulations of the pragmatic problem of counterfactuals, which is a problem in the application of conditional logic.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Premisses. “If J. Edgar Hoover were today a communist, then he would be a traitor.” “If J. Edgar Hoover had been born a Russian, then he would today be a communist.” Conclusion. “If J. Edgar Hoover had been born a Russian, he would be a traitor.” It seems reasonable to affirm these premisses and deny the conclusion.","phenomenon":"failure of transitivity (hypothetical syllogism)","near":["failure of antecedent strengthening","failure of contraposition","conditional excluded middle"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Consider “If this match were struck, it would light,” and “If this match had been soaked in water overnight and it were struck, it would light.”","phenomenon":"failure of antecedent strengthening","near":["failure of transitivity (hypothetical syllogism)","failure of contraposition","conditional excluded middle"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"“If the U.S. halts the bombing, then North Vietnam will not agree to negotiate.” A person would believe that this statement is true if he thought that the North Vietnamese were determined to press for a complete withdrawal of U.S. troops. But he would surely deny the contrapositive, “If North Vietnam agrees to negotiate, then the U.S. will not have halted the bombing.” He would believe that a halt in the bombing, and much more, is required to bring the North Vietnamese to the negotiating table.","phenomenon":"failure of contraposition","aliases":["contrapositive"],"near":["failure of antecedent strengthening","failure of transitivity (hypothetical syllogism)","failure of modus tollens"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"To deny “If Kennedy were alive today, we wouldn’t be in this Vietnam mess,” we say, “If Kennedy were alive today, we would so be in this Vietnam mess.”","phenomenon":"conditional excluded middle","near":["would/might duality of counterfactuals","failure of contraposition","failure of antecedent strengthening"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"The statement is, “If the Chinese enter the Vietnam conflict, the United States will use nuclear weapons.” … the following piece of reasoning is an obvious non sequitur: “I firmly believe that the Chinese will stay out of the conflict; therefore I believe that the statement is true.”","phenomenon":"paradox of material implication","near":["denying the antecedent","affirming the consequent","failure of contraposition"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/978-94-009-9117-0_2"},{"id":"kamp1981-theory-truth-semantic","title":"A Theory of Truth and Semantic Representation","authors":["Hans Kamp"],"year":"1981","area":"language","topic":"dynamic semantics & anaphora","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Indefinite descriptions are, on the account given here, referential terms, not existential quantifiers.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"A discourse D is true in M, given a DRS K of D iff there is a proper embedding into M of the principal DR of K.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Hearers' representations of a discourse—partial models built by rules from its syntax, in which indefinites introduce discourse referents and pronouns pick up available ones—fix truth conditions: the discourse is true iff its representation embeds properly into the world-model.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"'If A then B' is true iff every proper embedding f of A's DR m extends to a proper embedding g ⊇ f of m′ (m plus B's content); hence antecedent indefinites get universal force.","formal":true}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"discourse representation (DR)","aliases":["discourse representation","discourse representations","representation","representations","DR","DR's"],"definition":"The representations postulated here are … similar in structure to the models familiar from model-theoretic semantics. In fact, formally they are nothing other than partial models, typically with small finite domains.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"Discourse Representation Structure (DRS)","aliases":["Discourse Representation Structure","DRS","DRSs","DRS's"],"definition":"A partially ordered family of DRs rather than a single DR: a principal DR for the whole text, plus pairs of subordinate DRs representing the parts of each conditional or universal sentence, which cannot be decomposed inside the DR that contains it.","exact":false},{"id":"t3","term":"proper embedding","aliases":["proper embeddings"],"definition":"Compatibility of M with m … can be defined as the existence of a proper embedding of m into M, where a proper embedding is a map from the universe of m into that of M which, roughly speaking, preserves all the [conditions] which m specifies of the elements of its domain.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"accessibility","aliases":["accessible","available"],"definition":"the discourse referents available to a given pronoun must all occur in the same, or else in some superordinate, DR.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"(1) If Pedro owns a donkey he beats it (2) Every farmer who owns a donkey beats it. … How does an expression of a type which standardly (or so it always seemed) conveys existence manage to express universality in a sentence such as (1)?","phenomenon":"donkey anaphora","aliases":["donkey"],"near":["bound-variable anaphora","Bach–Peters sentence","quantifier scope ambiguity"],"avoid":["E-type pronoun","E-type anaphora","unselective binding"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"(8) Pedro owns a donkey. He beats it.","phenomenon":"cross-sentential (discourse) anaphora","near":["deictic use of a pronoun","bound-variable anaphora","pronoun of laziness"],"avoid":["donkey anaphora","E-type pronoun","E-type anaphora"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"In (17) “If Pedro owns every donkey then he beats it.” ‘it’ cannot have ‘every donkey’ for its antecedent. … in (19) “If Pedro likes every woman who owns a donkey he feeds it” ‘it’ cannot be co-referential with ‘a donkey’, whereas such a link does seem possible in (20) “If Pedro likes a woman who owns a donkey he feeds it.”","phenomenon":"accessibility constraint on anaphora","near":["weak crossover","cataphora (backward anaphora)","Bach–Peters sentence"],"avoid":["donkey anaphora","E-type pronoun","scope island"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"In a head-on collision both father and son are critically wounded. They are rushed into hospital where the chief surgeon performes an emergency operation on the son. But it is too late and the boy dies on the operating table. When an assistant asks the surgeon, \"Could you have a look at the other victim?\", the surgeon replies \"I could not bear it. I have already lost my son.\"","phenomenon":"implicit gender bias (stereotyping)","near":["presupposition failure","lexical ambiguity","scalar implicature"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"heim1983-file-change-semantics","title":"File Change Semantics and the Familiarity Theory of Definiteness","authors":["Irene Heim"],"year":"1983","area":"language","topic":"dynamic semantics & anaphora","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"For every indefinite, start a new card. For every definite, update an old card.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"logical forms themselves are not assigned truth conditions, only files are. Only in an indirect way, i.e. via the files they affect, will logical forms be associated with truth conditions.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Indefinites are not existential quantifiers but open formulas introducing a new variable; their existential force comes from what it is for the discourse record to be true, which is why they can antecede pronouns outside their scope.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"F + p is defined only if each definite NPᵢ in atomic p has i ∈ Dom(F) and each indefinite NPᵢ has i ∉ Dom(F); a file F is true iff Sat(F) ≠ ∅, i.e. some sequence satisfies it.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"file change potential","aliases":[],"definition":"Each logical form is assigned a “file change potential”, i.e. a function from files into files. Given an utterance with a certain logical form, this function will determine how you get from the file that obtains prior to the utterance to the file that comes to obtain as a result of the utterance.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"Novelty/Familiarity Condition","aliases":[],"definition":"Let F be a file, p an atomic proposition. Then p is appropriate with respect to F only if, for every noun phrase NPi with index i that p contains: if NPi is definite, then i ∈ Dom(F), and if NPi is indefinite, then i ∉ Dom(F).","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"file","aliases":[],"definition":"to understand an utterance is to keep a file which, at every time in the course of the utterance, contains the information that has so far been conveyed by the utterance.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"file card","aliases":["file cards"],"definition":"I would like to suggest that Karttunen's talk about “discourse referents” be rephrased by substituting “file card” for “discourse referent”: once we realize that discourse referents are essentially like file cards, their identity criteria and their relation to referents come to look much less mysterious.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"truth of a file","aliases":[],"definition":"To establish the truth of a file, we have to, so to speak, line up the sequence of cards in the file with a sequence of actual individuals, such that each individual fits the description on the corresponding card. Or, as I will put it, we have to find a sequence of individuals that satisfies the file.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Every time a soldier accompanies us he shoots. … The problem is that the “he” in [this sentence] fails to refer, and that deixis without reference is a contradiction in terms","phenomenon":"donkey anaphora","near":["cross-sentential (discourse) anaphora","deictic (referential) pronoun","cataphora (backward anaphora)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"A soldier will accompany us. He will shoot.","phenomenon":"cross-sentential (discourse) anaphora","near":["donkey anaphora","quantificational subordination (telescoping)","cataphora (backward anaphora)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"(a) Everybody found a cat and kept it. (b) It ran away. The relevant facts here are that the “it” in (5a), but not the “it” in (5b), can be interpreted as anaphoric to “a cat”, (the intended reading being one with “everybody” taking wider scope than “a cat”).","phenomenon":"inaccessible discourse referent (scope-limited lifespan)","near":["weak crossover","quantificational subordination (telescoping)","disjoint reference (Principle B)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Every cat ate its food. … [It] has a reading where “its” … doesn't refer to any particular cat.","phenomenon":"bound-variable pronoun","near":["donkey anaphora","E-type pronoun","cross-sentential (discourse) anaphora"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1515/9783110852820.164"},{"id":"groenendijk1991-dynamic-predicate-logic","title":"Dynamic Predicate Logic","authors":["Jeroen Groenendijk","Martin Stokhof"],"year":"1991","area":"language","topic":"dynamic semantics & anaphora","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"A distinctive feature of [this system] is that it allows for existential quantifiers to bind variables which are outside their syntactic scope.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[This logic] allows us to translate an indefinite term uniformly as an existentially quantified phrase in situ … without any need of re-analysis.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Formulas denote relations between assignments: ⟦φ∧ψ⟧ = {⟨g,h⟩ | ∃k: ⟨g,k⟩∈⟦φ⟧ & ⟨k,h⟩∈⟦ψ⟧} and ⟦∃xφ⟧ = {⟨g,h⟩ | ∃k: k[x]g & ⟨k,h⟩∈⟦φ⟧}; hence ∃xPx ∧ Qx ≃ ∃x[Px ∧ Qx] and ∃xPx → Qx ≃ ∀x[Px → Qx].","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Keeping the ordinary syntax of first-order logic but taking meanings to be relations between variable assignments yields a compositional, representation-free account of discourse anaphora, empirically equivalent to Kamp's DRT, on which truth conditions do not exhaust meaning.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"internally dynamic","aliases":[],"definition":"Because of its power to pass on variable bindings from its left conjunct to the right one, we call conjunction an internally dynamic connective.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"externally static","aliases":[],"definition":"the universal quantifier shares with implication the characteristic of being externally static. Neither a universal quantifier itself, nor any existential quantifier inside its scope can bind variables outside the scope of that universal quantifier.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"test","aliases":["tests"],"definition":"various kinds of DPL-formulas have the characteristic that they do not pass on bindings created by expressions which occur in them. They function as a kind of ‘test’ in this sense that they examine whether an input assignment meets a certain condition, return it as output if it does, and reject it otherwise.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"dynamic entailment","aliases":["entails","entailment"],"definition":"We say that φ entails ψ if every successful execution of φ guarantees a succesful execution of ψ. Or, to put it slightly differently, φ entails ψ iff every assignment that is a possible output of φ is a possible input for ψ.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"mentalist","aliases":[],"definition":"Thus, a mentalist we call someone who claims that a level of representation is necessary, not someone who merely claims that it exists.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"If a farmer owns a donkey, he beats it … Every farmer who owns a donkey, beats it","phenomenon":"donkey anaphora","aliases":["donkey"],"near":["cross-sentential anaphora","paycheck pronoun","telescoping"],"avoid":["E-type pronoun","E-type anaphora","donkey sentence"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"A man walks in the park. He whistles","phenomenon":"cross-sentential anaphora","near":["donkey anaphora","telescoping","modal subordination"],"avoid":["discourse anaphora","E-type pronoun","E-type anaphora"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"I dropped ten marbles and found all of them, except for one. It is probably under the sofa. (26) I dropped ten marbles and found only nine of them. It is probably under the sofa.","phenomenon":"formal link requirement on anaphora","near":["donkey anaphora","scalar implicature","modal subordination"],"avoid":["cross-sentential anaphora","E-type pronoun","E-type anaphora","bridging","bridging inference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"If a client turns up, you treat him politely. You offer him a cup of coffee and ask him to wait","phenomenon":"modal subordination","near":["telescoping","bathroom sentence","paycheck pronoun"],"avoid":["cross-sentential anaphora","donkey anaphora","discourse anaphora"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"Every player chooses a pawn. He puts it on square one.","phenomenon":"telescoping","near":["donkey anaphora","bathroom sentence","paycheck pronoun"],"avoid":["cross-sentential anaphora","discourse anaphora"],"exact":true},{"id":"e6","text":"Either there is no bathroom here, or it is in a funny place. In any case, it is not on the first floor","phenomenon":"bathroom sentence","aliases":["bathroom"],"near":["donkey anaphora","telescoping","modal subordination"],"avoid":["cross-sentential anaphora","discourse anaphora","anaphora under double negation","double negation anaphora"],"exact":true},{"id":"e7","text":"If I’ve got a quarter in my pocket, I’ll put in the parking meter … one who utters [it], probably does not intend to spend all the quarters in his pocket on the parking meter.","phenomenon":"weak (existential) reading of donkey sentences","near":["strong (universal) reading of donkey sentences","proportion problem","quantificational variability effect"],"avoid":["donkey anaphora","donkey sentence"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00628304"},{"id":"barwise1981-generalized-quantifiers-natural","title":"Generalized Quantifiers and Natural Language","authors":["Jon Barwise","Robin Cooper"],"year":"1981","area":"language","topic":"quantification & formal semantics","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"“most” must be treated as a determiner, not as a quantifier. It is the NP “most people” that is the quantifier.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"It is a universal semantic feature of determiners that they assign to any set A a quantifier (i.e. family of sets) that lives on A.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Noun phrases — proper names included — denote families of sets, and determiners denote functions from noun denotations to such families; studying their model-theoretic properties yields testable universals about which determiners human languages can have.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"‘Det N VP’ is true iff ⟦VP⟧ ∈ ⟦Det⟧(⟦N⟧), where NPs denote families of sets — ⟦every⟧(A) = {X⊆E : A⊆X}, ⟦Harry⟧ = {X⊆E : h∈X} — and each D(A) lives on A: X∈D(A) ↔ X∩A ∈ D(A).","formal":true}],"pick":2,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"lives on","aliases":["live on","living on"],"definition":"a quantifier Q lives on a set A ⊆ E if Q is a set of subsets of E with the property that, for any X ⊆ E, X ∈ Q iff (X ∩ A) ∈ Q.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"sieve","aliases":["sieves","sifting","proper quantifier denotation","proper quantifier"],"definition":"We think of a quantifier Q … as separating (“sifting”) the VP denotations into those that do and those that do not combine with it to make a true sentence … We call Q a proper quantifier denotation or sieve if … Q is a non-empty proper subset of Pow(E).","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"strong determiner","aliases":["positive strong","negative strong","strong","weak","determiner"],"definition":"A determiner D is positive strong (or negative strong, resp.) if for every model M = (E, ‖ ‖) and every A ⊆ E, if the quantifier ‖D‖(A) is defined then A ∈ ‖D‖(A). (Or A ∉ ‖D‖(A), resp.). If D is not (positive or negative) strong then D is weak.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"witness set","aliases":["witness sets","witness"],"definition":"A witness set for a quantifier D(A) living on A is any subset w of A such that w ∈ D(A). … A witness set for ‖most women‖ is any set of women which contains most women.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"fixed context assumption","aliases":[],"definition":"The simplifying stipulation, held throughout, that a rich background is kept constant which settles the precise meaning of basic expressions — even vague determiners like ‘most’, ‘many’ and ‘few’ — thereby finessing their vagueness.","exact":false}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"More than half of John's arrows hit the target. More than half the people voted for Carter. Most of John's arrows hit the target. Most people voted for Carter.","phenomenon":"proportional quantifier (not first-order definable)","near":["cardinal (intersective) quantifier","generic generalization","branching (Henkin) quantifier"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Many men run ⇔ Many men are men who run … Few women sneeze ⇔ Few women are women who sneeze; John loves Mary ⇔ John is John and loves Mary. … The peculiarity of the sentences on the right hand side of the biconditionals is presumably due to the fact that they are obviously redundant.","phenomenon":"conservativity (the ‘lives on’ property)","near":["symmetry (the intersection condition)","upward monotonicity","persistence"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Take VP1 to be entered the race early and VP2 to be entered the race. The following are valid: If some Republican / every linguist / John / most peanut farmers / many men entered the race early, then some Republican / every linguist / John / most peanut farmers / many men entered the race. Notice that the reverse implications do not hold, since there clearly could be people who entered the race but did not enter early.","phenomenon":"upward monotonicity (monotone increasing NP)","near":["downward monotonicity","persistence (monotonicity in the noun argument)","conservativity"],"exact":false},{"id":"e4","text":"given the utterance (21) we could naturally assume the truth of (22). However, this assumption may be explicitly contradicted as in (23). (21) No boy at the party kissed Mary. … (22) There were boys at the party. (23) No boy at the party kissed Mary since there weren't any boys at the party. … the explicit denial in (24c) sounds quite odd. … c. ?Every man at the party kissed Mary, but only because there weren't any men at the party.","phenomenon":"existential import of quantified noun phrases","near":["scalar implicature","quantifier domain restriction","negative polarity licensing"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00350139"},{"id":"kratzer1977-must-can-must","title":"What ‘Must’ and ‘Can’ Must and Can Mean","authors":["Angelika Kratzer"],"year":"1977","area":"language","topic":"modality","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"relative modal phrases like ‘must in view of’ and ‘can in view of’ should be considered as the foundation of the modals ‘must’ and ‘can’, respectively.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"We have found a method to clean up an inconsistent set of propositions while still staying as close as possible to all that these propositions tell us.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Modal verbs are not ambiguous among deontic, epistemic, dispositional and preferential senses: each has one invariant relational meaning, taking an 'in view of' argument (explicit or supplied by context) plus a proposition.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"With f(w) a possibly inconsistent set of propositions and X its consistent subsets: □p holds at w iff ∀A∈X ∃B∈X (A⊆B ∧ ∩B⊆p); ◇p iff ∃A∈X ∀B∈X (A⊆B → ∩B∩p≠∅).","formal":true}],"pick":2,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"relative modal phrase","aliases":["relative"],"definition":"… not an absolute ‘must’ but a relative ‘must in view of’. This relative modal phrase ‘must in view of’ has two arguments: … ‘what is known’ or ‘what is good for us’ etc. and a sentence.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"meaning proper","aliases":[],"definition":"It is the task of semantics to describe all those features of the meaning of utterances of linguistic expressions which stay invariable in whatever context these expressions may be used. This invariable element we may call the meaning proper of a linguistic expression.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"You must and you can store must in a can. … The must which you can store in a can has nothing to do with necessity, and the can you can store your must in has nothing to do with possibility.","phenomenon":"homonymy (lexical ambiguity)","near":["polysemy","structural (syntactic) ambiguity","context-dependence of modal flavour"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"“I must sneeze.” “Don’t be silly. … Everyone knows how to prevent sneezing. You feel that something fuzzy is going on in your nose. You feel it a good time in advance. And you can suppress it. That’s all.” “But once I have missed the right moment, I cannot help sneezing any more. It just comes out. It is too late to suppress it. I simply must sneeze.”","phenomenon":"dispositional (circumstantial) modality","near":["deontic modality","epistemic modality","bouletic (preferential) modality"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Suppose a judge asks himself whether a murderer could have acted otherwise then he eventually did. … Professor Schielrecht’s answer was: Given the whole situation of the crime, which includes of course all the dispositions of the murderer, this man could not have acted otherwise than as he did. … He misunderstood the judge: what the judge probably meant was: Given such and such aspects of the situation, could the murderer have acted otherwise than he eventually did?","phenomenon":"context-supplied conversational background (modal base)","near":["modal scope ambiguity","homonymy (lexical ambiguity)","Frankfurt-style case"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"In Wellington a judgement was handed down which provided that deer are not personally responsible for damage they inflict on young trees. In Auckland a judgement was handed down which provided that deer are personally responsible … Although no New Zealand judge has ever doubted that murder is a crime, our semantic analysis forces us to suppose nevertheless that murder must be a crime and not a crime in view of the New Zealand judgements.","phenomenon":"explosion (ex falso quodlibet)","near":["Ross's paradox","contrary-to-duty (Chisholm's) paradox","preface paradox"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00353453"},{"id":"kripke1977-speakers-reference-semantic","title":"Speaker’s Reference and Semantic Reference","authors":["Saul Kripke"],"year":"1977","area":"language","topic":"descriptions & definiteness","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the problems Donnellan handles by semantic ambiguity should instead be treated by a general theory of speech acts.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Do not posit an ambiguity unless you are really forced to, unless there are really compelling theoretical or intuitive grounds to suppose that an ambiguity really is present.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Donnellan's referential uses don't refute Russell: the same phenomenon would arise among speakers of a language stipulated to have Russellian truth conditions, and economy favors explaining it pragmatically over positing a semantic ambiguity.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"What a speaker refers to, fixed by a specific intention on an occasion, can differ from a designator's conventional referent, fixed by a general intention; referential uses of descriptions, like mistaken uses of names, are cases where they may diverge.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"speaker's referent","aliases":["speaker's referent","speaker’s referent","speaker","referent"],"definition":"we may tentatively define the speaker's referent of a designator to be that object which the speaker wishes to talk about, on a given occasion, and believes fulfills the conditions for being the semantic referent of the designator.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"semantic referent","aliases":["semantic reference","referent"],"definition":"If a speaker has a designator in his idiolect, certain conventions of his idiolect (given various facts about the world) determine the referent in the idiolect: that I call the semantic referent of the designator.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"simple case","aliases":["simple","simply","simple use"],"definition":"[the speaker's] specific intention is simply to refer to the semantic referent: that is, his specific intention is simply his general semantic intention.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"complex case","aliases":["complex","complex use"],"definition":"[The speaker] has a specific intention, which is distinct from his general intention, but which he believes, as a matter of fact, to determine the same object as the one determined by his general intention. (For example, he wishes to refer to the man \"over there\" but believes that he is Jones.)","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"weak Russell language","aliases":["Russell","language"],"definition":"a language similar to English except that the truth conditions of sentences with definite descriptions are stipulated to coincide with Russell's … the semantic referent of a definite description is the unique object that satisfies the description, if any; otherwise there is no semantic referent.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"D-languages","aliases":["D-language"],"definition":"In the D-languages the apparent ambiguity between referential and attributive definite descriptions is explicitly built into the semantics of the language and affects truth conditions.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Two people see Smith in the distance and mistake him for Jones. They have a brief colloquy: \"What is Jones doing?\" \"Raking the leaves.\" \"Jones,\" in the common language of both, is a name of Jones; it never names Smith. Yet, in some sense, on this occasion, clearly both participants in the dialogue have referred to Smith, and the second participant has said something true about the man he referred to if and only if Smith was raking the leaves (whether or not Jones was).","phenomenon":"speaker's reference diverging from a name's semantic reference","near":["referential use of a definite description","reference change of a proper name","reference failure"],"avoid":["misidentification"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Someone sees a woman with a man. Taking the man to be her husband, and observing his attitude towards her, he says, \"Her husband is kind to her,\" and someone else may nod, \"Yes, he seems to be.\" Suppose the man in question is not her husband. Suppose he is her lover, to whom she has been driven precisely by her husband's cruelty.","phenomenon":"referential use of a definite description","near":["attributive use of a definite description","presupposition failure","narrow-scope (de dicto) reading of a description"],"avoid":["misidentification"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"(2) The number of planets might have been necessarily even. In a natural use, (2) can be interpreted as true: for example, there might have been exactly eight planets, in which case the number of planets would have been even, and hence necessarily even.","phenomenon":"intermediate scope reading of a description","near":["wide-scope (de re) reading","narrow-scope (de dicto) reading","rigid reading of a definite description"],"avoid":["quantifier scope ambiguity","de dicto vs de re reading"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"\"Exactly one person (or: some person or other) is drinking champagne in that corner, and I hear he is romantically linked with Jane Smith.\" The circumlocution, in English, expresses the delicacy of the topic, but the speaker's reference (in quite an ordinary sense) may well be clear, even if he in fact is drinking sparkling water.","phenomenon":"existential statement carrying a speaker's reference","aliases":["speaker's reference","speaker’s reference"],"near":["referential use of a definite description","attributive use of a definite description","quantifier domain restriction"],"avoid":["cross-sentential anaphora","specific indefinite","specific use of an indefinite description"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1111/j.1475-4975.1977.tb00045.x"},{"id":"kripke1979-puzzle-belief","title":"A Puzzle about Belief","authors":["Saul Kripke"],"year":"1979","area":"language","topic":"attitude reports & de se","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The absurdities that disquotation plus substitutivity would generate are exactly paralleled by absurdities generated by disquotation plus translation, or even ‘disquotation alone’ (or: disquotation plus homophonic translation).","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Does Pierre, or does he not, believe that London is pretty? … our normal criteria for the attribution of belief lead, when applied to this question, to paradoxes and contradictions.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Ordinary principles of belief ascription—disquotation (sincere reflective assent to 'p' shows belief that p) and truth-preserving translation—yield contradictory verdicts about a speaker who doesn't realize two names co-refer, with no appeal to substitutivity.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Cases like Cicero/Tully do not show that coreferential names fail to be intersubstitutable in belief contexts: the same absurdities arise without substitutivity, so such cases strain belief ascription to breaking and support no Fregean conclusion.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"disquotational principle","aliases":["disquotational","disquotation"],"definition":"where ‘p’ is to be replaced, inside and outside all quotation marks, by any appropriate standard English sentence: “If a normal English speaker, on reflection, sincerely assents to ‘p’, then he believes that p.”","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"strengthened (biconditional) disquotational principle","aliases":["disquotational","biconditional","strengthened"],"definition":"A normal English speaker who is not reticent will be disposed to sincere reflective assent to ‘p’ if and only if he believes that p.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"principle of translation","aliases":["translation"],"definition":"if a sentence of one language expresses a truth in that language, then any translation of it into any other language also expresses a truth (in that other language).","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"Shakespearean","aliases":["Shakespearean"],"definition":"Said of a context in which codesignative proper names are interchangeable salva veritate; weaker than referential transparency in Quine's sense, which also requires coreferential definite descriptions to be interchangeable — modal contexts, for instance, have the property while being referentially opaque.","exact":false}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose Pierre is a normal French speaker who lives in France and speaks not a word of English … On the basis of what he has heard of London, he is inclined to think that it is pretty. So he says, in French, “Londres est jolie.” … Later, Pierre … moves to England, in fact to London itself, though to an unattractive part of the city … So he is inclined to assent to the English sentence: (5) London is not pretty. … he does not for a moment withdraw his assent from the French sentence, “Londres est jolie”","phenomenon":"Kripke's puzzle about belief","near":["substitution failure in belief contexts","Quine's indeterminacy of translation","Mates's puzzle about synonym substitution"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Peter … may learn the name ‘Paderewski’ with an identification of the person named as a famous pianist. Naturally, having learned this, Peter will assent to “Paderewski had musical talent,” … Later, in a different circle, Peter learns of someone called ‘Paderewski’ who was a Polish nationalist leader and prime minister. Peter is skeptical of the musical abilities of politicians. He concludes that probably two people, approximate contemporaries no doubt, were both named ‘Paderewski’. Using ‘Paderewski’ as a name for the statesman, Peter assents to, “Paderewski had no musical talent.”","phenomenon":"Paderewski case (puzzle without translation)","aliases":["Paderewski"],"near":["substitution failure in belief contexts","Mates's puzzle about synonym substitution","reference change (Madagascar case)"],"avoid":["Kripke's puzzle about belief"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Tom, a normal speaker of the language, may sincerely assent to “Tully denounced Catiline,” but not to “Cicero denounced Catiline.” He may even deny the latter. And his denial is compatible with his status as a normal English speaker who satisfies normal criteria for using both ‘Cicero’ and ‘Tully’ as names for the famed Roman (without knowing that ‘Cicero’ and ‘Tully’ name the same person).","phenomenon":"substitution failure in belief contexts","near":["substitution failure in modal contexts","Mates's puzzle about synonym substitution","the essential indexical"],"avoid":["Kripke's puzzle about belief","Frege's puzzle about identity statements"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"there are no counterfactual situations in which Hesperus and Phosphorus would have been different. Admittedly, the truth of ‘Hesperus is Phosphorus’ was not known a priori, and may even have been widely disbelieved before appropriate empirical evidence came in.","phenomenon":"necessary a posteriori truth","aliases":["truth"],"near":["contingent a priori truth","analytic truth","substitution failure in modal contexts"],"avoid":["Frege's puzzle about identity statements"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/978-1-4020-4104-4_13"},{"id":"evans1973-causal-theory-names","title":"The Causal Theory of Names","authors":["Gareth Evans"],"year":"1973","area":"language","topic":"reference & names","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I think we can say that in general a speaker intends to refer to the item that is the dominant source of his associated body of information.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the important causal relation lies between [the referent's] states and doings and the speaker's body of information—not between the item's being dubbed with a name and the speaker's contemporary use","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Names can change their bearers, as 'Madagascar' did, which a pure dubbing-and-transmission chain cannot allow; what fixes a name's referent is the dominant causal source of the information its users associate with it, not descriptive fit.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"'NN' names x if, in some community C, it is common knowledge that members use 'NN' to refer to x, success in reference being intended to rely on this, not on x's satisfying a predicate embedded in 'NN'.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"source (of a belief)","aliases":["source"],"definition":"X is the source of the belief S expresses by uttering 'Fa' if there was an episode which caused S's belief in which X and S were causally related in a type of situation apt for producing knowledge that something F-s","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"dominant source","aliases":["dominance","dominant","dominantly"],"definition":"Dominance is not simply a function of amount of information … each man's life presents a skeleton and the dominant source may be the man who contributed to covering most of it rather than the man who contributed most of the covering.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"deferential use","aliases":["deferentially","deferential"],"definition":"sometimes we use them with the over-riding intention to conform to the use made of them by some other person or persons. In that case I shall say that we use the expression deferentially (with respect to that other person or group of persons).","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"mouthpiece syndrome","aliases":["mouthpiece"],"definition":"what I call the 'mouthpiece syndrome' by which we attach sense and reference to a man's remarks only because we hear someone else speaking through him; as we might with a messenger, carrying a message about matters of which he was entirely ignorant.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"what the speaker denotes","aliases":["denotes"],"definition":"what the name denotes upon a particular occasion of its use when this is understood as being partly determinative of what the speaker strictly and literally said. I shall use the faintly barbarous coinage: what the speaker denotes (upon an occasion) for this notion.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"In the case of 'Madagascar' a hearsay report of Malay or Arab sailors misunderstood by Marco Polo . . . has had the effect of transferring a corrupt form of the name of a portion of the African mainland to the great African Island.","phenomenon":"reference change of a proper name","near":["reference failure","reference borrowing","descriptive name"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Archaeologists might find a tomb in the desert and claim falsely that it is the burial place of some little known character in the Bible. They could discover a great deal about the man in the tomb so that he and not the character in the Bible was the dominant source of their information. But, given the nature and point of their enterprise, the archaeologists are using the name deferentially to the authors of the Bible.","phenomenon":"deferential use of a name","aliases":["deferentially"],"near":["reference change of a proper name","reference fixed by dominant source","mouthpiece case"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"I may form the belief about the wife of some colleague that she has nice legs upon the basis of seeing someone else—but the girl I saw is the source.","phenomenon":"misidentification","near":["confusion of two individuals","hallucination","reference change of a proper name"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"we may deduce that there is a tallest man in the world and deduce that he is over 6 feet tall. No man is the source of this information; a name introduced in relation to it might function very much as the unamended Description Theory suggested.","phenomenon":"descriptive name","aliases":["Description Theory"],"near":["empty name","reference borrowing","deferential use of a name"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"Suppose for example on a T.V. quiz programme I am asked to name a capital city and I say 'Kingston is the capital of Jamaica'; I should want to say that I had said something strictly and literally true even though it turns out that the man from whom I had picked up this scrap of information was actually referring to Kingston-upon-Thames and making a racist observation.","phenomenon":"contextual disambiguation of a shared name","near":["reference borrowing","deferential use of a name","mouthpiece case"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1093/aristoteliansupp/47.1.187"},{"id":"lewis1979-attitudes-de-dicto","title":"Attitudes De Dicto and De Se","authors":["David Lewis"],"year":"1979","area":"language","topic":"attitude reports & de se","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Some belief and some knowledge cannot be understood as propositional, but can be understood as self-ascription of properties.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"To believe a proposition is to self-ascribe the corresponding property … the property that belongs to all and only the inhabitants of a certain region of logical space.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Take propositions as sets of worlds and properties as sets of possible individuals: believing P is self-ascribing {x : x's world ∈ P}, and some beliefs self-ascribe properties that correspond to no proposition.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Two gods who know every proposition true at their world but not which of them each is show that some knowledge is not propositional; so the uniform objects of all attitudes should be properties, not sets of worlds.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"belief de se","aliases":["de se","knowledge de se"],"definition":"Self-ascription of properties might suitably be called belief or knowledge de se. My thesis is that the de se subsumes the de dicto, but not vice versa.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"self-locating belief","aliases":["self-locating"],"definition":"I say that all belief is \"self-locating belief.\" Belief de dicto is self-locating belief with respect to logical space; belief irreducibly de se is self-locating belief at least partly with respect to ordinary time and space, or with respect to the population.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"relation of acquaintance","aliases":["acquaintance","related"],"definition":"in each case, I and the one of whom I have beliefs de re are so related that there is an extensive causal dependence of my states upon his; and this causal dependence is of a sort apt for the reliable transmission of information.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"belief de re","aliases":["de re","beliefs de re"],"definition":"A subject ascribes property X to individual Y if and only if the subject ascribes X to Y under some description Z such that either (1) Z captures the essence of Y, or (2) Z is a relation of acquaintance that the subject bears to Y.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"property","aliases":["properties"],"definition":"The word \"property\" also is used in many senses. I mean a set: the set of exactly those possible beings, actual or not, that have the property in question.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Consider the case of the two gods. They inhabit a certain possible world, and they know exactly which world it is. Therefore they know every proposition that is true at their world. Insofar as knowledge is a propositional attitude, they are omniscient. Still I can imagine them to suffer ignorance: neither one knows which of the two he is. … Neither one knows whether he lives on the tallest mountain or on the coldest mountain; nor whether he throws manna or thunderbolts.","phenomenon":"irreducibly de se ignorance","aliases":["ignorance"],"near":["temporal self-locating ignorance","logical omniscience problem","knowledge argument (Mary's room)"],"avoid":["self-locating belief","self-locating knowledge","essential indexical","de se belief","de se knowledge"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"[Heimson] believes he is Hume. Hume believed that too. Hume was right. If Hume believed he was Hume by believing a proposition, that proposition was true. Heimson believes just what Hume did. But Hume and Heimson are worldmates. Any proposition true for Hume is likewise true for Heimson. So Heimson, like Hume, believes he is Hume by believing a true proposition. So he's right. But he's not right. He's wrong, because he believes he's Hume and he isn't.","phenomenon":"false de se belief","near":["belief in a necessary falsehood","de re (not de se) belief about oneself","Moore's paradox"],"avoid":["de se belief","self-locating belief","essential indexical","same character, different content"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Consider the insomniac. Tonight, as most nights, he lies awake for hours. While he lies awake, his state of mind changes little. He does not keep track of the time. So all through the night he wonders what time it is. To wonder is to lack knowledge, but what relevant knowledge does he lack? Not propositional knowledge; he knows, well enough, what sort of world is his. And not self-ascription of properties to his continuant self; he knows, well enough, what locus through space and time he occupies and what his various segments are like.","phenomenon":"temporal self-locating ignorance","near":["problem of temporary intrinsics","knowledge argument (Mary's room)","logical omniscience problem"],"avoid":["irreducibly de se ignorance","self-locating belief","self-locating knowledge","essential indexical","de se belief","cognitive dynamics: losing track of time"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"I watch myself in reflecting glass, unaware that I am watching myself. I ascribe to myself, under the description \"the one I am watching,\" the property of wearing pants that are on fire. … But I do not self-ascribe the property of wearing pants that are on fire. Very soon I will, but not yet.","phenomenon":"de re (not de se) belief about oneself","near":["de se belief","de dicto belief","self-deception"],"avoid":["essential indexical","Frege's puzzle","Frege's puzzle about informative identity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"Consider Lingens when he knows almost enough to get out. He has narrowed the possibilities down to two. Perhaps he is in aisle five, floor six, of Main Library, Stanford, in which case the way out is to go downstairs. Or perhaps he is on one of the lower floors in the stacks of Widener, in which case the thing to do is to go up. The books tell him that there are amnesiacs lost in both places, and he has figured out that he is one of the two. His deliberation concerns eight alternative possibilities.","phenomenon":"decision under self-locating uncertainty","near":["Newcomb's problem","Buridan's ass","Ellsberg paradox"],"avoid":["self-locating belief","self-locating knowledge","irreducibly de se ignorance","essential indexical","de se belief"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2184843"},{"id":"perry1977-frege-demonstratives","title":"Frege on Demonstratives","authors":["John Perry"],"year":"1977","area":"language","topic":"indexicals & demonstratives","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Though senses considered as roles, and thoughts considered as information, cannot be identified, each does its job in a way that meshes with the other.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"To have a thought we need an object and an incomplete sense. The demonstrative in context gives us the one, the rest of the sentence the other.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Indexicals force a split between a sentence's sense and the thought it expresses: context supplies only an object, not a completing sense, and no associated or incommunicable sense can fill the gap.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"An indexical sentence's sense is a role: S(d) uttered in context c is true iff the value of d in c falls under the concept S( ) refers to; the thought is ⟨value, incomplete sense⟩.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"avoid":["perry1979-problem-essential-indexical"],"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"role","aliases":["roles"],"definition":"When we understand a word like “today,” what we seem to know is a rule taking us from an occasion of utterance to a certain object. “Today” takes us to the very day of utterance, … “I” to the speaker, and so forth. I shall call this the role of the demonstrative.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"informationally equivalent","aliases":["informational equivalent","informational equivalents","informationally equivalent thoughts"],"definition":"just those thoughts obtainable by completing the sense of “Russia and Canada quarreled” with a sense completer that determines, as reference, August 1, 1976. I shall call thoughts related in this manner informationally equivalent.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"irrelevancy of belief","aliases":[],"definition":"[The sense] I associate with my use of a demonstrative does not determine the thought expressed by a sentence containing that demonstrative.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"nonnecessity of belief","aliases":[],"definition":"I can express a thought with “Today is sunny and bright”—that is, say something for which the question of truth arises—whether or not I associate any correct sense at all with “today.” I may have no idea at all what day it is","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"nonsufficiency of belief","aliases":["sufficient"],"definition":"So with “now,” as with “I,” it is not sufficient to grasp the thought expressed with a demonstrative, to grasp an informational equivalent with a complete sense.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose I am viewing the harbor from downtown Oakland; the bow and stern of the aircraft carrier Enterprise are visible, though its middle is obscured by a large building. The name “Enterprise” is clearly visible on the bow, so when I tell a visitor, “This is the Enterprise,” pointing towards the bow, this is readily accepted. When I say, pointing to the stern clearly several city blocks from the bow, “That is the Enterprise,” however, she refuses to believe me.","phenomenon":"Frege's puzzle about informative identity","near":["referential use of a definite description","reference failure","de re/de dicto ambiguity"],"avoid":["cognitive significance","same content, different character","informative identity statement"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Let us imagine David Hume, alone in his study, on a particular afternoon in 1775, thinking to himself, “I wrote the Treatise.” … Now suppose Heimson is a bit crazy and thinks himself to be David Hume. Alone in his study, he says to himself, “I wrote the Treatise.” However much his inner life may, at that moment, resemble Hume’s on that afternoon in 1775, the fact remains: Hume was right, Heimson is wrong.","phenomenon":"same character, different content","near":["same content, different character","Frege's puzzle","reference failure"],"avoid":["same role, different thought","de se belief","self-locating belief","essential indexical"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"An amnesiac, Rudolf Lingens, is lost in the Stanford library. He reads a number of things in the library, including a biography of himself, and a detailed account of the library in which he is lost. He believes any Fregean thought you think might help him. He still will not know who he is and where he is, and no matter how much knowledge he piles up, until that moment when he is ready to say, This place is aisle five, floor six, of Main Library, Stanford. I am Rudolf Lingens.","phenomenon":"self-locating knowledge","near":["Mary's room (knowledge argument)","Gettier case","brain-in-a-vat scenario"],"avoid":["self-locating belief","locating belief","de se belief","de se knowledge","essential indexical"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"When you and I entertain the sense of “A bear is about to attack me,” we behave similarly. We both roll up in a ball and try to be as still as possible. … When you and I both apprehend the thought that I am about to be attacked by a bear, we behave differently. I roll up in a ball, you run to get help.","phenomenon":"essential indexical","near":["immunity to error through misidentification","Moore's paradox","weakness of will"],"avoid":["de se belief","self-locating belief","self-locating knowledge","locating belief","cognitive significance"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2184564"},{"id":"grice1956-defense-dogma","title":"In Defense of a Dogma","authors":["H. P. Grice","P. F. Strawson"],"year":"1956","area":"language","topic":"analyticity & holism","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"if we are to give up the notion of sentence-synonymy as senseless, we must give up the notion of sentence-significance (of a sentence having meaning) as senseless too.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"If we can make sense of [conceptual revision], then we can perfectly well preserve [the analytic-synthetic distinction], while conceding to Quine the revisability-in-principle of everything we say.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"That 'analytic' and its kin lack non-circular strict definitions cannot show the distinction illusory: agreed application to an open class of cases creates a presumption that it exists, and informal explanations (not believing vs. not understanding) escape the circle.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Holism and universal revisability leave analyticity intact: giving up a sentence by admitting falsity differs from giving it up through a shift in its words' sense, and confirmational synonymy can be relativized to background assumptions.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"family circle","aliases":["circle","family","family-circle"],"definition":"There is a certain circle or family of expressions, of which \"analytic\" is one, such that if any one member of the circle could be taken to be satisfactorily understood or explained, then other members of the circle could be verbally, and hence satisfactorily, explained in terms of it.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"satisfactory explanation","aliases":[],"definition":"Quine requires of a satisfactory explanation of an expression that it should take the form of a pretty strict definition but should not make use of any member of a group of interdefinable terms to which the expression belongs.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"sentence-synonymy","aliases":["synonymous"],"definition":"Two sentences are synonymous if and only if any true answer to the question \"What does it mean?\" asked of one of them, is a true answer to the same question, asked of the other.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"conceptual revision","aliases":["concept","concepts"],"definition":"that kind of giving up which involves changing or dropping a concept or set of concepts. … Where such a shift in the sense of the words is a necessary condition of the change in truth-value, … the form of words in question changes from expressing an analytic statement to expressing a synthetic statement.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"(1) \"My neighbor's three-year-old child understands Russell's Theory of Types,\" … (4) \"I don't believe you—the thing's impossible.\" But if the child were then produced, and did (as one knows he would not) expound the theory correctly, answer questions on it, criticize it, and so on, one would in the end be forced to acknowledge that the claim was literally true and that the child was a prodigy.","phenomenon":"natural (causal) impossibility","near":["logical impossibility","metaphysical impossibility","category mistake"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"(1') \"My neighbor's three-year-old child is an adult.\" … (3') \"No, I mean what I say,\" … (5') \"No, he's not a freak, he's just an adult.\" At this stage—or possibly if we are patient, a little later—we shall be inclined to say that we just don't understand what Y is saying, and to suspect that he just does not know the meaning of some of the words he is using.","phenomenon":"logical impossibility","near":["natural (causal) impossibility","hyperbole","presupposition failure"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"I do not know whether the statement \"Everything green is extended\" is analytic. … The indecision over \"analytic\" (and equally, in this case, the indecision over \"true\") arises, of course, from a further indecision: viz., that which we feel when confronted with such questions as \"Should we count a point of green light as extended or not?\"","phenomenon":"open texture (indeterminate word boundaries)","near":["indeterminacy of translation","conceptual revision","confirmation holism"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"\"bachelor\" means the same as \"unmarried man\" but that \"creature with kidneys\" does not mean the same as \"creature with a heart\"—supposing the last two expressions to be coextensional","phenomenon":"coextensive but non-synonymous predicates","aliases":["coextensional"],"near":["referential opacity","open texture","rigid designation"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2182828"},{"id":"carnap1952-meaning-postulates","title":"Meaning Postulates","authors":["Rudolf Carnap"],"year":"1952","area":"language","topic":"analyticity & holism","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Si is L-true in L' = Df Si is L-implied by P in L. L-truth in L' is then the explicatum for analyticity.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[System builders] are free to choose their postulates, guided not by their beliefs concerning facts of the world but by their intentions with respect to the meanings","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"With P the conjunction of the postulates adopted for a language L, a sentence S is analytic (L-true with respect to P) iff P L-implies S, i.e. iff P → S is L-true.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Rejecting Quine's skepticism, analytic non-logical truths like 'no bachelor is married' are secured by stipulated postulates that rule out impossible state-descriptions; confirmation in the enriched language equals confirmation with the postulates added to the evidence.","formal":false}],"pick":2,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"meaning postulate","aliases":[],"definition":"a new semantical concept (either 'directly L-true,' i.e., 'meaning postulate,' or 'directly L-false' in an alternative procedure), defined by enumeration in each semantical system or taken as primitive in general semantics.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"L-true with respect to P","aliases":[],"definition":"A statement Si in L is L-true with respect to P = Df Si is L-implied by P (in L).","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"state-description","aliases":[],"definition":"A state-description is a conjunction containing for every atomic statement either it or its negation but not both, and no other statements.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"'If Jack is a bachelor, then he is not married' … it is sufficient to understand the statement in order to establish its truth; knowledge of (extra-linguistic) facts is not involved. … [Here the] meanings of some descriptive words are involved, viz., those of 'bachelor' and 'married.'","phenomenon":"analytic but not logical truth","near":["logical truth","synthetic a priori truth","contingent empirical truth"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"let 'W' be a primitive predicate designating the relation Warmer. Then 'W' is transitive, irreflexive, and hence asymmetric in virtue of its meaning. Therefore the statements 'Wab · Wbc · ~Wac,' 'Wab · Wba,' and 'Waa' are false due to their meanings. The same holds for state-descriptions which contain one of these statements as subconjunctions; hence they do not represent possible cases.","phenomenon":"logical dependence among atomic statements","near":["logical independence of atomic statements","synonymy of primitive predicates","category mistake"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"While the meaning of 'black' is fairly clear, that of 'raven' is rather vague in the everyday language. There is no point for him to make an elaborate study, based either on introspection or on statistical investigation of common usage, in order to find out whether 'raven' always or mostly entails 'black.' It is rather his task to make up his mind whether he wishes the predicates 'R' and 'Bl' of his system to be used in such a way that the first logically entails the second.","phenomenon":"conventionalism about analyticity","near":["confirmation holism","a posteriori necessity","semantic externalism"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf02350366"},{"id":"dummett1959-truth","title":"Truth","authors":["Michael Dummett"],"year":"1959","area":"language","topic":"theory of meaning & truth conditions","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"We are entitled to say that a statement P must be either true or false … only when P is … an effectively decidable statement.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The sense of a sentence is determined wholly by knowing the case in which it has a designated value and the cases in which it has an undesignated one.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Like winning a game, truth has a point not captured by classifying sentences as true or false: we aim at true assertions. Truth-conditions explain meaning only given an account of assertion, which the redundancy theory cannot supply.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Extending intuitionism beyond mathematics, meaning is explained by assertibility: a compound may be asserted under conditions fixed by when its constituents may be asserted. Excluded middle then fails for undecidable statements, with no third truth-value.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"realism","aliases":["realist","realists"],"definition":"Realism consists in the belief that for any statement there must be something in virtue of which either it or its negation is true","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"effectively decidable statement","aliases":["effectively decidable","decidable"],"definition":"P is a statement of such a kind that we could in a finite time bring ourselves into a position in which we were justified either in asserting or in denying P","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"designated value","aliases":["designated","undesignated","designated values","undesignated values"],"definition":"A statement has a designated value in a case if someone who asserts it is not taken as excluding that case, an undesignated one if he is; knowing which cases are which wholly determines its sense.","exact":false},{"id":"t4","term":"redundancy theory","aliases":["redundancy theory of truth"],"definition":"The theory that 'It is true that p if and only if p' and 'It is false that p if and only if not p' give the whole meaning of 'true' and 'false'.","exact":false},{"id":"t5","term":"bounded language","aliases":["bounded","language"],"definition":"A language is bounded if we may not introduce into it new words or new senses for old words.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"let us envisage a dispute over the logical validity of the statement ‘Either Jones was brave or he was not’. A imagines Jones to be a man, now dead, who never encountered danger in his life. B retorts that it could still be true that Jones was brave, namely if it is true that if Jones had encountered danger, he would have acted bravely.","phenomenon":"undecidable concealed conditional","near":["truth-value gap from reference failure","borderline case of a vague predicate","future contingent"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Consider the statement ‘A city will never be built on this spot’. Even if we have an oracle which can answer every question of the kind, ‘Will there be a city here in 1990?’, ‘In 2100?’, etc., we might never be in a position either to declare the statement true or to declare it false.","phenomenon":"undecidable statement of unlimited generality","near":["undecidable concealed conditional","truth-value gap from reference failure","borderline case of a vague predicate"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"If P turns out true, then ‘If P, then Q’ is said to be true or false according as Q is true or false, while if P turns out false we say that nothing was said true or false.","phenomenon":"conditional assertion","near":["material conditional","strict conditional","counterfactual conditional"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"It is part of the concept of winning a game that a player plays to win, and this part of the concept is not conveyed by a classification of the end positions into winning ones and losing ones. We can imagine a variant of chess in which it is the object of each player to be checkmated, and this would be an entirely different game; but the formal description we imagined would coincide with the formal description of chess.","phenomenon":"truth as the aim of assertion","near":["knowledge norm of assertion","correspondence theory of truth","redundancy theory of truth"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"searle1958-proper-names","title":"Proper Names","authors":["John Searle"],"year":"1958","area":"language","topic":"reference & names","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"referring uses of \"Aristotle\" presuppose the existence of an object of whom a sufficient but so far unspecified number of [the descriptive statements its users accept] are true","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the looseness of the criteria for proper names is a necessary condition for isolating the referring function from the describing function of language","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Referring with a name presupposes, without asserting, that its bearer satisfies a sufficient but unspecified number of the identifying descriptions speakers associate with it; which ones, and how many, is not settled in advance.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Because each name carries a loose cluster of descriptive presuppositions, 'Aristotle never existed' denies that enough of them hold of anything, and 'Tully = Cicero' is analytic when both names share their presuppositions, synthetic when they differ.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"proper name","aliases":["proper names","proper name"],"definition":"Unlike demonstratives, a proper name refers without presupposing any stage settings or any special contextual conditions surrounding the utterance of the expression. Unlike definite descriptions, they do not in general specify any characteristics at all of the objects to which they refer.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"conventional presupposition (of a name)","aliases":["presuppose","presupposed","presupposition","presuppositions","conventional presuppositions"],"definition":"To use a proper name referringly is to presuppose the truth of certain uniquely referring descriptive statements, but it is not ordinarily to assert these statements or even to indicate which exactly are presupposed.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"degenerate proper name","aliases":["degenerate proper names","degenerate"],"definition":"degenerate proper names like \"The Bank of England\". For these latter, it seems the sense is given as straightforwardly as in a definite description; the presuppositions, as it were, rise to the surface.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"analytic statement","aliases":["analytic","analytically"],"definition":"A statement is analytic if and only if it is true in virtue of linguistic rules alone, without any recourse to empirical investigation.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"(a) \"Tully = Tully\" is analytic. But is (b) \"Tully = Cicero\" synthetic? If so, then each name must have a different sense, which seems at first sight most implausible, for we do not ordinarily think of proper names as having a sense at all in the way that predicates do; we do not, e.g. give definitions of proper names. But of course (b) gives us information not conveyed by (a).","phenomenon":"Frege's puzzle about identity statements","near":["Kripke's puzzle about belief (Pierre)","substitution failure in attitude reports","Paderewski case (one name, two bearers)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Suppose, for example, that we teach the name 'Aristotle' by explaining that it refers to a Greek philosopher born in Stagira, and suppose that our student continues to use the name correctly, that he gathers more information about Aristotle, and so on. Let us suppose it is discovered later on that Aristotle was not born in Stagira at all, but in Thebes. We will not now say that the meaning of the name has changed, or that Aristotle did not really exist at all.","phenomenon":"argument from error against descriptivism","near":["reference change (Madagascar case)","modal argument against descriptivism","argument from ignorance (Feynman case)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"if anyone did say that Aristotle did not exist he might mean much more than simply that the name does not denote anyone. If, for example, we challenged his statement by pointing out that a man named \"Aristotle\" lived in Hoboken in 1903, he would not regard this as a relevant countercharge. We say of Cerberus and Zeus that neither of them ever existed, without meaning that no object ever bore these names","phenomenon":"negative existential statements","aliases":["exist","existed","existence"],"near":["metalinguistic negation","de re/de dicto ambiguity","referential use of a definite description"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"suppose we agree to drop \"Aristotle\" and use, say, \"the teacher of Alexander\", then it is a necessary truth that the man referred to is Alexander's teacher—but it is a contingent fact that Aristotle ever went into pedagogy","phenomenon":"modal argument against descriptivism","near":["argument from error (Gödel/Schmidt case)","epistemic argument against descriptivism","Frege's puzzle about identity statements"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1093/mind/lxvii.266.166"},{"id":"strawson1964-intention-convention-speech","title":"Intention and Convention in Speech Acts","authors":["P. F. Strawson"],"year":"1964","area":"language","topic":"speech acts & assertion","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Some illocutionary acts are conventional; others are not (except in so far as they are locutionary acts).","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the understanding of the force of an utterance in all cases involves recognizing … an audience-directed intention and recognizing it as wholly overt, as intended to be recognized.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"S non-naturally means something by x only if S intends (i₁) to produce response r in A, (i₂) that A recognize i₁, (i₃) that this recognition be A's reason for r, and (i₄) that A recognize i₂.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Illocutionary force is conventional only for acts within rule-governed procedures, like verdicts, umpiring or redoubling; warnings, entreaties and objections need no convention beyond meaning, their force resting on a wholly overt audience-directed intention.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"uptake","aliases":[],"definition":"The audience's understanding of an utterance's meaning and force, tentatively analyzed as its taking the speaker to have the complex intention that it recognize, and recognize as intended to be recognized, his intention to produce a response.","exact":false},{"id":"t2","term":"essential avowability","aliases":["essential"],"definition":"An essential feature of the intentions which make up the illocutionary complex is their overtness. They have, one might say, essential avowability.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"essentially conventional illocutionary act","aliases":["essentially","convention"],"definition":"marrying, redoubling, giving out, pronouncing sentence, bringing in a verdict. Such acts could have no existence outside the rule- or convention-governed practices and procedures of which they essentially form parts.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"explicit performative formula","aliases":["first-person performative verb","explicit"],"definition":"the first-person performative verb … serves not exactly to ascribe an intention to the speaker but rather, in Austin's phrase, to make explicit the type of communication intention with which the speaker speaks, the type of force which the utterance has.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"to utter the words \"The ice over there is very thin\" to a skater is to issue a warning (is to say something with the force of a warning)","phenomenon":"non-conventional (communicative) illocutionary act","near":["conventional (institutional) illocutionary act","perlocutionary act","explicit performative utterance"],"avoid":["non-natural meaning"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"S intends by a certain action to induce in A the belief that p … He arranges convincing-looking \"evidence\" that p … He does this, knowing that A is watching him at work, but knowing also that A does not know that S knows that A is watching him at work. He realizes that A will not take the arranged \"evidence\" as genuine or natural evidence that p, but realizes, and indeed intends, that A will take his arranging of it as grounds for thinking that he, S, intends to induce in A the belief that p.","phenomenon":"sneaky intention (counterexample to Gricean meaning)","near":["non-natural meaning","deliberately and openly letting someone know","natural meaning"],"avoid":["covert intention to induce belief"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"the fact that the word \"guilty\" is pronounced by the foreman of the jury in court at the proper moment constitutes his utterance as the act of bringing in a verdict … if the appropriate umpire pronounces a batsman \"out,\" he thereby performs the act of giving the man out, which no player or spectator shouting \"Out!\" can do.","phenomenon":"conventional (institutional) illocutionary act","near":["non-conventional (communicative) illocutionary act","perlocutionary act","indirect speech act"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"A player might let slip the word \"redouble\" without meaning to redouble; but if the circumstances are appropriate and the play strict, then he has redoubled (or he may be held to have redoubled).","phenomenon":"unintentional illocutionary act (forms taking charge)","near":["misfire (void performative)","abuse (insincere performative)","etiolated (parasitic) use of language"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2183301"},{"id":"lewis1973-languages-language","title":"Languages and Language","authors":["David Lewis"],"year":"1973/1975","area":"language","topic":"interpretation & convention","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"a language ℒ is used by a population P if and only if there prevails in P a convention of truthfulness and trust in ℒ","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[T]he primary sort of conventional coordination in language use [is] that between the action of the truthful speaker and the responsive believing of his trusting hearer.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Languages are functions from strings to sets of possible worlds (σ is true in ℒ at w iff w ∈ ℒ(σ)); a population uses ℒ iff it has a convention of truthfulness and trust in ℒ.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Facts about a population's beliefs and desires objectively fix which languages—whole-sentence truth conditions—it uses, but not which grammar; sub-sentential meaning is only as objective as our standards for the best grammar.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"convention","aliases":["conventions"],"definition":"Conventions are regularities in action, or in action and belief, which are arbitrary but perpetuate themselves because they serve some sort of common interest.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"truthfulness in a language","aliases":["truthful","truthfulness"],"definition":"To be truthful in ℒ is to act in a certain way: to try never to utter any sentences of ℒ that are not true in ℒ. Thus it is to avoid uttering any sentence of ℒ unless one believes it to be true in ℒ.","exact":false},{"id":"t3","term":"trust in a language","aliases":["trusting","trust"],"definition":"To be trusting in ℒ is to form beliefs … to impute truthfulness in ℒ to others, and thus to tend to respond to another's utterance of any sentence of ℒ by coming to believe that the uttered sentence is true in ℒ.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"language","aliases":["languages"],"definition":"Something which assigns meanings to certain strings of types of sounds or of marks. It could therefore be a function, a set of ordered pairs of strings and meanings.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"serious communication situation","aliases":["serious communication","communication situation"],"definition":"a serious communication situation exists with respect to a sentence σ of ℒ whenever it is true, and common knowledge between a speaker and a hearer, that (a) the speaker does, and the hearer does not, know whether σ is true in ℒ; (b) the hearer wants to know; (c) the speaker wants the hearer to know …","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"What is the meaning of the sentence \"Mik Karthee was wise\" in the language of our 137th-century descendants, if all we can find in any of their minds is the inadequate dictionary entry: \"Mik Karthee: controversial American politician of the early atomic age\"? It depends, we might think, partly on which man stands at the beginning of the long causal chain ending in that inadequate dictionary entry.","phenomenon":"causal-historical determination of reference","aliases":["causal chain","causal"],"near":["descriptivist reference-fixing","referential use of a definite description","inscrutability of reference"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"We should regard with suspicion any method that purports to settle objectively whether, in some tribe, \"gavagai\" is true of temporally continuant rabbits or time-slices thereof. You can give their language a good grammar of either kind - and that's that.","phenomenon":"indeterminacy (inscrutability) of reference","aliases":["indeterminacy","inscrutability"],"near":["lexical ambiguity","unsharp analyticity","rule-following skepticism"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"A man isolated all his life from others might begin - through genius or a miracle - to use language, say to keep a diary.","phenomenon":"accidentally private language","aliases":["language"],"near":["necessarily private language","language of thought (Mentalese)","radical interpretation"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"If I utter a sentence σ of our language ℒ, you … will conclude that I take σ to be true in ℒ. … But you will draw other conclusions as well, based on your legitimate assumption that it is for some good reason that I chose to utter σ rather than remain silent, and rather than utter any of the other sentences of ℒ that I also take to be true in ℒ. I can communicate all sorts of misinformation by exploiting your beliefs about my conversational purposes, without ever being untruthful in ℒ.","phenomenon":"conversational implicature","aliases":["conversational"],"near":["conventional implicature","presupposition accommodation","lying"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"grice1969-utterers-meaning-intentions","title":"Utterer’s Meaning and Intentions","authors":["H. P. Grice"],"year":"1969","area":"language","topic":"speaker meaning & intention","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"why not make the direct intended effect not that A should think that p, but that A should think that U thinks that p?","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"eliminate potential counterexamples not by requiring U to have certain additional (\"backward-looking\") intentions, but rather by requiring U not to have a certain sort of intention or complex of intentions.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"U meant something by x ↔ U intended A to produce r, to recognize this intention, and to produce r on that basis; ¬∃ inference-element E: U intends A to rely on E yet think U intends A not to.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Since examinees and reminders can mean that p without trying to make anyone believe p, the directly intended response is the hearer's thinking the speaker believes p; imperatives must further aim to make the hearer form an intention.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"utterer's occasion-meaning","aliases":[],"definition":"The fourth and last type to be considered involves, instead, the use of indirect speech. … To have meant that he would then be dead, U would have to have committed himself to its being the case that he would then be dead","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"M-intending","aliases":["M-intended","M-intend"],"definition":"abbreviate \"U utters x intending A: (1) to produce r (2) to think U intends A to produce r (3) to think U intends the fulfillment of (1) to be based on the fulfillment of (2)\" to \"U utters x M-intending that A produce r.\"","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"anti-deception clause","aliases":["anti-deception","prohibitive clause"],"definition":"there is no inference-element E such that U uttered x intending both (1') that A's determination of r should rely on E and (2') that A should think U to intend that (1') be false.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"activated belief","aliases":[],"definition":"supposing the standard M-intended effect to be not just a belief but an \"activated belief\" (that A should be in a state of believing that p and having it in mind that p). … So one who reminds intends the same final response as one who informs, but is intending to remedy a different deficiency.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"purely exhibitive","aliases":["exhibitive"],"definition":"\"purely exhibitive\" utterances (utterances by which the utterer U intends to impart a belief that he [U] has a certain propositional attitude)","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"protreptic","aliases":[],"definition":"utterances which are not only exhibitive but also what I might call \"protreptic\" (that is, utterances by which U intends, via imparting the belief that he [U] has a certain propositional attitude, to induce a corresponding attitude in the hearer)","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"A man is playing bridge against his boss. He wants to earn his boss's favor, and for this reason he wants his boss to win, and furthermore he wants his boss to know that he wants him to win … when he gets a good hand, he smiles in a certain way; the smile is very like, but not quite like, a spontaneous smile of pleasure. He intends his boss to detect the difference and to argue as follows: \"That was not a genuine give-away smile, but the simulation of such a smile.\"","phenomenon":"sneaky intention (counterexample to Gricean meaning)","near":["non-natural meaning","conversational implicature","bluffing"],"avoid":["covert intention to induce belief","deliberately and openly letting someone know","misleading without lying"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Consider the following sentence (S): \"If I shall then be helping the grass to grow, I shall have no time for reading.\" … It might be true to say of a particular utterer (U) of S that when U uttered S … he meant by the words, \"I shall be helping the grass to grow\" (which occur within S), \"I shall then be dead.\" … it would not be true to say that he meant by these words that he would then be dead.","phenomenon":"utterance-type occasion-meaning","near":["applied timeless meaning","utterer's occasion-meaning","timeless meaning"],"avoid":["non-natural meaning","metaphor"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"The proprietor of a shop full of knickknacks for tourists is standing in his doorway in Port Said, sees a British visitor, and in dulcet tones and with an alluring smile says to him the Arabic for \"You pig of an Englishman.\" I should be quite inclined to say that he had meant that the visitor was to come in, or something of the sort. I would not of course be in the least inclined to say that he had meant by the words which he uttered that the visitor was to come in","phenomenon":"speaker meaning independent of sentence meaning","near":["verbal irony","conversational implicature","malapropism"],"avoid":["non-natural meaning","non-conventional (communicative) illocutionary act"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Q: \"When was the Battle of Waterloo\"? A: \"1815\" (\"1816\") Here the examinee meant that the Battle of Waterloo was fought in 1815 (1816) but hardly M-intended to induce a belief to that effect in his examiner. The examiner's beliefs (whatever they may be) are naturally to be thought of by the examinee as independent of candidates' answers.","phenomenon":"purely exhibitive utterance","near":["protreptic utterance","reminding","conversational implicature"],"avoid":["non-natural meaning"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2184179"},{"id":"fine1975-vagueness-truth-logic","title":"Vagueness, Truth and Logic","authors":["Kit Fine"],"year":"1975","area":"language","topic":"vagueness & gradability","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the super-truth theory, that a vague sentence is true if it is true for all admissible and complete specifications.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"an adequate account of penumbral connection appears to require that the logic be classical.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Truth is supertruth: at a specification point t, t ⊨ A iff u ⊨ A for every complete admissible u ≥ t, and A is true simpliciter iff true at the base point; so every classical validity remains valid.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Three-valued truth-functional semantics cannot respect penumbral connections ('the blob is pink and red' is false though both conjuncts are indefinite), so a vague language should be evaluated across its admissible precisifications, securing excluded middle despite truth-value gaps.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"penumbral connection","aliases":["penumbral connection","penumbral truths","penumbral","penumbra"],"definition":"Let us refer to the possibility that logical relations hold among indefinite sentences as penumbral connection; and let us call the truths that arise, wholly or in part, from penumbral connection, truths on a penumbra or penumbral truths.","exact":false},{"id":"t2","term":"super-truth","aliases":["super-truth","supertruth"],"definition":"a sentence is true (or false) at a partial specification point if and only if it is true (or false) at all complete extensions. A sentence is true simpliciter if and only if it is true at the appropriate specification-point, i.e. at all complete and admissible specifications. Truth is super-truth, truth from above.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"intensional vagueness","aliases":["intensionally vague","intensional vagueness"],"definition":"A predicate F is extensionally vague if it has borderline cases, intensionally vague if it could have borderline cases. Thus 'bald' is extensionally vague, I presume, and remains intensionally vague in a world of hairy or hairless men.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"truth-value shift","aliases":["truth-value shift","shifts","shift"],"definition":"what one might call truth-value shift. This also lies behind LEM. Thus A ∨ ¬A holds in virtue of a truth that shifts from disjunct to disjunct for different complete specifications","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"higher-order vagueness","aliases":["higher-order vagueness","higher-order","higher order"],"definition":"The vague may itself be vague, or vaguely vague, and so on. … Thus higher-order vagueness can be expressed in the material mode, with the help of the definitely-operator, or in the formal mode, with the help of the truth-predicate.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose that a certain blob is on the border of pink and red and let P be the sentence 'the blob is pink' and R the sentence 'the blob is red'. Then the conjunction P & R is false since the predicates 'is pink' and 'is red' are contraries.","phenomenon":"penumbral connection","near":["higher-order vagueness","sorites paradox","truth-value glut"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"let J be the ambiguous sentence 'John went to the bank'; let J₁ and J₂ be its disambiguations, viz. 'John went to the money bank' and 'John went to the river bank'; and suppose that John is after fish rather than money. Then the disjunction J ∨ ¬J is true, for its disambiguations, J₁ ∨ ¬J₁ and J₂ ∨ ¬J₂, are true. However, neither disjunct is true, for each disjunct has a false disambiguation.","phenomenon":"excluded middle without bivalence","near":["truth-value glut","presupposition failure","failure of non-contradiction"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"A man with no hairs on his head is bald. If a man with n hairs on his head is bald then a man with (n + 1) hairs on his head is bald. ∴ A man with a million hairs on his head is bald. The conclusion follows from the premisses with the help of a million applications of modus ponens and universal instantiation.","phenomenon":"sorites paradox","near":["liar paradox","lottery paradox","higher-order vagueness"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Suppose the meaning of the natural-number term 'nice₁' is fixed by just two clauses: (a) n is nice₁ if n > 15; (b) n is not nice₁ if n < 13.","phenomenon":"vague predicate","near":["ambiguous predicate","highly general predicate","undecidable predicate"],"exact":false},{"id":"e5","text":"suppose that James has a few fewer hairs on his head than his friend Herbert. Then he may well be a borderline case of a borderline case or a borderline case of a borderline case of a borderline case of a bald man.","phenomenon":"higher-order vagueness","near":["sorites paradox","penumbral connection","ontic vagueness"],"exact":true},{"id":"e6","text":"Suppose I press my hand against my eyes and 'see stars'. Then LEM should hold for the sentence S = 'I see many stars', if it is taken as a vague description of a precise experience. However, LEM should fail for S if it is taken as a precise description of an intrinsically vague experience.","phenomenon":"ontic vagueness","near":["higher-order vagueness","vagueness as ignorance","penumbral connection"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00485047"},{"id":"langton1993-speech-acts-unspeakable","title":"Speech Acts and Unspeakable Acts","authors":["Rae Langton"],"year":"1993","area":"language","topic":"speech acts & assertion","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Some speech acts are unspeakable for women in some contexts: although the appropriate words can be uttered, those utterances fail to count as the actions they were intended to be.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The claims that pornography subordinates and silences women make perfect sense … Moreover, if pornographic speech is indeed authoritative, the claims may well be true.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Read as illocutionary acts, pornography can subordinate women, ranking them as sex objects and legitimating sexual violence, and can silence them by disabling acts like refusal and protest, provided pornographers' speech carries authority.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Silencing need not stop anyone uttering words: authoritative speech such as pornography can set the felicity conditions for others' speech acts, so that a woman's 'no' fails to secure uptake and does not count as refusal.","formal":false}],"pick":2,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"illocutionary disablement","aliases":["illocutionary","disablement","disabled"],"definition":"silencing that happens when one speaks, one utters words, and fails not simply to achieve the effect one aims at, but fails to perform the very action one intends. … although the appropriate words are uttered, with the appropriate intention, the speaker fails to perform the intended illocutionary act.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"perlocutionary frustration","aliases":["frustrated","frustration"],"definition":"where a speaker says words, succeeds in performing the intended illocution, but fails to achieve the intended effect","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"authoritative illocutions","aliases":["authority","authoritative illocution"],"definition":"actions whose felicity conditions require that the speaker occupy a position of authority in a relevant domain","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"illocutionary act of subordination","aliases":["subordinate","subordinates","subordinating"],"definition":"speech can subordinate when it has a certain verdictive and exercitive force: when it unfairly ranks members of a social group as inferior, when it legitimates discriminatory behavior towards them, and when it unjustly deprives them of some important powers.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"exercitive","aliases":["exercitives"],"definition":"illocutions that confer powers and rights on people, or deprive people of powers and rights. Actions of ordering, permitting, prohibiting, authorizing, enacting law, and dismissing an employee are illocutions of this kind","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Consider this utterance: \"Blacks are not permitted to vote.\" Imagine that it is uttered by a legislator in Pretoria in the context of enacting legislation that underpins apartheid. It is a locutionary act: by \"Blacks\" it refers to blacks. It is a perlocutionary act: it will have the effect, among others, that blacks stay away from polling booths. But it is, first and foremost, an illocutionary act: it makes it the case that blacks are not permitted to vote.","phenomenon":"subordinating speech act","near":["depiction of subordination","perlocutionary frustration","commissive illocutionary act"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"An umpire calls \"Fault\" at a tennis match. He expresses his opinion. He describes the world as he sees it. But he does much more than that: he gives his verdict. A bystander says \"Fault.\" He expresses his opinion. He describes the world as he sees it. What he says has just the same content as what the umpire says: they perform the same locutionary act. But the bystander's utterance makes no difference to the score. The umpire's does.","phenomenon":"verdictive illocution","aliases":["verdict"],"near":["commissive illocutionary act","behabitive illocutionary act","expositive illocutionary act"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Sometimes \"no,\" when spoken by a woman, does not count as the act of refusal. The hearer fails to recognize the utterance as a refusal; uptake is not secured. In saying \"no\" she may well intend to refuse. By saying \"no\" she intends to prevent sex, but she is far from doing as she intends.","phenomenon":"illocutionary silencing","near":["perlocutionary frustration","simple (locutionary) silencing","insincere illocution (Austinian abuse)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"In saying \"no,\" she really does refuse. By saying \"no,\" she intends to prevent her hearer from continuing his advances. But the hearer goes ahead and forces sex on the woman. She prohibits, but he fails to obey. She fails to achieve the goal of her refusal.","phenomenon":"perlocutionary frustration","near":["illocutionary silencing","simple (locutionary) silencing","illocutionary misfire"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"Ordeal is an act of protest, a resounding denunciation of the industry in which Marchiano says she was forced to perform. … Why then is Ordeal in a mail-order catalog for adult reading? … here, in this context, for these intended hearers, the uptake secured is bound to be that of pornography. Marchiano says the words appropriate for an act of protest. … She intends to protest. But her speech misfires.","phenomenon":"illocutionary silencing","near":["perlocutionary frustration","simple (locutionary) silencing","etiolated (non-serious) utterance"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"williamson1996-knowing-asserting","title":"Knowing and Asserting","authors":["Timothy Williamson"],"year":"1996","area":"language","topic":"speech acts & assertion","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The rule of assertion is the knowledge rule; one must not assert that P unless one knows that P.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"One can think of the knowledge rule as giving the condition on which a speaker has the authority to make an assertion.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Assertion is the speech act uniquely constituted by the rule One must: (assert p → Kp), the conditional inside 'must' but outside 'assert'; since Kp → p, the truth norm is a mere corollary.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Against truth and belief-based norms: lottery cases show no evidential probability short of 1 warrants flat-out assertion, while Moorean 'p but I don't know that p' and 'How do you know?' challenges show knowledge is required.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"knowledge rule","aliases":["knowledge rules"],"definition":"One must: assert that P only if one knows that P.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"constitutive rule","aliases":["Constitutive rules","constitutive rules","constitutive"],"definition":"Constitutive rules are not conventions. … a rule will count as constitutive of an act only if it is essential to that act: necessarily, the rule governs every performance of the act.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"warrant","aliases":["Warrant","warrants","warranted"],"definition":"'Warrant' is used here as a term of art, for that evidential condition (if any) which plays the role of the condition that C(P) in the correct simple account of assertion.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"RBK rule","aliases":["RBK"],"definition":"One must: assert that P only if one rationally believes that one knows that P.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"factiveness","aliases":["factive"],"definition":"A way of having warrant to assert that P is factive just in case a necessary condition of having warrant to assert that P in that way is that P.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose that you have bought a ticket in a very large lottery. Only one ticket wins. Although the draw has been held, the result has not yet been announced. In fact, your ticket did not win, but I have no inside information to that effect. On the merely probabilistic grounds that your ticket was only one of very many, I assert to you flat-out, \"Your ticket did not win,\" without telling you my grounds. Intuitively, my grounds are quite inadequate for that outright unqualified assertion","phenomenon":"lottery proposition","aliases":["lottery"],"near":["Gettier case","Moore's paradox","preface paradox"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Something is wrong with any assertion of the form \"P and I do not know that P,\" even though such assertions would often be true if made.","phenomenon":"Moore's paradox","near":["preface paradox","lottery paradox","Fitch's paradox of knowability"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"It is winter, and it looks exactly as it would if there were snow outside, but in fact that white stuff is not snow but foam put there by a film crew of whose existence I have no idea. I do not know that there is snow outside, because there is no snow outside, but it is quite reasonable for me to believe not just that there is snow outside but that I know that there is … Surely it is then reasonable for me to assert that there is snow outside.","phenomenon":"blameless (excusable) norm violation","near":["Gettier case","selfless assertion","lottery proposition"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"When I have a genuine proof, expert mathematicians may tell me falsely that it contains a fallacy. They may give me a complicated explanation of the supposed fallacy, blinding me with science. I may recall other occasions on which what I believed for broadly similar reasons to be a proof really did turn out to be fallacious. In such cases, it would be unreasonable for me to assert that P, for it is unreasonable for me to believe that I have warrant to assert that P.","phenomenon":"defeat by misleading higher-order evidence","near":["Gettier case","lottery proposition","Moore's paradox"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2998423"},{"id":"karttunen1973-presuppositions-compound-sentences","title":"Presuppositions of Compound Sentences","authors":["Lauri Karttunen"],"year":"1973","area":"language","topic":"presupposition & projection","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"an adequate solution to the projection problem makes it necessary to distinguish between three different types of complementizable predicates, which I will informally call \"plugs\", \"holes\", and \"filters\".","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The most important aspect of the filtering conditions is that the cancelling of presuppositions depends on the semantic relation between the sentences involved, not on their actual truth values.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A complex sentence's presuppositions cannot be computed from its clauses alone: whether a connective cancels a clause's presupposition depends on which background assumptions are in play, so presupposition must be relativized to a linguistic context.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Relative to assumed facts X, 'if A then B', 'A and B' and 'A or B' inherit every presupposition of A, and a presupposition C of B unless X ∪ {A} ⊨ C (for 'or', X ∪ {¬A} ⊨ C).","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"plug","aliases":["plugs"],"definition":"predicates which block off all the presuppositions of the complement sentence; … verbs that are commonly called \"verbs of saying\" or \"performatives\", such as say, mention, tell, ask, promise, warn, request, order, accuse","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"hole","aliases":["holes"],"definition":"The class of holes contains all ordinary run-of-the-mill complementizable predicates, such as know, regret, understand, surprise, be significant, begin, stop, continue, manage … If the main verb of the sentence is a hole, then the sentence has all the presuppositions of the complement sentences embedded in it.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"filter","aliases":["filters"],"definition":"predicates which, under certain conditions, cancel some of the presuppositions of the complement","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"filtering conditions","aliases":["filtering condition"],"definition":"Assuming that B presupposes C (relative to X), a sentence of the form \"If A then B\" or \"A and B\" need not have this presupposition (relative to X) provided that C is entailed by the set which results from adding A to X.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"projection problem","aliases":["projection"],"definition":"question of how the presuppositions of a complex sentence are determined by the presuppositions of the clauses it contains","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"If Jack has children, then all of Jack's children are bald. In (4) the consequent clause presupposes that Jack has children. Nevertheless, the conditional as a whole has no such presupposition.","phenomenon":"presupposition filtering","aliases":["presupposition"],"near":["presupposition accommodation","presupposition failure","presupposition plugging by a verb of saying"],"avoid":["filtering relative to background assumptions"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Bill ordered Fred to stop beating Zelda. … assume that Bill mistakenly believed that Fred was beating Zelda and ordered him to stop. In reporting on what Bill did, the speaker does not have to commit himself to the false belief which led Bill to issue his order.","phenomenon":"presupposition plugging by a verb of saying","near":["presupposition filtering","presupposition projection through holes","presupposition accommodation"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"\"Cecilia knew that it surprised Mary that Fred hesitated to stop beating his wife.\" From this one can conclude that Fred has a wife, that he has been beating her, that he hesitated to stop, and that this surprised Mary, but nothing about whether he stopped.","phenomenon":"presupposition projection through holes","near":["presupposition plugging by a verb of saying","presupposition filtering","presupposition accommodation"],"exact":false},{"id":"e4","text":"Suppose that our speaker, Fred, believes that Mormons are required to wear a special kind of undergarment, called \"holy underwear\". … Furthermore, Fred has some reason to believe that a certain girl, Geraldine, is a Mormon. However, Fred has never caught even a glimpse of what Geraldine is wearing under her dress. One evening, Fred manages to peer into Geraldine's bedroom and sees her in an ordinary bra and a pair of panties. Being somewhat surprised Fred utters … Either Geraldine is not a Mormon or she has given up wearing her holy underwear.","phenomenon":"filtering relative to background assumptions","near":["presupposition projection through holes","presupposition plugging by a verb of saying","presupposition-cancelling external negation"],"avoid":["presupposition filtering"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"I ask you to stop beating Zelda. In order to count as a sincere utterance, [this] requires that it be part of the linguistic context that the addressee has been beating Zelda.","phenomenon":"leaky plug","near":["presupposition plugging by a verb of saying","presupposition filtering","presupposition accommodation"],"avoid":["presupposition projection through holes"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"heim1983-projection-problem-presuppositions","title":"On the Projection Problem for Presuppositions","authors":["Irene Heim"],"year":"1983","area":"language","topic":"presupposition & projection","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"we have reduced two seemingly independent semantic properties, the content and the heritage property, to just one, the [context change potential].","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"admittance conditions are conditions on the definedness of the [context change potential], i.e. that c+S is defined iff c admits S.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Projection needs no lexical stipulation: a connective's context change potential, motivated by truth conditions alone, fixes each constituent's local context, and a context admits a complex sentence only if every constituent's presupposition is entailed by its local context.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"c + If A, B = c \\ (c+A \\ c+A+B), with c+S defined iff c admits S; hence c admits 'If A, B' iff c admits A and c+A admits B.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"context change potential","aliases":["CCP","CCPs","context change","context"],"definition":"What are CCPs? Intuitively, they are instructions specifying certain operations of context change. The CCP of \"It is raining\", for instance, is the instruction to conjoin the current context with the proposition that it is raining.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"heritage property","aliases":[],"definition":"[Lexical information that] becomes relevant only for items that are functors rather than arguments, and … concerns the item's permeability for the presuppositions of its arguments. E.g. … \"if\" lets through the full presupposition of its left argument, as well as as much of the presupposition of its right argument as doesn't follow from the left argument.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"local context","aliases":["context"],"definition":"If \"If A, B\" is uttered in context c, then c is the local context for A, and c + A (read: \"c incremented by A\") is the local context for B.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"global accommodation","aliases":["global"],"definition":"The \"global\" option: Amend c to c&p and, instead of c + Not S, calculate c&p + Not S. … [This] is more like pretending that c&p obtained instead of c all along (hence the word \"global\").","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"local accommodation","aliases":["local"],"definition":"The \"local\" option: Amend c to c&p so that you can calculate c&p + S instead of c + S. … [This] is rather like adjusting the context only for the immediate purpose of evaluating the constituent sentence S (hence \"local\").","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"If the king has a son, the king's son is bald.","phenomenon":"presupposition filtering","near":["local accommodation","presupposition failure","the proviso problem"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"If John has children, then Mary will not like his twins. … [This] is slightly strange, at least out of context. It somehow suggests that it is a matter of course that someone with children will have twins among them.","phenomenon":"conditional presupposition","near":["presupposition cancellation","local accommodation","presupposition failure"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"The king of France didn't come. … uttered in a context which is compatible with France having no king. … the speaker continues [it] with \"because France doesn't have a king\".","phenomenon":"local accommodation","near":["global accommodation","presupposition filtering","presupposition projection from under negation"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"A fat man was pushing his bicycle. [Karttunen and Peters's] rules assign to [it] a presupposition that they admit is too weak: that some fat man had a bicycle. On the other hand, a universal presupposition that every fat man had a bicycle would be too strong. What one would like to predict is, vaguely speaking, a presupposition to the effect that the same fat man that verifies the content of [the sentence] had a bicycle.","phenomenon":"binding problem for presuppositions","aliases":["presupposition"],"near":["donkey anaphora","universal presupposition projection","presupposition filtering"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"karttunen1974-presupposition-linguistic-context","title":"Presupposition and Linguistic Context","authors":["Lauri Karttunen"],"year":"1974","area":"language","topic":"presupposition & projection","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Do not ask what the [presuppositions] of a complex sentence are, ask what it takes to satisfy them.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Context X satisfies-the-presuppositions-of S just in case the presuppositions of each of the constituent sentences in S are satisfied by the corresponding local context.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Instead of projecting presuppositions to compound sentences, state when a context admits one: each clause's presuppositions must be entailed by its local context, the initial context incremented left to right with the preceding clauses (negated in disjunctions).","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A context X admits a simple sentence iff X entails its basic presuppositions; X admits 'If A then B' (or 'A and B') iff X admits A and X ∪ {A} admits B.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"local context","aliases":["context"],"definition":"In compound sentences, the initial context is incremented in a left-to-right fashion giving for each constituent sentence a local context that must satisfy its presuppositions.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"admittance","aliases":["satisfies-the-presuppositions-of","satisfaction","admits"],"definition":"Context X satisfies-the-presuppositions-of A just in case X entails all of the basic presuppositions of A … For compound sentences we define satisfaction recursively by associating each part of the sentence with a different context.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"pragmatic presupposition","aliases":["pragmatically presupposes"],"definition":"Surface sentence A pragmatically presupposes a logical form L, if and only if it is the case that A can be felicitously uttered only in contexts which entail L.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"context","aliases":[],"definition":"a set of logical forms that describe the set of background assumptions, that is, whatever the speaker chooses to regard as being shared by him and his intended audience","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"fully explicit discourse","aliases":[],"definition":"In a fully explicit discourse, the presuppositions of the next sentence uttered are satisfied by the current context.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"(a) If Dean told the truth, Nixon is guilty too. (b) If Haldeman is guilty, Nixon is guilty too. … the consequent clause \"Nixon is guilty too\" is interpreted in the sense in which it presupposes the guilt of someone else. The question is: does the compound sentence as a whole carry that presupposition? In the case of (3a), the answer seems to be definitely yes, in the case … of (3b) definitely no","phenomenon":"presupposition filtering","aliases":["presupposition"],"near":["presupposition accommodation","presupposition failure","factive presupposition"],"avoid":["presupposition projection","local satisfaction of presuppositions"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"We regret that children cannot accompany their parents to commencement exercises. … (26a) presupposes that its complement is true. Yet the sentence could readily be used in a conversational context that does not satisfy this presupposition. Perhaps the whole point of uttering (26a) is to let it be known that parents should not bring their kids along.","phenomenon":"presupposition accommodation","aliases":["presupposition"],"near":["presupposition failure","presupposition cancellation","presupposition filtering"],"avoid":["factive presupposition","informative presupposition","global accommodation"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Ziegler announced that Nixon will stop protecting his aides. (23) can be spoken felicitously, perhaps even truly, no matter what the facts are understood to be or whether anyone is supposed to hold a set of beliefs that satisfy the presuppositions of the complement.","phenomenon":"presupposition plug","aliases":["presuppositions"],"near":["presupposition hole","presupposition filter","presupposition accommodation"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"The courts may force Nixon to stop protecting his aides. For example, a context for [this sentence] ought to entail that Nixon has or will have been protecting his aides.","phenomenon":"presupposition hole","near":["presupposition plug","presupposition filter","presupposition accommodation"],"avoid":["presupposition projection","presupposition inheritance"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1515/thli.1974.1.1-3.181"},{"id":"lewis1976-probabilities-conditionals-conditional","title":"Probabilities of Conditionals and Conditional Probabilities","authors":["David Lewis"],"year":"1976","area":"language","topic":"conditionals","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"if a class of probability functions is closed under conditionalizing, then there can be no probability conditional for that class unless the class consists entirely of trivial probability functions.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The quest for a probability conditional is futile, and we must admit that assertability does not go by absolute probability in the case of indicative conditionals.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"If P(A→C) = P(C|A) for every P in a class closed under conditionalizing, then P(A→C|C) = 1 and P(A→C|¬C) = 0, so expanding by cases gives P(C|A) = P(C): absurd unless every P is trivial.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Indicative conditionals are assertable to the degree P(C|A), but that cannot be a probability of truth; they may instead be material conditionals whose probability, minus a Gricean penalty for mostly vacuous truth, equals P(C|A).","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"probability conditional","aliases":["probability"],"definition":"→ is interpreted in such a way that, for some particular probability function P, and for any sentences A and C, … P(A → C) = P(C/A), if P(A) is positive","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"trivial language","aliases":["language"],"definition":"If an interpreted language cannot provide three possible but pairwise incompatible sentences, then we may justly call it a trivial language.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"imaging","aliases":["image"],"definition":"the image on A of a probability function is formed by shifting the original probability of each world W over to W_A, the closest A-world to W. Probability is moved around but not created or destroyed","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"probability-revision conditional","aliases":[],"definition":"the connective → is interpreted in such a way that for any probability function P, and for any sentences A and C, … P(A → C) = P_A(C), if A is possible, where P_A is (in some sense) the minimal revision of P that raises the probability of A to 1","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"opinionated","aliases":["opinion"],"definition":"a probability function P that gives all the probability to W and none to any other world … it would represent the beliefs of someone who was absolutely certain that the world W was actual and who therefore held a firm opinion about every question","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"We are gathering mushrooms; I say to you \"You won't eat that one and live.\" A dirty trick: I thought that one was safe and especially delicious, I wanted it myself, so I hoped to dissuade you from taking it … I thought it highly probable that my trick would work, that you would not eat the mushroom, and therefore that I would turn out to have told the truth.","phenomenon":"misleading without lying","near":["lying","bullshit (indifference to truth)","Moore's paradox"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"let P(W), P(W′), and P(W″) each equal 1/3; let A hold at W and W′ but not W″; and let W′ be the closest A-world to W″. Then the probability function that comes from P by conditionalizing on A assigns probability 1/2 to both W and W′; whereas the probability function that comes from P by imaging on A assigns probability 1/3 to W and 2/3 to W′.","phenomenon":"probabilistic imaging","aliases":["imaging"],"near":["Bayesian conditionalization","Jeffrey conditionalization","AGM belief revision"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"I'll probably flunk, and it doesn't matter whether I study; I'll flunk if I do and I'll flunk if I don't.","phenomenon":"non-interference conditional","near":["biscuit conditional","counterfactual conditional","conditional perfection"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2184045"},{"id":"kaplan1968-quantifying","title":"Quantifying In","authors":["David Kaplan"],"year":"1968","area":"language","topic":"attitude reports & de se","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"α represents x to Ralph … if and only if (i) α denotes x, (ii) α is a name of x for Ralph, and (iii) α is (sufficiently) vivid.","formal":true},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"According to my analysis, Ralph must have quite a solid conception of x before we can say that Ralph believes x to be a spy.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Relational belief is defined from notional belief: Ralph Bel('x is a spy', y) iff ∃α[R(α, y, Ralph) ∧ Ralph B ⌜α is a spy⌝], α being a name that denotes y, is of y for Ralph, and is vivid.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Against Quine, expressions in opaque contexts are treated Frege's way, as denoting intermediate entities; de re necessity and belief then need privileged names: standard names (numerals) for abstract objects, vivid causally grounded names for perceptible individuals.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"intermediate occurrence","aliases":["intermediate occurrences","intermediate contexts","intermediate","occurrence"],"definition":"[Uses of 'nine' as in 'Hegel believed that nine is greater than five'] diverge from the paradigm of vulgar occurrence (they fail the substitution test, the existential generalization test, and probably others as well), but they are not … clearly orthographic accidents either: for in them, the meaning of 'nine' seems, somehow, relevant.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"standard name","aliases":["standard names","standard"],"definition":"A standard name is one whose denotation is fixed on logical, or perhaps I should say linguistic, grounds alone. Numerals and quotation names are prominent among the standard names.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"descriptive content","aliases":[],"definition":"Returning now to names, it is their descriptive content that determines what if anything they denote. Thus, denotation is the analogue for names to resemblance for pictures.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"genetic character","aliases":[],"definition":"The genetic character of a name in a given person's usage will account for how he acquired the name … It is the genetic character of the name that determines what if anything it is a name of","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"vivid name","aliases":["vivid names","vividness","vivid"],"definition":"the conglomeration of images, names, and partial descriptions which Ralph employs to bring x before his mind. Such a conglomeration, when suitably arranged and regimented, is what I call a vivid name. … The crucial feature of this notion is that it depends only on Ralph's current mental state, and ignores all links whether by resemblence or genesis with the actual world.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"There is a certain man in a brown hat whom Ralph has glimpsed several times under questionable circumstances on which we need not enter here; suffice it to say that Ralph suspects he is a spy. Also there is a gray-haired man, vaguely known to Ralph as rather a pillar of the community, whom Ralph is not aware of having seen except once at the beach. Now Ralph does not know it, but the men are one and the same.","phenomenon":"believing of one person that he is two","near":["believing of two persons that they are one","merely de dicto belief","referential use of a definite description"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Believing that spies differ widely in height, Ralph believes that one among them is shortest. Thus, (39) Ralph B 'the shortest spy is a spy'. Supposing that there is in fact one shortest spy … we would not expect the interest of [the F.B.I.] to be piqued by Ralph's conviction that no two spies share a size.","phenomenon":"merely de dicto (notional) belief","near":["de re belief","de se belief","failure of substitutivity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"if we were to drop clause (iii), and allow any name which both denotes x and is a name of x to represent x to Holmes, then after Holmes observed the victim, 'the murderer' would represent the murderer to him. … There is someone whom Holmes believes to be the murderer. But this last should presage an arrest and not the mere certification of homicide.","phenomenon":"vividness requirement on de re belief","near":["genetic (causal) requirement on de re belief","denotation requirement on de re belief","standard-name requirement on de re necessity"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"[Suppose I] perform a dubbing in absentia, as by solemnly declaring \"I hereby dub the first child to be born in the twenty-second century 'Newman 1'\", and thus grant myself standing to have beliefs about that as yet unborn child.","phenomenon":"descriptive name (name introduced by description)","near":["standard name","vivid name","referential use of a definite description"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"had a photograph of Julius Caesar been xeroxed, and the xerox copy televised to a monastery, where it was copied by a monk, and so was reproduced down through the ages, I would call the resulting copy, no matter how distorted, no matter who, if anyone, it resembled, a picture of Julius Caesar.","phenomenon":"causal-historical chain of reference","near":["resemblance-based (descriptivist) reference","vividness of a representation","standard name"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00568057"},{"id":"burge1977-belief-de-re","title":"Belief De Re","authors":["Tyler Burge"],"year":"1977","area":"language","topic":"attitude reports & de se","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"A de re belief is a belief whose correct ascription places the believer in an appropriate nonconceptual, contextual relation to objects the belief is about.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"de re belief is in important ways more fundamental than the de dicto variety; and this can be seen if one attends to its role in basic cognitive activities.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Formally, de dicto belief relates a believer to what a closed sentence expresses, Bd(Ortcutt, ⌜∃x Spy(x)⌝); de re belief relates a believer to an open sentence's content and a res: ∃x Br(Ortcutt, ⟨x⟩, ⌜Spy(y)⌝).","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Understanding language, having any propositional attitude, and gaining empirical knowledge require attitudes tied to objects by perception or context; these are irreducible to purely conceptual ones, since context rather than an individuating concept often fixes the object.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"de dicto belief","aliases":["de dicto","dictum"],"definition":"From a semantical viewpoint, a de dicto belief is a belief in which the believer is related only to a completely expressed proposition (dictum). The epistemic analogue is a belief that is fully conceptualized.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"de re locution","aliases":["de re locutions","de re"],"definition":"De re locutions are about predication broadly conceived. They describe a relation between open sentences (or what they express) and objects.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"nonconceptual","aliases":["non-conceptual"],"definition":"The term 'nonconceptual' does not imply that no concepts or other mental notions enter into a full statement of the relation. … The crucial point is that the relation not be merely that of the concepts' being concepts of the object—concepts that denote or apply to it.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"sense₃","aliases":["sense","senses","sense3"],"definition":"The third of the three functions Frege's notion of sense fills: providing the entities that expressions denote in oblique contexts, roughly the dicta attributed in ordinary belief talk; not identifiable with mode of presentation, mode of designation, or linguistic meaning.","exact":false}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"On seeing a man coming from a distance in a swirling fog, we may plausibly be said to believe of him that he is wearing a red cap. But we do not see the man well enough to describe or image him in such a way as to individuate him fully.","phenomenon":"perceptual de re belief","near":["de dicto belief","attributive use of a definite description","de se belief"],"exact":false},{"id":"e2","text":"If Alfie says, \"The most powerful man on earth in 1970 (whoever he is) is a crook,\" not having the slightest idea who the most powerful man is, a friend of the potentate may say to him, \"Alfie believes that you are a crook.\"","phenomenon":"de re report of a de dicto belief","near":["genuine (en rapport) de re belief","referential use of a definite description","de se belief"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"We would find boring a claim of the form \"this = this\", where 'this' is twice used to refer to some object under the same circumstances … But we might be surprised by a claim of the form \"this = this\", where the first 'this' is used with a nod toward a picture of the Hope diamond, and the second with a gesture to a dirty stone.","phenomenon":"Frege's puzzle about informative identity","near":["essential indexical","rigid designation","pronoun of laziness"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"if we say that Alfie believes … (i) that 2 squared is 4, we may refuse to say that Alfie believes (ii) that the only even prime squared is 4. … Alfie may not realize that 2 is the only even prime number. Even if he does, we would say that his believing (i) is one thing, and his believing (ii) another.","phenomenon":"referential opacity (substitution failure)","near":["de re belief","exportation","essential indexical"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2025871"},{"id":"kratzer1981-notional-category-modality","title":"The Notional Category of Modality","authors":["Angelika Kratzer"],"year":"1981","area":"language","topic":"modality","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The full range of possible modal meanings expressed in natural languages can now be characterized by conditions on three parameters: modal base, ordering source, and modal force.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Simplifying slightly, a proposition is a necessity just in case it is true in all accessible worlds that come closest to the ideal determined by the ordering source.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Every modal is interpreted relative to two context-supplied conversational backgrounds: a modal base, which fixes the accessible worlds, and an ordering source, which ranks them by some norm or ideal; modal force then quantifies over the best-ranked worlds.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Facts and norms must be kept apart: ranking the worlds compatible with the facts by an ideal is what yields graded possibility ('a good possibility', 'more likely'), correct predictions for practical inference, and non-vacuous deontic conditionals.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"modal base","aliases":["modal bases"],"definition":"The modal base determines the set of accessible worlds, and the ordering source induces an ordering on it.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"ordering source","aliases":["ordering sources"],"definition":"ordering sources are used as domain restrictions for the set of accessible worlds: not all, but only the “closest” accessible worlds matter for what is possible or necessary.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"conversational background","aliases":["conversational backgrounds"],"definition":"I take conversational backgrounds to be functions mapping possible worlds to premise sets—that is, sets of propositions.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"realistic conversational background","aliases":["realistic conversational backgrounds","realistic"],"definition":"A realistic conversational background is a function f such that for any world w, w ∈ ∩f(w). That is, f assigns to every possible world a set of propositions that are true in it.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"totally realistic conversational background","aliases":["totally realistic conversational backgrounds","totally realistic"],"definition":"A totally realistic conversational background is a function f such that for any w ∈ W, ∩f(w) = {w}. That is, f assigns to any world a set of propositions that characterizes it uniquely.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"stereotypical conversational background","aliases":["stereotypical conversational backgrounds","stereotypical"],"definition":"A stereotypical conversational background is a function f such that for any world w, f(w) represents what is normal in w according to some suitable normalcy standard for w.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Much-Girgl has been murdered on his way home. There is an ongoing investigation. Conclusions … are being drawn from the available evidence, and utterances of the following sentences might have occurred: … Kastenjakl may be the murderer. … Gauzner-Michl must be the murderer. In uttering [the first], a police inspector may have claimed that given the available evidence, it is possible that Kastenjakl committed the murder.","phenomenon":"epistemic modality","near":["deontic modality","circumstantial (root) modality","teleological modality"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Suppose I am traveling in an exotic country and discover that soil and climate are very much like that in my own country, where plum trees prosper everywhere. In such a situation, an utterance of [‘In this area can plum trees grow’] … would probably be true. But [‘It may be that in this area plum trees grow’] might very well be false, given that that country has had no contacts whatsoever with Western civilization and the vegetation is altogether different from ours.","phenomenon":"circumstantial (root) modality","aliases":["circumstantial"],"near":["epistemic modality","deontic modality","bouletic modality"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Suppose two burglars are trying to enter a farmhouse and whisper to each other: … Jetzt dürfen wir keinen Lärm machen. Now may we no noise make. It is not that they are not allowed to make a noise. They can't make a noise in view of their goal to burgle the farmers without getting caught.","phenomenon":"teleological modality","aliases":["goal"],"near":["deontic modality","epistemic modality","ability (dynamic) modality"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Imagine that tomorrow is the coronation of the King and I say: … Morgen darf es nicht regnen. Tomorrow may it not rain. What I say here is roughly that according to what we all want, it shouldn't rain tomorrow.","phenomenon":"bouletic modality","aliases":["want"],"near":["deontic modality","epistemic modality","ability (dynamic) modality"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"(59) Every person must be treated justly. (60) If someone was treated unjustly, the injustice must be amended for. (61) If someone was treated unjustly, the injustice must be rewarded. Read as 'necessarily (if p, q)' over the morally accessible worlds, if (59) is true there is no injustice in any morally accessible world, so (60) and (61) both come out vacuously true.","phenomenon":"contrary-to-duty obligation","near":["Ross's paradox","free choice permission","the miners puzzle"],"exact":false}],"doi":null},{"id":"derrida1972-signature-event-context","title":"Signature Event Context","authors":["Jacques Derrida"],"year":"1972","area":"language","topic":"speech acts & assertion","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Every sign, linguistic or nonlinguistic … can be cited … thereby it can break with every given context, and engender infinitely new contexts in an absolutely nonsaturable fashion.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"[The] iterability of the mark is not an accident or an anomaly, but is that … without which a mark could no longer even have a so-called \"normal\" functioning.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Because every mark must be iterable—repeatable and citable in the absence of its sender, addressee, referent and intended meaning—no context is ever fully determinable or saturated, and intention cannot anchor meaning.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Speech-act theory cannot quarantine 'non-serious' citation (actors, poems, soliloquy) as parasitic: even a successful performative works only by repeating a coded, iterable formula, so citationality is a condition of ordinary utterance, not a deviation.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"iterability","aliases":[],"definition":"this unity of the signifying form is constituted only by its iterability, by the possibility of being repeated in the absence not only of its referent … but of a determined signified or current intention of signification, as of every present intention of communication.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"dissemination","aliases":[],"definition":"The semantic horizon which habitually governs the notion of communication is exceeded or punctured by the intervention of writing, that is of a dissemination which cannot be reduced to a polysemia.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"deconstruction","aliases":[],"definition":"Deconstruction cannot limit itself or proceed immediately to a neutralization: it must, by means of a double gesture, a double science, a double writing, practice an overturning of the classical opposition and a general displacement of the system.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"spacing","aliases":[],"definition":"the spacing which constitutes the written sign: the spacing which separates it from other elements of the internal contextual chain (the always open possibility of its extraction and grafting), but also from all the forms of a present referent … This spacing is not the simple negativity of a lack, but the emergence of the mark.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"If I say, while looking out the window, \"The sky is blue,\" the statement will be intelligible (let us provisionally say, if you will, communicable), even if the interlocutor does not see the sky; even if I do not see it myself, if I see it poorly, if I am mistaken, or if I wish to trick my interlocutor.","phenomenon":"absence of the referent","near":["absence of the signified","agrammaticality (Sinnlosigkeit)","absence of the addressee"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"a performative utterance will, for example, be in a peculiar way hollow or void if said by an actor on the stage, or if introduced in a poem, or spoken in soliloquy.","phenomenon":"parasitic (non-serious) use of language","near":["misfire (failed convention)","abuse (insincere performative)","indirect speech act"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"\"green is or\" or \"abracadabra\" do not constitute their context in themselves, nothing prevents their functioning in another context as signifying marks … Not only in the contingent case in which, by means of the translation of German into French \"le vert est ou\" might be endowed with grammaticality, ou (oder, or) becoming when heard où (where, the mark of place) … But even \"green is or\" still signifies an example of agrammaticality.","phenomenon":"citational grafting (recontextualization)","near":["absence of the referent","countersense (Widersinnigkeit)","conversational implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Let us imagine a writing with a code idiomatic enough to have been founded and known, as a secret cipher, only by two \"subjects.\" Can it still be said that upon the death of the addressee, that is, of the two partners, the mark left by one of them is still a writing?","phenomenon":"iterability of the mark","near":["absence of the referent","parasitic (non-serious) use of language","polysemy"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"putnam1980-models-reality","title":"Models and Reality","authors":["Hilary Putnam"],"year":"1980","area":"language","topic":"reference & names","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The metaphysical realist's claim that even the ideal theory T1 might be false \"in reality\" seems to collapse into unintelligibility.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Models are not lost noumenal waifs looking for someone to name them; they are constructions within our theory itself, and they have names from birth.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Any consistent ideal theory T₁ has a model (by completeness) that can be made standard on observables; such a model meets all operational and theoretical constraints, so T₁ is true in every intended model and cannot be false 'in reality'.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Skolem-style indeterminacy of reference dissolves once understanding a language is taken to be mastery of verification procedures rather than grasp of truth conditions: use then fixes reference, so permuted models are trivially unintended.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"metaphysical realism","aliases":["metaphysical realist","metaphysical"],"definition":"the hard-core metaphysical realist—wishes it to be the case that truth and rational acceptability should be independent notions. … holds that our intentions single out \"the\" model, and that our beliefs are then either true or false in \"the\" model whether we can find out their truth values or not.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"operational constraints","aliases":["operational","operational constraint"],"definition":"Let OP be the \"correct\" assignment of values; that is, the assignment which assigns to each member of MAG the value that that magnitude actually has at each rational space-time point. Then all the information \"operational constraints\" might give us … is coded into OP.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"nonrealist semantics","aliases":["non-realist semantics","nonrealist","semantic","semantics"],"definition":"a semantic theory which holds that a language is completely understood when a verification procedure is suitably mastered, and not when truth conditions (in the classical sense) are learned.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"soft facts","aliases":["soft","soft fact","facts"],"definition":"some facts are \"soft\" in the sense of depending for their truth value on the speaker, the circumstances of utterance, etc.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"a model in which, as it might be, the set of cats and the set of dogs are permuted (i.e., 'cat' is assigned the set of dogs as its extension, and 'dog' is assigned the set of cats) … even if corresponding adjustments in the extensions of all the other predicates make it end up that the operational and theoretical constraints of total science or total belief are all \"preserved\".","phenomenon":"indeterminacy of reference","near":["inverted spectrum","natural-kind externalism (Twin Earth)","reference change (Madagascar case)"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"So your formalized set theory says that a certain set (call it \"S\") is nondenumerable. So S must be nondenumerable in all models of your set theory. … But this is impossible! … no theory can have only nondenumerable models; if a theory has a nondenumerable model, it must have denumerably infinite ones as well.","phenomenon":"Skolem's paradox","aliases":["Skolem"],"near":["Russell's paradox","Cantor's paradox","Banach–Tarski paradox"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"if \"mentalese\" is to be a vehicle for describing the external world, then the various predicate letters must have extensions which are sets of external things … But if the way \"mentalese\" is \"understood\" by the deep structures in the brain that compute, record, etc. in this \"language\" is via what artificial intelligence people call \"procedural semantics\"—that is, if the brain's program for using \"mentalese\" comprises its entire \"understanding\" of \"mentalese\"—where the program for using \"mentalese\", like any program, refers only to what is inside the computer—then how do extensions ever come into the picture at all?","phenomenon":"symbol grounding problem","near":["frame problem","homunculus regress","binding problem"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"If \"refers\" can be defined in terms of some causal predicate or predicates in the metalanguage of our theory, then … unless the word 'causes' (or whatever the causal predicate or predicates may be) is already glued to one definite relation with metaphysical glue, this does not fix a determinate extension for 'refers' at all.","phenomenon":"\"just more theory\" objection","near":["qua problem","Madagascar problem (reference shift)","argument from ignorance and error"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2273415"},{"id":"searle1975-indirect-speech-acts","title":"Indirect Speech Acts","authors":["John Searle"],"year":"1975","area":"language","topic":"speech acts & assertion","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"In indirect speech acts the speaker communicates to the hearer more than he actually says by way of relying on their mutually shared background information, both linguistic and nonlinguistic","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"there can be conventions of usage that are not meaning conventions. … can you, could you, I want you to, and numerous other forms are conventional ways of making requests","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"When 'Can you pass the salt?' is used to request, the literal question is still asked; the hearer infers the request from speech-act felicity conditions, conversational cooperation and shared background, without positing ambiguity or conversational postulates.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A directive can be issued by asserting or querying that its preparatory or propositional-content condition holds, or that there are good reasons for the act, but only by asserting, not querying, its sincerity condition (the speaker's want).","formal":false}],"pick":2,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"indirect speech act","aliases":["indirectly"],"definition":"cases in which one illocutionary act is performed indirectly by way of performing another.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"primary illocutionary act","aliases":["illocutionary act"],"definition":"the primary illocutionary act performed in Y's utterance is the rejection of the proposal made by X … the secondary illocutionary act is literal; the primary illocutionary act is not literal","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"secondary illocutionary act","aliases":["illocutionary act"],"definition":"He performs the secondary illocutionary act by way of uttering a sentence the literal meaning of which is such that its literal utterance constitutes a performance of that illocutionary act.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"maxim of idiomaticity","aliases":["idiomatically","unidiomatically"],"definition":"In general, if one speaks unidiomatically, hearers assume that there must be a special reason for it, and in consequence, various assumptions of normal speech are suspended. … Speak idiomatically unless there is some special reason not to.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Student X: Let's go to the movies tonight. … Student Y: I have to study for an exam.","phenomenon":"indirect speech act","near":["indirect request","explicit performative utterance","scalar implicature"],"avoid":["conversational implicature","particularized conversational implicature","implicature from observing the maxim of Relation","flouting the maxim of Relation","indirect rejection"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"At the dinner table, X says to Y, Can you pass the salt? by way of asking Y to pass the salt.","phenomenon":"indirect request","near":["explicit performative utterance","rhetorical question","conventional implicature"],"avoid":["indirect speech act","conventional indirect request","whimperative","speech act idiom","conversational implicature","generalized conversational implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Can I help you? I can do that for you. I could get it for you. Could I be of assistance? … Would you like some help? Do you want me to go now, Sally?","phenomenon":"indirect offer (indirect commissive)","near":["indirect request","explicit performative promise","request for permission"],"avoid":["indirect speech act","conversational implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"I'd like you to do this for me, Bill, but I am not asking you to do it or requesting that you do it or ordering you to do it or telling you to do it.","phenomenon":"cancellation of an indirect directive","near":["Moore's paradox","performative contradiction","presupposition cancellation"],"avoid":["cancellation of a conversational implicature","cancellability of implicature","implicature cancellation"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1163/9789004368811_004"},{"id":"karttunen1977-syntax-semantics-questions","title":"Syntax and Semantics of Questions","authors":["Lauri Karttunen"],"year":"1977","area":"language","topic":"questions & focus","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"I choose to make questions denote the set of propositions expressed by their true answers instead of the set of propositions expressed by their possible answers.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The meaning assigned to WH-phrases such as who and which man is the same as the meaning of the existentially quantified noun phrases someone and a man.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Indirect questions of every kind — yes/no, alternative, single or multiple wh — denote the set of true propositions jointly forming a complete answer, rather than all possible answers, which makes 'tell who' veridical.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A proto-question ?φ translates as λp[ˇp ∧ p = ˆφ′]; wh-phrases translate like existential NPs (who = λP∃x P(x)), so quantifying one in gives ⟦who walks⟧ = λp∃x[ˇp ∧ p = ˆwalk′(x)], the true propositions 'x walks'.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"proto-question","aliases":["proto-questions"],"definition":"the indirect questions generated by [prefixing a declarative sentence with ‘?’] (let us call them proto-questions) are not proper expressions of English. They are just embryonic structures which exist in order to be developed into genuine indirect questions","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"WH-Quantification Rule","aliases":["WH-Quantification","WHQ"],"definition":"forms wh-questions by inserting a WH-phrase into a proto-question or a wh-question that contains an occurrence of the corresponding unbound pronoun (that is heₙ, himₙ, or hisₙ). The pronoun is either replaced in its original location by the incoming WH-phrase or deleted in case the WH-phrase is preposed.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"search question","aliases":["search questions","search"],"definition":"The label the paper prefers for wh-interrogatives (who, which, how…): semantically they involve a search for a suitable value for a variable, or for a set of variables in the multiple case, rather than a choice between alternative propositions.","exact":false},{"id":"t4","term":"WH-phrase","aliases":["WH-phrases","interrogative noun phrases","interrogative noun phrase","WH"],"definition":"The syntactic category of interrogative noun phrases, let us call it ‘WH’, is defined as t//IV. One immediate consequence of this definition is that WH-phrases come to have the same type of denotation as ordinary noun phrases (Montague’s T-phrases). Furthermore, for semantic reasons, we make WH-phrases equivalent to existentially quantified noun phrases.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"pragmatically self-verifying","aliases":["self-verifying"],"definition":"any felicitous utterance of [Is it raining?] is a request to tell whether it is raining. On any occasion where [it] is uttered, [‘I ask you (to tell me) whether it is raining’] expresses a true proposition. … [It] is, so to speak, pragmatically self-verifying. Whenever it is uttered, it is true.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"a person who asks (iii) Who, for instance, came to the party last night? may be perfectly satisfied with an answer that lists some but not all of the people who came to the party.","phenomenon":"mention-some reading","near":["exhaustive (mention-all) reading","pair-list reading","echo question"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"(10) (a) John remembers who came. … (ii) John remembers who came although he doesn’t remember that Mary came. However, sentences of the above sort are generally felt to be contradictory","phenomenon":"exhaustivity of embedded wh-questions","near":["mention-some reading","existential presupposition of a question","pair-list reading"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"questions of the sort in (53) are ambiguous; they can be answered in two ways. (53) Who remembers where Mary keeps which book? The two kinds of admissible answers are exhibited in (54). (54) (a) Bill remembers where Mary keeps which book. (b) Joe remembers where Mary keeps Aspects and Max remembers where Mary keeps Syntactic Structures.","phenomenon":"Baker ambiguity (scope of in-situ wh-phrase)","aliases":["ambiguous"],"near":["superiority effect","wh-island violation","mention-some reading"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"What grade does every student deserve? … Under the second interpretation, [the question] is not one but several requests for information at once. It can be paraphrased roughly as ‘For every student y, I ask you (to tell me) what grade y deserves.’ [This interpretation] requires multiple answers; for example, ‘Mary deserves an A, Bill deserves a B, . . .’ and so on for each of the students.","phenomenon":"pair-list reading","near":["mention-some reading","functional reading","echo question"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"(83) (a) Tell me who killed whom. (b) *Tell me whom who killed. (c) Tell me who went where. (d) *Tell me where who went. (e) Tell me what you bought where. (f) Tell me where you bought what.","phenomenon":"superiority effect","near":["wh-island violation","weak crossover","Complex NP Constraint violation"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1007/bf00351935"},{"id":"black1954-metaphor","title":"Metaphor","authors":["Max Black"],"year":"1954–55","area":"language","topic":"metaphor & figurative language","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The metaphor works by applying to the principal subject a system of “associated implications” characteristic of the subsidiary subject.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Metaphorical statement is not a substitute for a formal comparison or any other kind of literal statement, but has its own distinctive capacities and achievements.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Metaphors are not ornamental substitutes for literal paraphrases or elliptical similes: the subsidiary subject's system of associated commonplaces acts as a filter that organizes our view of the principal subject.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Genuine interaction metaphors have irreducible cognitive content: a literal paraphrase says too much, with the wrong emphasis, and fails to give the same insight; such metaphors can create rather than record similarities.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"interaction view","aliases":["interaction-metaphors","interaction"],"definition":"A metaphorical statement has two distinct subjects—a “principal” subject and a “subsidiary” one. … The metaphor selects, emphasizes, suppresses, and … organizes features of the principal subject by implying statements about it that normally apply to the subsidiary subject.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"substitution view","aliases":["substitution"],"definition":"Any view which holds that a metaphorical expression is used in place of some equivalent literal expression, I shall call a substitution view of metaphor.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"comparison view","aliases":["comparison"],"definition":"If a writer holds that a metaphor consists in the presentation of the underlying analogy or similarity, he will be taking what I shall call a comparison view of metaphor. … This is a view of metaphor as a condensed or elliptical simile.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"system of associated commonplaces","aliases":["system of commonplaces associated","associated commonplaces","commonplaces"],"definition":"Imagine some layman required to say, without taking special thought, those things he held to be true about wolves; the set of statements resulting would approximate to what I am here calling the system of commonplaces associated with the word “wolf”.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"focus","aliases":["focal"],"definition":"the focus of a metaphor, the word or expression having a distinctively metaphorical use within a literal frame","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"catachresis","aliases":[],"definition":"the use of a word in some new sense in order to remedy a gap in the vocabulary. Catachresis is the putting of new senses into old words.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"If the man is a wolf, he preys upon other animals, is fierce, hungry, engaged in constant struggle, a scavenger, and so on. … A suitable hearer will be led by the wolf-system of implications to construct a corresponding system of implications about the principal subject. … Any human traits that can without undue strain be talked about in “wolf-language” will be rendered prominent, and any that cannot will be pushed into the background.","phenomenon":"interaction view of metaphor","aliases":["interaction"],"near":["comparison view of metaphor","substitution view of metaphor","catachresis"],"avoid":["interaction metaphor","metaphor as filter"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Mathematicians spoke of the “leg” of an angle because there was no brief literal expression for a bounding line; we say “cherry lips”, because there is no form of words half as convenient for saying quickly what the lips are like.","phenomenon":"catachresis","near":["metonymy","hyperbole","personification"],"avoid":["dead metaphor","lexical gap filling"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"When Schopenhauer called a geometrical proof a mousetrap, he was, according to such a view, saying (though not explicitly): “A geometrical proof is like a mousetrap, since both offer a delusive reward, entice their victims by degrees, lead to disagreeable surprise, etc.”","phenomenon":"comparison view of metaphor","aliases":["comparison"],"near":["interaction view of metaphor","catachresis","metonymy"],"avoid":["substitution view of metaphor","elliptical simile","metaphor as elliptical simile","elliptical simile theory","simile"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Thus in the somewhat unfortunate example, “Richard is a lion”, which modern writers have discussed with boring insistence, the literal meaning is taken to be the same as that of the sentence, “Richard is brave”.","phenomenon":"substitution view of metaphor","aliases":["substitution"],"near":["comparison view of metaphor","interaction view of metaphor","catachresis"],"avoid":["metaphor as decoration"],"exact":true}],"doi":null},{"id":"davidson1978-metaphors-mean","title":"What Metaphors Mean","authors":["Donald Davidson"],"year":"1978","area":"language","topic":"metaphor & figurative language","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"metaphors mean what the words, in their most literal interpretation, mean, and nothing more.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"We must give up the idea that a metaphor carries a message, that it has a content or meaning (except, of course, its literal meaning).","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A metaphorical sentence has only its ordinary literal meaning and literal truth value, usually a patent falsehood; metaphor belongs to use, not meaning, and carries no second figurative meaning or special cognitive content.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Metaphor works not by encoding a message but by prompting us to see one thing as another; since what it makes us notice is endless and largely non-propositional, attempts to paraphrase its 'content' are misguided.","formal":false}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"metaphor","aliases":[],"definition":"I think metaphor belongs exclusively to the domain of use. It is something brought off by the imaginative employment of words and sentences and depends entirely on the ordinary meanings of those words and hence on the ordinary meanings of the sentences they comprise.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"elliptical simile theory","aliases":["elliptical simile","simile"],"definition":"the common theory that a metaphor is an elliptical simile. This theory makes no distinction in meaning between a metaphor and some related simile and does not provide any ground for speaking of figurative, metaphorical, or special meanings.","exact":false},{"id":"t3","term":"paraphrase","aliases":["paraphrasing"],"definition":"If this is right, what we attempt in \"paraphrasing\" a metaphor cannot be to give its meaning, for that lies on the surface; rather we attempt to evoke what the metaphor brings to our attention.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"metaphorical truth","aliases":[],"definition":"This is not to deny that there is such a thing as metaphorical truth, only to deny it of sentences. Metaphor does lead us to notice what might not otherwise be noticed, and there is no reason, I suppose, not to say these visions, thoughts, and feelings inspired by the metaphor, are true or false.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"When Shakespeare's Cressida is welcomed bawdily into the Grecian camp, Nestor says, \"Our general doth salute you with a kiss.\" Here we are to take \"general\" two ways: once as applying to Agamemnon, who is the general; and once, since she is kissing everyone, as applying to no one in particular, but everyone in general.","phenomenon":"pun (deliberate ambiguity)","near":["metaphor","dead metaphor","simile"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Once upon a time, I suppose, rivers and bottles did not, as they do now, literally have mouths. … when \"mouth\" applied only metaphorically to bottles, the application made the hearer notice a likeness between animal and bottle openings. … Once one has the present use of the word, with literal application to bottles, there is nothing left to notice.","phenomenon":"dead metaphor","aliases":["metaphorically"],"near":["pun","elliptical simile","metonymy"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"A famous critic said that Tolstoy was \"a great moralizing infant.\" The Tolstoy referred to here is obviously not the infant Tolstoy but Tolstoy the adult writer","phenomenon":"metaphor","near":["simile","pun","dead metaphor"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"if I show you Wittgenstein's duck-rabbit, and I say, \"It's a duck,\" then with luck you see it as a duck; if I say, \"It's a rabbit,\" you see it as a rabbit. But no proposition expresses what I have led you to see.","phenomenon":"seeing-as (aspect perception)","aliases":["see it as"],"near":["seeing-that (propositional perception)","perceptual illusion","lexical ambiguity"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1086/447971"},{"id":"geach1965-assertion","title":"Assertion","authors":["Peter Geach"],"year":"1965","area":"language","topic":"speech acts & assertion","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"A proposition may occur in discourse now asserted, now unasserted, and yet be recognizably the same proposition.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"In all the kinds of case I have mentioned, the very same sentence can occur in an \"if\" clause; and to such occurrences the anti-descriptive theories will not apply.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A proposition keeps the same content whether or not it is asserted; so analyses of 'bad', 'true' or 'I know' as non-descriptive acts (condemning, conceding, warranting) fail, since those sentences also occur unasserted in conditionals and modus ponens.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"No ordinary expression—'it is true that', 'exists', the copula, the predicate—carries assertoric force, since each occurs unasserted too; force shows only in a clause's standing alone, and rejecting is just asserting a negation.","formal":false}],"pick":1,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"Frege point","aliases":["Frege"],"definition":"A thought may have just the same content whether you assent to its truth or not; a proposition may occur in discourse now asserted, now unasserted, and yet be recognizably the same proposition.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"proposition","aliases":["propositions"],"definition":"a form of words in which something is propounded, put forward for consideration; it is surely clear that what is then put forward neither is ipso facto asserted nor gets altered in content by being asserted","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"double-barreled assertion","aliases":["double-barreled","assertoric"],"definition":"\"A has pointed out that p\" is exponible as the double-barreled assertion of \"A has maintained that p\" and of \"p\" itself. … [Such sentences] will certainly retain part of their assertoric force when put, for example, into an \"if\" clause","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"Ludwig's self-mate","aliases":["self-mate"],"definition":"The Hume-Brentano-Gilson thesis cannot be intelligibly stated if it is true; it claims that existence is unconceptualizable and can be grasped only in existential judgments, but this very claim is not an existential judgment and treats existence as conceptualizable. … This suicide of a thesis might be called Ludwig's self-mate","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"The theory that to call a kind of act \"bad\" is not to describe but to condemn it is open to similar objections. … If doing a thing is bad, getting your little brother to do it is bad. Tormenting the cat is bad. Ergo, getting your little brother to torment the cat is bad. The whole nerve of the reasoning is that \"bad\" should mean exactly the same at all four occurrences … But in the major premise the speaker (a father, let us suppose) is certainly not uttering acts of condemnation","phenomenon":"Frege–Geach problem","near":["open question argument","is–ought gap","Moore's paradox"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"If I ask, \"Is Jim aware of the fact that his wife is deceiving him?\" I am not just asking a question; I am asserting that Jim's wife is deceiving him. The question as I pose it cannot be properly answered \"Yes\" or \"No\" by someone who does not accept this assertion","phenomenon":"factive presupposition","near":["conversational implicature","referential opacity","Moore's paradox"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"[Ryle argues against the two-premise account of modus ponens] that if we needed to supply \"if p, then q\" as a premise for the inference of \"q\" from \"p,\" then by parity of reasoning we should need to supply \"if both p and if p then q, then q\" as a premise for the inference of \"q\" from \"p\" and \"if p then q\"","phenomenon":"Lewis Carroll's regress","near":["epistemic regress problem","rule-following paradox","paradoxes of material implication"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Buridan points out that if I just say \"a God\" or \"a horse\" I have not yet said anything true or false, but if I add the verb \"exists,\" then I have said something true or false; therefore, \"a God exists\" must signify something more than the bare term \"a God\" signifies. But, on the orthodox view, only after the world was created was there something more than God for the proposition \"a God exists\" to signify; therefore before the creation it did not signify anything more; therefore it was not true; therefore God did not then exist!","phenomenon":"fallacy of equivocation","near":["begging the question","self-refuting thesis","Liar paradox"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2183123"},{"id":"stanley2000-quantifier-domain-restriction","title":"On Quantifier Domain Restriction","authors":["Jason Stanley","Zoltán Gendler Szabó"],"year":"2000","area":"language","topic":"quantification & formal semantics","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the phenomenon of quantified contexts shows that quantifier expressions contain variables which can be bound, and whose purpose is to supply the domains for quantifier expressions.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"in the logical form of quantified sentences, there are variables whose values, relative to a context, are (often restricted) quantifier domains.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Domain variables attach to common nouns, not determiners: ⟦⟨man, f(i)⟩⟧c = ⟦man⟧ ∩ {x : x ∈ c(f)(c(i))}, where context assigns f a function from objects to sets and i an object.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Context's role in restricting quantifier domains is semantic: positing an unpronounced restricting predicate founders on underdetermination, and purely pragmatic accounts cannot capture readings in which a quantifier's domain is bound by a higher quantifier.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"quantified contexts","aliases":["quantified context"],"definition":"cases involving sentences containing multiple [determiner phrases] whose intuitive readings are only possible to capture by assuming that an index representing the [domain] of the second [one] is bound by the first [one].","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"descriptive problem","aliases":["descriptive problem of context dependence","problem"],"definition":"The descriptive problem of context dependence for an expression e relative to a context c is the problem of deriving the interpretation of e relative to c, given a prior characterization of what features of the context c have a bearing on the interpretation.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"foundational problem","aliases":["foundational problem of context dependence"],"definition":"the foundational problem of context dependence for an expression e relative to a context c is specifying what it is about the context in virtue of which certain entities (be they objects, properties, or propositions) play the role they do in the interpretation of an occurrence of e.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"pragmatic approach","aliases":["pragmatic","approach"],"definition":"an approach is pragmatic just in case the quantifier domain restriction determined by contextual features does not affect what is said, but rather only what is communicated.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"semantic role of context","aliases":["context"],"definition":"the role context plays in supplying additional values to what is said by an occurrence of a grammatical sentence, values that are not determined just from the linguistic meaning of the sentence uttered","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose Lisa went to the store to buy some bottles to give to Max, who wanted to fill them with his home-made beer. Max asks whether the bottles Lisa bought need to be emptied first. In response, Lisa utters (1): (1) Every bottle is empty. In this situation, we can plausibly assume that by uttering (1) Lisa conveyed to Max the proposition that every bottle she just bought is empty.","phenomenon":"quantifier domain restriction","near":["incomplete definite description","domain widening","comparison class of a gradable adjective"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"(24) In most of John’s classes, he fails exactly three Frenchmen. (25) In every room in John’s house, every bottle is in the corner. (26) Whatever John does, most of the class falls asleep.","phenomenon":"quantifier domain bound by a higher quantifier","aliases":["quantified contexts","quantified context"],"near":["bound-variable pronoun","donkey anaphora","inverse scope reading"],"avoid":["contextual quantifier domain restriction","quantifier domain restriction"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"(32) Every sailor waved to every sailor. This sentence can express the proposition that every sailor on the ship waved to every sailor on the shore","phenomenon":"different domains for quantifiers in one clause","near":["reciprocal reading","inverse scope reading","collective reading"],"avoid":["contextual quantifier domain restriction","quantifier domain restriction"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"Imagine a child who is crying because of a minor cut. Her mother attempts to calm her by uttering: (22) You are not going to die. Intuitively, the proposition communicated in this case is something like that expressed by an occurrence of ‘You are not going to die from that cut’.","phenomenon":"conversational impliciture (expansion)","near":["scalar implicature","presupposition accommodation","conventional implicature"],"avoid":["conversational implicature","free enrichment","pragmatic enrichment","unarticulated constituent","impliciture"],"exact":true},{"id":"e5","text":"(44) Most people regularly scream. They are crazy. Suppose the domain is the set of things in a certain village. There are two anaphoric readings of the pronoun in the second sentence in (44). On the first reading, it refers to all of the people in the village. On the second reading, it refers to those people in the village who regularly scream.","phenomenon":"cross-sentential anaphora","aliases":["anaphoric"],"near":["bound-variable anaphora","cataphora","deictic use of a pronoun"],"avoid":["E-type pronoun","E-type anaphora","discourse anaphora","cross-sentential (discourse) anaphora","donkey anaphora"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.1111/1468-0017.00130"},{"id":"hamblin1973-questions-montague-english","title":"Questions in Montague English","authors":["C. L. Hamblin"],"year":"1973","area":"language","topic":"questions & focus","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"Pragmatically speaking a question sets up a choice-situation between a set of propositions, namely, those propositions that count as answers to it.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"a unified set of rules can be constructed quite simply if indicatives are regarded as equivalent to one-alternative or Hobson's-choice interrogatives.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"A question denotes the set of propositions that would answer it, true and false alike, and a statement is the limiting case of a question with exactly one answer, so one compositional semantics covers both.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Expressions denote sets of ordinary denotations, combined pointwise: ⟦a b⟧ = {f(x) : f ∈ ⟦b⟧, x ∈ ⟦a⟧}. Indicatives get singletons (⟦Mary⟧ = {m}), so ⟦who walks⟧ = {that x walks : x a person}.","formal":true}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"denotation-set","aliases":["denotation-sets"],"definition":"What every expression, indicative or interrogative, is assigned in place of a single denotation: a set of ordinary denotations — a unit set for an indicative ('Mary' stands for {Mary}), a multi-membered set for an interrogative ('who walks' stands for the set of propositions that Mary walks, that John walks, …).","exact":false},{"id":"t2","term":"joint image","aliases":["images"],"definition":"Formally a‴b = Df{c : (∃d∈a, e∈b)(c = d(e))}. We might call a‴b the joint image of b by a: it is the union of the set of images d″b of b by members d of a.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"Hobson's-choice interrogative","aliases":["Hobson's-choice interrogatives","Hobson's choice","one-alternative interrogative","one-alternative interrogatives"],"definition":"A question whose denotation-set has exactly one member, so that it offers only one possible answer; indicatives are treated as such, which lets one unified set of rules cover statements and questions.","exact":false},{"id":"t4","term":"answer","aliases":["answers","answer-relation"],"definition":"Semantically, a statement whose denotation, on a suitable reading, is one of the propositions in the question's denotation-set — possibly subject to further syntactic requirements, e.g. that tautological replies such as 'the dog that barks barks' to 'what dog barks' be excluded.","exact":false}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Prefixing 'is it the case that' to a formula such as 'John sleeps' yields an expression whose denotation-set has just two members: the proposition that John sleeps and the proposition that John sleeps not. Hence 'is it the case that' is given the denotation-set {I, Dadf('not')}, where I is the identity function.","phenomenon":"polar (yes/no) question","aliases":["yes-no","yes/no"],"near":["constituent (wh-) question","multiple wh-question","echo question"],"exact":false},{"id":"e2","text":"Questions like 'who does what' and 'does what to whom': on the recast rule for two-place verbs, the proposition denoted by 'Mary walks Rover' is a member of the denotation-set of 'who does what', which holds one proposition for each choice of a doer and a deed.","phenomenon":"multiple wh-question","near":["polar (yes/no) question","alternative question","echo question"],"exact":false},{"id":"e3","text":"Questions such as 'John does what' or 'Rover is a what' keep the order of the corresponding statement. In many languages, for example all Melanesian ones, word-order is always that of the corresponding indicative and there is not even a distinct inflexion.","phenomenon":"wh-in-situ","aliases":["in situ"],"near":["wh-movement (wh-fronting)","subject-auxiliary inversion","polar (yes/no) question"],"exact":false}],"doi":null},{"id":"katz1963-structure-semantic-theory","title":"The Structure of a Semantic Theory","authors":["Jerrold J. Katz","Jerry A. Fodor"],"year":"1963","area":"language","topic":"theory of meaning & truth conditions","formal":true,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the semantic relations among the various senses of a lexical item and among the various senses of different lexical items are represented by formal relations between markers and distinguishers","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"the doctrine that the meaning of a sentence is a function of the meanings of its parts. The system of projection rules is just this function.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"With k the number of readings dictionary plus projection rules assign sentence S: k = 0 → S is anomalous; k = 1 → unambiguous; k > 1 → k-ways ambiguous; S and P share a reading → paraphrases.","formal":true},{"kind":"summary","text":"Explaining speakers' grasp of novel sentences requires a dictionary decomposing each word sense into semantic markers and distinguishers, plus projection rules that amalgamate senses bottom-up through the syntactic tree wherever selection restrictions are satisfied.","formal":false}],"pick":3,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"projection problem","aliases":["project"],"definition":"the speaker's knowledge of his language takes the form of rules which project the finite set of sentences he has fortuitously encountered to the infinite set of sentences of the language. … The problem of formulating these rules we shall refer to as the projection problem.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"projection rules","aliases":["projection rule"],"definition":"rules for applying the information in the dictionary—rules which take account of semantic relations between morphemes and of the interaction between meaning and syntactic structure in determining the correct semantic interpretation for any of the infinitely many sentences which the grammar generates.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"semantic marker","aliases":["semantic markers","Semantic markers"],"definition":"Semantic markers are the elements in terms of which semantic relations are expressed in a theory. … The semantic markers assigned to a lexical item in a dictionary entry are intended to reflect whatever systematic semantic relations hold between that item and the rest of the vocabulary of the language.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"distinguisher","aliases":["distinguishers"],"definition":"the distinguishers assigned to a lexical item are intended to reflect what is idiosyncratic about its meaning. … a change in a distinguisher merely alters the relation between one item and its synonyms.","exact":true},{"id":"t5","term":"amalgamation","aliases":["Amalgamation"],"definition":"Amalgamation is the joining of elements from different sets of paths under a given grammatical marker if these elements satisfy the appropriate selection restrictions represented by the material in angles.","exact":true},{"id":"t6","term":"semantic anomaly","aliases":["Semantic anomaly"],"definition":"Semantic anomaly can then be construed as the limiting case of selection: the case where there is a lexical item in a sentence whose paths are ALL excluded by selections due to other material in the sentence.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"Suppose S is the sentence The bill is large. Speakers of English will agree that this sentence is ambiguous, i.e. that it has at least two readings. According to one it means that some document demanding a sum of money to discharge a debt exceeds in size most such documents; according to the other it means that the beak of a certain bird exceeds in bulk those of most similar birds.","phenomenon":"lexical ambiguity","aliases":["ambiguous"],"near":["structural (syntactic) ambiguity","vagueness","semantic anomaly"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"Now let S be the sentence The paint is silent. English speakers will at once recognize that this sentence is anomalous in some way. For example, they will distinguish it from such sentences as The paint is wet and The paint is yellow by applying to it such epithets as 'odd', 'peculiar', 'paradoxical', and 'bizarre'.","phenomenon":"semantic anomaly","aliases":["anomalous"],"near":["ungrammaticality","contradiction (analytic falsehood)","presupposition failure"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"Consider (1) Our store sells alligator shoes and (2) Our store sells horse shoes. In normal settings (e.g. as signs in a store window or as newspaper advertisements), occurrences of (1) will be taken on the reading 'our store sells shoes made from alligator skins' while (2) will be taken on the reading 'our store sells shoes for horses'. Notice, however, that (1) is open to the reading 'our store sells shoes for alligators' and (2) is open to the reading 'our store sells shoes made from the skin of horses'.","phenomenon":"disambiguation by world knowledge","near":["disambiguation by selection restrictions","disambiguation by grammatical category","conversational implicature"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"The limiting case, where the addition to the compound of semantic material from the modifier is zero, is of considerable theoretical significance. The compound unmarried bachelor is a case in point. … in forming the compound unmarried bachelor all the semantic information in the path of the modifier unmarried will be deleted so that the derived path for unmarried bachelor will contain no more than the semantic material which comes from the path for bachelor.","phenomenon":"semantic redundancy (pleonasm)","near":["contradiction (analytic falsehood)","semantic anomaly","lexical ambiguity"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/411200"},{"id":"brandom1983-asserting","title":"Asserting","authors":["Robert Brandom"],"year":"1983","area":"language","topic":"speech acts & assertion","formal":false,"theses":[{"kind":"excerpt","text":"In asserting a claim one not only authorizes further assertions, but commits oneself to vindicate the original claim, showing that one is entitled to make it.","formal":false},{"kind":"excerpt","text":"The meanings do not determine the appropriate inferences, but what inferences are socially appropriate determines the meanings of the sentences involved in those inferences.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Making a claim institutes a social status with two dimensions: it licenses others to rely on and re-assert it, deferring challenges back to the speaker, and commits the speaker to justify it if challenged.","formal":false},{"kind":"summary","text":"Taking another's claim as justified is recognizing its authority to license further claims; taking it as true is being prepared to endorse it oneself. So justification and truth differ perspectivally, as attributed versus undertaken commitment.","formal":false}],"pick":0,"terms":[{"id":"t1","term":"inference license","aliases":["inference licenses","assertion license"],"definition":"asserting is issuing an inference license. Since inferring is drawing a conclusion, such an inference license amounts to a warrant for further assertions, specifically assertions of those sentences which can appropriately be inferred from the sentence originally asserted.","exact":true},{"id":"t2","term":"bare assertion","aliases":["bare assertions"],"definition":"bare assertions, which involve no offer to defend a claim, operating solely as licenses authorizing inferences to further claims.","exact":true},{"id":"t3","term":"basic repertoire","aliases":["repertoire"],"definition":"Each speaker has, to begin with, a basic repertoire consisting of those sentences he or she is currently prepared to authorize and explicitly to undertake justificatory responsibility for, that is, to assert.","exact":true},{"id":"t4","term":"extended repertoire","aliases":["repertoire"],"definition":"From the point of view of other speakers, however, there is also an extended repertoire consisting of all those sentences others are prepared to take that individual as committed to. In general the speaker need not be aware of all that he or she has become socially committed to in virtue of the commitments explicitly undertaken.","exact":true}],"examples":[{"id":"e1","text":"The sentence 'Saturn is larger than Mars' has the logical form of a predication. In the conditional 'If Saturn is larger than Mars, then it is larger than Mercury' … though the same predication occurs, it is not asserted that Saturn is larger than Mars.","phenomenon":"the Frege point (force–content distinction)","near":["Frege's puzzle about informative identities","presupposition projection","use–mention distinction"],"exact":true},{"id":"e2","text":"a parrot trained reliably to say \"It's getting warmer\" only as the temperature … climbs past 80 degrees never succeeds in asserting that it is getting warmer. However well the bird's responsive dispositions to produce this report match our own, it is incompetent as an assertor. The parrot cannot tell justifications of his remark from arguments against it, and cannot discriminate the further assertions which would thereby be licensed from those incompatible with what it has said.","phenomenon":"reliable differential responsiveness without conceptual understanding","aliases":["reliably","responsive dispositions"],"near":["bare assertion","insincere assertion","deferential use of a term"],"exact":true},{"id":"e3","text":"I may be willing to accept as justified your claim that there is a candle in front of you, given your information. Yet if I accept, as you do not, that there is a mirror in front of you, I will not be prepared to endorse your claim as true, that is to make inferences from it myself.","phenomenon":"the justification–truth distinction","aliases":["justified","true"],"near":["Gettier case","bare assertion","deferral of justificatory responsibility"],"exact":true},{"id":"e4","text":"in Football a referee confers the social status \"Offsides\" on a performance by responding to it in a special way. That special response gets its significance in turn from its being appropriately responded to on the part of other officials by their imposition of a 5-yard penalty.","phenomenon":"institutional fact","near":["brute fact","natural kind","regulative rule"],"exact":true}],"doi":"10.2307/2215086"}]};
